window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("backend-fundamentals", {"topics":[{"key":"backend-fundamentals/python","sources":["Official docs: Python (asyncio, concurrent.futures)","PEP 703 — Making the Global Interpreter Lock Optional in CPython","Official docs: Pydantic"],"why":"An AI service is glue: it takes a request, builds a prompt, calls a model (or several), validates the\nstructured answer, maybe hits a vector store and a tokenizer along the way, and streams a result back.\nEach of those pieces exists first, and usually only, as a Python library: PyTorch, Hugging Face,\nsentence-transformers, tiktoken, every provider's official SDK, the eval harnesses, the notebooks the\nresearch team used to find the prompt that works. Choosing another language for the backend means\nreimplementing those pieces, shelling out to Python anyway, or maintaining two stacks.\n\nThe obvious objection is \"Python is slow, serve it in Go or Java\". It fails on arithmetic. A model call\ntakes hundreds of milliseconds to tens of seconds; the request-handling overhead that a faster\nlanguage would shave is measured in microseconds to low milliseconds. The hot loops that matter, matrix multiplies and\ntokenization, already run in C, C++, Rust or CUDA underneath the Python API. What Python must do well\nis wait on many model calls at once, and `asyncio` does exactly that.\n\nThe opposite objection, \"Python cannot do concurrency because of the GIL\", fails for the same reason:\nthe GIL is released while a thread is blocked on a socket, and an event loop does not need threads at\nall to hold thousands of in-flight requests. The GIL is a real limit only for CPU-bound pure-Python\nloops, and the answer there is a native library or a separate process, not a different language.","how":"### Execution model and the GIL\nCPython compiles source to bytecode and interprets it. The Global Interpreter Lock lets only one\nthread execute Python bytecode at a time *because* the reference-counting memory model is not\nthread-safe without it. Threads still help for I/O: the interpreter drops the GIL during blocking\nsystem calls, and well-behaved C extensions (NumPy, PyTorch, hashing, compression) release it during\nlong native work. For CPU-bound pure-Python code, use `multiprocessing` or `ProcessPoolExecutor`, which\nsidestep the GIL by running separate interpreters. Python 3.13 added an experimental free-threaded\nbuild (PEP 703) that removes the GIL, and 3.14 made that build officially supported, but it is still a\nseparate opt-in build rather than the default and not every C extension supports it yet, so plan as if\nthe GIL is there.\n\n### asyncio: waiting without threads\nAn `async def` function is a coroutine; `await` hands control back to the event loop until the awaited\nI/O completes. One thread can therefore hold hundreds of pending model calls, each costing a small\nobject rather than a thread stack. This is the shape of an LLM backend: fan out calls, cap concurrency\nwith a `Semaphore` to respect provider rate limits, gather results. The one rule that matters is that\na blocking call inside a coroutine (a sync HTTP client, `time.sleep`, a heavy loop) freezes the whole\nloop, because nothing else can run until it returns.\n\nThe arithmetic is what makes this the right shape. If 500 requests each await a 4-second model call,\na thread-per-request design needs 500 threads, each with its own stack, and pays OS context switches\nto do nothing; the event loop holds 500 suspended coroutine objects and wakes each one when its socket\nhas bytes. Work done *per request* in Python (building the payload, parsing the reply) is typically\nwell under a millisecond, so a single loop is idle most of the time even at that concurrency.\n\n### Picking the tool for each kind of work\nThe decision follows from two questions: does the work wait or compute, and does it hold the GIL?\n\n| Work | Holds the GIL? | Where it runs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Async I/O (httpx, async DB driver, provider async client) | No, it waits | `await` it on the loop |\n| Blocking I/O from a sync-only library | Released while blocked | `await asyncio.to_thread(fn, ...)` |\n| Native compute that releases the GIL (NumPy, PyTorch, hashing) | Released | a thread (`to_thread`) so the loop stays free |\n| Pure-Python CPU work (parsing, loops over dicts) | Held | `ProcessPoolExecutor`, or move it to a worker service |\n\nThreads are cheaper than processes (shared memory, no pickling of arguments and results) but only help\nwhen the GIL is released; processes always parallelise but every call pays serialisation, so they pay\noff only when the work is much larger than its inputs and outputs.\n\n### Types and Pydantic\nType hints are annotations; the interpreter does not enforce them. That is fine inside your code with\n`mypy` checking it, but data crossing a boundary (an HTTP body, a model's JSON output, a config file)\nneeds runtime validation. Pydantic models validate and coerce at runtime and emit JSON Schema, so one\nclass can be the API contract, the structured-output schema you hand to an LLM, and the parsed result.\nThis single-source-of-truth role is why Pydantic became the lingua franca of AI services.\n\n### Packaging and environments\nEach project gets a virtual environment and a lockfile; `uv`, Poetry or plain `pip` with a\nrequirements lock all work. The characteristic pain is heavy native dependencies (PyTorch with a\nspecific CUDA build), which is why AI services pin exact versions and ship in containers.\n\n### Where it sits in the stack\nTypically: a FastAPI service for the API, worker processes for long jobs, scripts and notebooks for\nevals and data prep. The product UI and sometimes a thin gateway live in TypeScript. The boundary\nbetween them is an HTTP contract, usually generated from the Python service's OpenAPI schema.","example":"Fan out a batch of classification calls concurrently with a concurrency cap, validate each model\nanswer at runtime, and push a CPU-bound step out of the GIL-bound main process. The `Semaphore` is the\nrate-limit guard; the `ProcessPoolExecutor` is the GIL escape hatch.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\nimport httpx\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError\n\nclass Verdict(BaseModel):\n    label: str\n    confidence: float\n\nSEM = asyncio.Semaphore(8)                      # at most 8 model calls in flight\n\nasync def classify(client: httpx.AsyncClient, text: str) -> Verdict | None:\n    async with SEM:                             # waits without holding a thread\n        r = await client.post(\"http://localhost:8000/v1/classify\", json={\"text\": text}, timeout=30)\n    try:\n        return Verdict.model_validate_json(r.content)  # runtime check; bad JSON is a ValidationError too\n    except ValidationError:\n        return None\n\ndef count_words(text: str) -> int:              # stand-in for CPU-heavy pure-Python work\n    return sum(1 for _ in text.split())\n\nasync def main(texts: list[str]):\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient() as client:   # one connection pool for the whole batch\n        verdicts = await asyncio.gather(*(classify(client, t) for t in texts))\n    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor() as pool:         # CPU-bound work runs in separate interpreters\n        counts = await asyncio.gather(*(loop.run_in_executor(pool, count_words, t) for t in texts))\n    return list(zip(verdicts, counts))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":                      # required when workers are spawned, not forked\n    print(asyncio.run(main([\"refund my order\", \"great product\"] * 50)))\n```","tradeoffs":["You get the entire ML ecosystem and the fastest path from notebook to service, but you pay with a runtime that is slow for CPU-bound pure-Python code; this flips only when your service does heavy per-request computation in Python itself rather than in native libraries or a model.","`asyncio` gives cheap concurrency for I/O-bound work, but every library on the request path must be async-aware; one blocking call undoes it, and mixing sync and async code is a constant tax.","Dynamic typing speeds experimentation, but production code needs `mypy` plus runtime validation at boundaries; skipping either shows up as type errors discovered by users instead of CI.","Rich native dependencies (PyTorch, CUDA) are the ecosystem's strength and its deployment weakness: images are large and version pinning is non-negotiable."],"pitfalls":["Calling a synchronous client (`requests`, a sync DB driver) inside an `async def` handler; the symptom is throughput collapsing to one request at a time while CPU sits idle.","Treating the GIL as the reason a service is slow when it is I/O-bound; profiling shows threads blocked on sockets, and the fix is concurrency, not multiprocessing.","Trusting type hints as validation; a model returns `\"confidence\": \"high\"` and the code crashes three functions later instead of at the boundary.","Firing `asyncio.create_task(...)` and dropping the returned task; the loop keeps only a weak reference, so the task can be garbage-collected mid-flight and the symptom is background work (a log write, a cache fill) that silently never completes. Keep a reference or use a `TaskGroup`.","Unpinned dependencies in an AI service; a transitive upgrade of a tokenizer or SDK changes outputs and the symptom is evals drifting with no code change."],"when":"Default to Python for anything that touches models, tokenizers, embeddings, evals or data preparation,\nand for the service layer that orchestrates them: the library support decides it. Keep CPU-bound hot\npaths in native code or separate processes, and measure before assuming Python is the bottleneck;\nin most LLM services the model call dominates by two or three orders of magnitude. The measurements\nthat settle it are event-loop lag (asyncio's debug mode logs any callback that runs longer than\n`slow_callback_duration`, 100 ms by default), CPU utilisation per worker under load, and a profiler\nsample (py-spy) showing whether time is spent in Python frames or waiting on sockets. Consider another\nlanguage for the product edge (TypeScript next to the UI) or for a tiny, extremely high-throughput\nproxy where microseconds matter. In interviews, the expected depth is explaining the GIL precisely\n(what it blocks, when it is released), why `asyncio` fits LLM workloads, and why Pydantic sits at\nevery boundary. The `python-for-ai` module covers each of these in detail.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes async service code, keeps blocking work off the event loop, validates every boundary with Pydantic, and pins the environment so evals stay reproducible.","architect":"Decides the language split: Python for the model core and data work, TypeScript or another language where the product or throughput argument wins, joined by a typed contract.","fde":"Lands inside a customer's existing Python (or non-Python) stack and must explain why the AI core stays Python and how it integrates with their Java or .NET services over HTTP."},"check":{"q":"A colleague argues the new LLM gateway should be written in Go because \"Python's GIL cannot handle\n2,000 concurrent requests\". How do you respond, and what would change your answer?","a":"The GIL serialises Python bytecode execution, not waiting. A gateway's work per request is\nbuilding a payload and awaiting a model call that takes seconds; with `asyncio`, one process holds\nthousands of pending awaits on a single thread, and the GIL is irrelevant while they wait on sockets.\nMultiple uvicorn workers add CPU headroom for serialisation. I would ask what CPU-bound work the\ngateway does per request; if it must tokenize or transform megabytes in pure Python per call, that is\nthe argument for native code or another language. I would also weigh the cost of a second language\nwhen the model, eval and SDK ecosystem is Python-first."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the GIL rarely limit an LLM-serving backend?","options":["Modern Python releases the GIL for all threads automatically","The service is I/O-bound, and the GIL is released while waiting on sockets, so asyncio or threads overlap the waits","Uvicorn runs each request in its own interpreter","LLM responses are small, so bytecode execution is negligible regardless of concurrency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The GIL blocks concurrent execution of Python bytecode, not concurrent waiting. An LLM backend mostly awaits network I/O, during which the GIL is not held, so one process serves many requests."},{"q":"Which piece of work should leave the main Python process rather than run inside a coroutine?","options":["Awaiting 200 concurrent HTTP calls to a model provider","A NumPy matrix multiply over a batch of embeddings","A pure-Python loop that parses a 500 MB log file line by line","Reading configuration from environment variables at startup"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pure-Python CPU work holds the GIL, so neither the event loop nor a thread can overlap it; only a separate process can. A long NumPy multiply also blocks the loop if run inline, but it releases the GIL, so a thread in the same process (`asyncio.to_thread`) suffices. Awaited HTTP calls are I/O-bound."},{"q":"What does a Pydantic model add that a type hint alone does not?","options":["It makes the interpreter reject wrongly typed arguments at call time","Runtime validation and coercion of external data plus JSON Schema generation","Faster attribute access through compiled slots","Automatic retries when validation fails"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Type hints are inert annotations checked only by tools like mypy. Pydantic validates data at runtime, which is what an HTTP body or a model's JSON output needs, and emits the schema you give the LLM."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/fastapi","sources":["Official docs: FastAPI","Official docs: Starlette","Official docs: Pydantic"],"why":"An AI endpoint is unusual HTTP. A request may hold its connection open for ten seconds, stream\nhundreds of chunks, carry a large structured body in and a large structured body out, and share\nexpensive clients (a model-provider connection pool, a vector store) across every request. The\nclassic Python web stack was built for the opposite: WSGI frameworks like Flask and traditional\nDjango dedicate one thread or process to a request from start to finish. With 200 in-flight model\ncalls of five seconds each, a WSGI server needs 200 workers to avoid queueing, and those workers sit\nidle on sockets. An ASGI server runs one event loop per process that holds all 200 pending awaits at\nnegligible cost, so concurrency is bounded by memory and the upstream, not by worker count.\n\nThe second forcing problem is contract drift. Hand-written validation and hand-written API docs\ndiverge the week after launch; a client sends `temperature: \"0.7\"` and the handler crashes deep in a\nprovider call. FastAPI reads the Pydantic types on the handler signature and derives validation, a\nprecise 422 error, response filtering and the OpenAPI schema from one declaration, so the docs cannot\nlie about the code. In AI services that same model class is also the JSON Schema you hand a model for\nstructured output, which closes the loop between API contract and prompt contract.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"use raw Starlette or aiohttp and validate by hand\", works but reinvents those\nlayers, and Flask-with-async-bolted-on keeps the WSGI mental model where it hurts most.","how":"### ASGI and the request path\nUvicorn accepts the connection and hands an ASGI `scope` to the app. The request passes through the\nmiddleware stack (CORS, auth, tracing), the router matches the path and method, FastAPI resolves the\nhandler's dependencies, validates path, query and body parameters against the declared types, calls\nthe handler, then serialises the return value through `response_model`. Validation failures stop at\nthe boundary with a 422 and a field-level error list, before the handler body runs (dependencies\nmay already have executed, so keep side effects out of them).\n\n### `async def` versus `def` handlers\nAn `async def` handler runs directly on the event loop, so every `await` yields to other requests. A\nplain `def` handler is run in a bounded worker threadpool so that it cannot block the loop. The choice\nis deliberate: use `async def` when everything you call is async (httpx, async DB drivers, provider\nSDKs' async clients); use `def` when you must call a blocking library. The failure mode is the\nmix: a sync SDK call inside `async def` blocks the loop and every other request on that worker waits.\nThe same rule applies to dependencies: a plain `def` dependency is also run in the threadpool.\n\nThe capacity arithmetic differs between the two. A `def` handler's concurrency per worker is capped by\nthe threadpool (AnyIO's default limiter is 40 tokens), so if each call holds a thread for 5 seconds,\none worker serves at most 40 / 5 = 8 requests per second before requests queue for a thread. An\n`async def` handler that awaits the same 5-second call is capped by memory and the upstream's limits\ninstead, which is why the async path is the default for model-calling endpoints.\n\n### Workers and processes\nOne uvicorn process is one event loop on one core. Production runs several worker processes per\nmachine (uvicorn `--workers`, gunicorn with uvicorn workers, or one process per container behind the\norchestrator) *because* JSON parsing, validation and serialisation still use CPU, and a single loop\ncannot use a second core. Workers share nothing, so in-memory caches, rate-limit counters and\n\"which streams are open\" state belong in Redis or the database, not in a module-level dict.\n\n### Pydantic as the contract\nDeclaring `body: ChatRequest` validates and parses the JSON body; `response_model=Answer` filters the\noutput to the declared fields (so a leaked internal attribute never reaches a client) and documents\nit. `Field` constraints (`max_length`, `ge`, `le`) become both runtime checks and schema metadata.\n\n### Dependency injection and lifespan\n`Depends` lets a handler ask for a current user, a DB session or a shared HTTP client without\nconstructing it; dependencies compose and can be overridden in tests. `lifespan` runs startup and\nshutdown code, which is where you build application-scoped resources (one `httpx.AsyncClient`, one\nmodel client) instead of recreating them per request.\n\n### Streaming\n`StreamingResponse` takes an async generator and writes each yielded chunk as it arrives. With\n`text/event-stream` framing you get server-sent events, the standard way to stream tokens to browsers\nand SDKs. Because each `yield` happens between awaits, a slow stream does not block other requests.\n\n### Background tasks are not a job queue\n`BackgroundTasks` runs a function after the response is sent, in the same process. That suits a\nfew hundred milliseconds of follow-up (write an audit row, emit a metric). It is the wrong home for a\ntwo-minute summarisation *because* the work dies with the process on a deploy or crash, nothing\nretries it, and it competes with live requests for the same loop or threadpool. Long or must-complete\nwork goes to a durable queue and a separate worker, with a `202` and a job resource on the API side.\n\n### Compared with the alternatives\nFlask is simpler and sync-first, fine for small internal tools but a poor fit for long-lived streaming\ncalls. Django brings an ORM, admin and auth; choose it when those matter more than async purity, and\nnote its async support is partial. Starlette is FastAPI without validation, DI and docs. Litestar is\na comparable ASGI framework with a different DI style; the reasoning in this lesson applies to it too.","example":"A streaming chat endpoint: one shared upstream client created in `lifespan`, a validated request\nbody, and an SSE stream produced by an async generator. Every `await` inside `events()` releases the\nloop to other requests, which is the whole point of the async design.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nimport httpx\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, Request\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app: FastAPI):\n    app.state.http = httpx.AsyncClient(base_url=\"http://model-server:8000\", timeout=60)\n    yield                                          # the app serves requests here\n    await app.state.http.aclose()                  # shutdown: close the pool cleanly\n\napp = FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan)\n\nclass ChatRequest(BaseModel):\n    prompt: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=8000)\n    temperature: float = Field(0.2, ge=0, le=2)   # validated and documented from one declaration\n\ndef http(request: Request) -> httpx.AsyncClient:   # dependency: one shared pool, not one per request\n    return request.app.state.http\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/chat\")\nasync def chat(body: ChatRequest, client: httpx.AsyncClient = Depends(http)):\n    async def events():\n        async with client.stream(\"POST\", \"/generate\", json=body.model_dump()) as r:\n            async for line in r.aiter_lines():     # each await yields the loop to other requests\n                if line:\n                    yield f\"data: {json.dumps({'token': line})}\\n\\n\"\n        yield \"data: [DONE]\\n\\n\"\n    return StreamingResponse(events(), media_type=\"text/event-stream\")\n```","tradeoffs":["You gain high concurrency for I/O-bound handlers, but every library on the hot path must be async or explicitly offloaded; one blocking call turns the advantage into a liability.","Pydantic-driven validation removes boilerplate and keeps docs honest, but it adds per-request parsing cost; for very large bodies or extreme throughput that cost is measurable and worth profiling.","FastAPI is deliberately thin (no ORM, no admin, no auth framework), which keeps it flexible but means you assemble those pieces yourself; Django wins when a full-stack CRUD app is the actual job.","Single-process asyncio scales I/O, not CPU; you still run several uvicorn workers per machine and design them to be stateless."],"pitfalls":["Using a synchronous provider SDK or `requests` inside `async def`; the symptom is latency on every endpoint of that worker growing with the sum of the blocking upstream calls queued ahead of it.","Creating an `httpx.AsyncClient` per request; the symptom is a new TLS handshake per call and socket exhaustion under load, fixed by one client in `lifespan`.","Returning ORM objects or internal dicts without `response_model`; the symptom is an internal field (a cost figure, a secret flag) appearing in client responses.","Doing CPU-heavy work (tokenizing megabytes, re-ranking in pure Python) inside the handler; the symptom is event-loop stalls visible as every endpoint slowing together.","Running long jobs through `BackgroundTasks`; the symptom is work that silently vanishes on every deploy or pod restart, with no error logged because the request already returned 200.","Keeping state in module-level variables; the symptom is behaviour that changes with which worker served the request (a rate limit that allows N times the configured rate with N workers)."],"when":"Reach for FastAPI when building the HTTP layer of a Python AI service: model gateways, RAG endpoints,\nagent runners, anything that awaits a model and benefits from streaming and typed contracts. Prefer\nDjango when the application is mostly conventional CRUD with an ORM and admin, and Flask only for\ntiny sync tools. Measure event-loop lag (schedule a no-op every 100 ms and record how late it fires),\nthe health check's latency under load (it should stay flat; if it tracks the model endpoint's\nlatency, something is blocking the loop), threadpool saturation for `def` handlers, and upstream\nconnection reuse. In day-to-day work the skill is keeping the request path fully async,\ndesigning Pydantic models that double as structured-output schemas, and wiring shared clients\nthrough dependencies; interviews probe whether you know why `def` and `async def` handlers behave\ndifferently. The `fastapi-for-ai` module covers each mechanism in depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds endpoints with Pydantic contracts, shared async clients via `Depends`, and SSE streaming, and keeps anything blocking out of the event loop.","architect":"Decides that the AI service layer is ASGI and stateless, sets worker and concurrency limits per process, and treats the OpenAPI schema as the published contract for other teams.","fde":"Stands up a FastAPI service inside a customer's network, exposes `/docs` so their integrators can self-serve, and explains why blocking code in their middleware (a sync auth lookup) stalls every async handler behind it."},"check":{"q":"A FastAPI service has one `async def` endpoint that calls a provider through a synchronous SDK.\nUnder light load it is fine; under load every endpoint, including the health check, becomes slow.\nExplain the mechanism and two fixes with their trade-offs.","a":"The sync SDK call blocks the event loop thread for the full duration of the model request, so\nno other coroutine on that worker, including the health check, can run until it returns; under load\nrequests queue behind each other. Fix one: switch to the SDK's async client (or httpx) so the call\nyields to the loop; this is the right long-term fix but requires the whole path to be async. Fix two:\nchange the handler to plain `def` so FastAPI runs it in the threadpool; this unblocks the loop\nimmediately but caps concurrency at the threadpool size (AnyIO's default limiter is 40 threads,\ntunable) and adds thread overhead; at 5 seconds per call, 40 threads is only 8 requests per second per\nworker. A middle path is to keep the handler async and wrap just the sync call in\n`await asyncio.to_thread(...)`, with the same thread cap. Either way, add a metric for event-loop lag\nso a regression is visible, and treat a slow health check as the first sign of a blocked loop."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does FastAPI run a plain `def` handler in a threadpool but an `async def` handler on the loop?","options":["Threadpools are faster for JSON serialisation","A sync function would block the single event loop, so it is isolated in a thread while coroutines yield at each await","Pydantic validation only works in threads","The threadpool enables HTTP/2 multiplexing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The event loop is one thread; a blocking function on it stops every other request. Running sync handlers in threads protects the loop, while async handlers cooperate by yielding at awaits."},{"q":"What do you get from declaring the request body as a Pydantic model?","options":["Automatic caching of identical request bodies","Guaranteed backward compatibility when fields are renamed","Runtime validation with a 422 error, parsed typed access, and the matching OpenAPI schema from one declaration","Automatic retries of the upstream model call on validation failure"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The handler's type annotations drive validation, parsing and documentation together, so the docs and the behaviour cannot diverge. Caching, compatibility and retries are separate concerns."},{"q":"When is Django a better choice than FastAPI for a service that also calls an LLM?","options":["When the service must stream tokens to a browser","When the application is mostly CRUD with an ORM, admin and auth, and the LLM call is a small part of it","When the service needs OpenAPI documentation","When the provider SDK is async-only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"FastAPI's strengths are async I/O and typed contracts; Django's are batteries for conventional web apps. If the bulk of the work is CRUD and admin, Django's ecosystem outweighs async purity."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/node-js-typescript","sources":["Official docs: Node.js (event loop, streams, worker_threads)","Official docs: TypeScript handbook","Official docs: Zod"],"why":"AI products are mostly chat and streaming interfaces, and the web is built in TypeScript: React,\nNext.js and the server-side rendering frameworks that render them, the serverless and edge platforms\nthat host them, and the Vercel AI SDK that streams model output into components. A team shipping a\nproduct cannot avoid TypeScript, and putting the request path that talks to the model next to the UI\nin the same language removes a translation layer, a deployment and a second on-call rota.\n\nThe naive \"everything in Python\" plan fails at the edge: the frontend team does not write Python,\nSSR frameworks are TypeScript, and edge runtimes are built around V8 and JavaScript (where Python is\noffered at all, it runs through WebAssembly with a restricted package set). The naive \"everything in\nTypeScript\" plan fails at the core: training, fine-tuning, evaluation harnesses, tokenizers, vector\nand data tooling are Python-first, and rewriting them is not a project any product team wants. The\nhonest outcome is a two-language system, and the real engineering question becomes where the boundary\nsits and how its contract is kept honest.\n\nNode fits the edge role for a mechanical reason. Its single-threaded event loop with non-blocking I/O\nhandles thousands of concurrent streaming connections cheaply, which is what a chat gateway does all\nday: open an upstream stream, relay chunks, apply backpressure, close. What it does badly is the same\nthing Python does badly, CPU-bound work on the main thread, and unlike Python it has no deep native ML\necosystem (NumPy, PyTorch) to hand that work to.","how":"### The event loop\nJavaScript runs on one thread; libuv provides a thread pool for file, DNS and crypto work and a loop\nthat dispatches completed I/O callbacks. A request handler that awaits a network call costs a promise,\nnot a thread, so concurrency is cheap. The corollary is that any synchronous CPU work on the main\nthread (parsing a 50 MB JSON body, tokenizing a book in pure JavaScript) stops every other request.\nThe escape hatches are `worker_threads` for CPU-bound tasks and keeping heavy compute in the Python\ncore, *because* nothing on the main thread can preempt a running function.\n\n### Streams and backpressure\nNode 18 and later ship `fetch` and the Web Streams API globally, so a handler can read an upstream\nmodel stream chunk by chunk and write it to the client as it arrives. On the Node side, `res.write()`\nreturns `false` once the response's internal buffer passes its high-water mark, meaning the client is\nreading slower than you are writing; honouring that signal (stop pulling from upstream, wait for\n`drain`) is what keeps memory flat when a slow browser consumes a fast model, and ignoring it is the\nclassic way a streaming proxy runs out of memory. Web Streams express the same idea through\n`desiredSize` and pull-based reads, and `pipeTo` handles it for you.\n\n### Cancellation\nThe other direction matters as much for AI traffic: when the user closes the tab, the upstream model\nis still generating tokens you are billed for. Node signals the hang-up as a `close` event on the\nresponse before it finished writing; wiring that to an `AbortController` whose `signal` was passed to\n`fetch` tears down the upstream connection. That saves money only if the upstream in turn stops\ngenerating when its client disconnects, so check that both hops propagate cancellation.\n\n### TypeScript: static types, no runtime\nTypeScript's types are erased at compile time. Inside your code they catch mistakes; at the boundary\nthey guarantee nothing, *because* the data arrives at runtime from a model, a webhook or an API that\nnever saw your types. That is why TypeScript AI code pairs a runtime schema library (Zod and similar)\nwith inferred static types: one schema validates the LLM's JSON, derives the TypeScript type, and can\nbe converted to JSON Schema for the model's tool definition. `strict` mode and exhaustive switches on\ndiscriminated unions are the other half of the discipline.\n\n### Where it fits in an AI system\nTypical Node/TS responsibilities: the chat UI and its streaming route handlers, a backend-for-frontend\nthat adds auth and rate limits in front of the Python service, serverless functions and edge\nmiddleware, MCP servers exposing product data as tools, and agent orchestration through the OpenAI,\nAnthropic and Vercel SDKs. Typical Python responsibilities: model serving, RAG pipelines, evals,\nfine-tuning, batch data jobs. The boundary is an HTTP (often SSE) contract; generating the TypeScript\nclient from the Python service's OpenAPI schema is how the two sides stay in sync.","example":"A minimal Node gateway that relays a streaming response from the Python core while honouring\nbackpressure and cancelling the upstream call when the client disconnects, plus a Zod schema that validates a model-produced tool call at runtime and provides the\nstatic type for free.\n\n```ts\nimport http from \"node:http\";\nimport { Readable } from \"node:stream\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst ToolCall = z.object({                       // runtime schema: TS types vanish at runtime\n  name: z.literal(\"lookup_order\"),\n  arguments: z.object({ orderId: z.string().regex(/^ord_[a-z0-9]+$/) }),\n});\ntype ToolCall = z.infer<typeof ToolCall>;        // static type derived from the runtime schema\n\nasync function* relay(upstream: Response): AsyncGenerator<string> {\n  const reader = upstream.body!.getReader();\n  const dec = new TextDecoder();\n  for (;;) {\n    const { value, done } = await reader.read();  // yields the event loop between chunks\n    if (done) return;\n    yield dec.decode(value, { stream: true });\n  }\n}\n\nhttp.createServer(async (req, res) => {\n  const ac = new AbortController();               // client hangs up -> cancel upstream generation\n  res.on(\"close\", () => { if (!res.writableFinished) ac.abort(); });\n  try {\n    const upstream = await fetch(\"http://python-core:8000/v1/chat\", {\n      method: \"POST\", headers: { \"content-type\": \"application/json\" }, signal: ac.signal,\n      body: Readable.toWeb(req) as ReadableStream, // forward the client body without buffering it\n      duplex: \"half\",\n    } as RequestInit);\n    res.writeHead(200, { \"content-type\": \"text/event-stream\" });\n    for await (const chunk of relay(upstream)) {\n      if (!res.write(chunk))                       // buffer past high-water mark: client is slow\n        await new Promise((r) => { res.once(\"drain\", r); res.once(\"close\", r); });\n    }\n    res.end();\n  } catch (e) { if (!ac.signal.aborted) res.destroy(e as Error); } // abort is expected, not an error\n}).listen(3000);\n\nconst parsed = ToolCall.safeParse(JSON.parse('{\"name\":\"lookup_order\",\"arguments\":{\"orderId\":\"ord_42x\"}}'));\nif (!parsed.success) console.error(parsed.error.issues);   // never trust model output structurally\n```","tradeoffs":["One language from browser to gateway cuts translation and team overhead, but the model, eval and data ecosystem stays in Python, so you pay for a cross-language contract instead.","The event loop makes streaming proxies cheap, but any CPU-bound step on the main thread blocks everyone; the balance flips when per-request compute grows and you need worker threads or Python.","Static types give refactoring safety, but they vanish at runtime, so every external boundary needs a schema library and the duplication that comes with it.","Serverless and edge deployment is frictionless for Node, but long-running agent loops and large streaming responses hit platform timeouts and body-size limits that a long-lived server never sees."],"pitfalls":["Parsing and validating model output with a TypeScript cast (`as ToolCall`); the symptom is a runtime `undefined is not a function` far from the boundary that produced the bad data.","Buffering a whole model stream before writing it to the client; the symptom is time-to-first-token equal to total generation time and memory spikes under concurrent chats.","Not propagating client disconnects upstream; the symptom is token spend that exceeds what users received, and open upstream connections that outnumber open client connections.","Running tokenization or large JSON transforms synchronously in a route handler; the symptom is every concurrent request's latency rising together, visible as event-loop lag.","Letting the Python service's API and the hand-written TypeScript client drift; the symptom is a field rename on one side surfacing as silent `undefined` on the other."],"when":"Use Node/TypeScript for the layer that faces the product: UI streaming routes, a backend-for-frontend,\nserverless glue, MCP servers for product data, and lightweight agent orchestration where the SDKs are\nexcellent. Keep inference, evaluation, fine-tuning and data pipelines in Python. If a team is entirely\nTypeScript and the AI work is API calls to a provider, a TypeScript-only stack is reasonable until\nevals or custom models appear. Measure event-loop lag (`perf_hooks.monitorEventLoopDelay` gives a\nhistogram), heap and RSS under many concurrent slow streams, and the ratio of upstream to client\nconnections; generate the client from\nOpenAPI rather than typing it by hand. In interviews, expect to explain why types are erased, how\nbackpressure works in a streaming proxy, and where the Python boundary belongs. The `node-ts-for-ai`\nmodule goes deep on each.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes streaming route handlers, validates model output with Zod, keeps CPU work off the main thread, and generates the client types from the Python service's OpenAPI schema.","architect":"Draws the TypeScript/Python boundary, chooses between a BFF and direct calls, and owns the contract-generation pipeline that keeps the two sides from drifting.","fde":"Meets customers whose product teams are TypeScript-only and must show how the AI core integrates through SSE and typed clients without forcing a Python rewrite on them."},"check":{"q":"A customer's TypeScript team wants to build the entire AI feature, including evals and a\nretrieval pipeline, in Node to avoid \"a second language\". What do you tell them, and where would you\ndraw the line?","a":"I would separate the edge from the core. The chat route, streaming, auth and the MCP tools can\nlive in TypeScript today with first-class SDKs, and that is where their team is strongest. Evals,\nembedding pipelines, reranking and any fine-tuning depend on Python-first libraries and the research\nworkflow; rewriting them in Node means maintaining ports of tools that move monthly. The line is\n\"anything that touches model weights, tokenizers or evaluation datasets is Python; anything that\ntouches the user is TypeScript\", with an OpenAPI-generated client as the contract so the second\nlanguage costs a build step rather than a translation team."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must a TypeScript service validate an LLM's JSON output with a runtime schema even though the response type is declared?","options":["Because JSON.parse returns `any` only in strict mode","TypeScript types are erased at compile time, so declared types guarantee nothing about data that arrives at runtime","Because LLM output is always a string and must be converted","Because Node's fetch strips type information from responses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Static types describe what the compiler assumes, not what the network delivers. A Zod-style schema checks the bytes that actually arrived and derives the static type from the same definition."},{"q":"Which operation blocks every other request in a Node gateway?","options":["Awaiting a 5-second streaming response from the model provider","Writing chunks to 500 concurrent SSE clients","Synchronously parsing a 50 MB JSON document in the route handler","Reading a secret from an environment variable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Node runs JavaScript on one thread; synchronous CPU work cannot be interrupted, so all pending callbacks wait. Awaited I/O and buffered writes yield to the loop between operations."},{"q":"In a mixed TypeScript/Python AI stack, which responsibility belongs in the Python core?","options":["The streaming chat route handler next to the React UI","Edge middleware that adds auth headers","The evaluation harness and embedding pipeline","An MCP server exposing the product's CRM records as tools"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Evals, embeddings and anything near model weights depend on Python-first tooling. Routes, middleware and MCP servers for product data are edge concerns where TypeScript's SDKs and team fit win."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","sources":["Fielding, 2000 — Architectural Styles and the Design of Network-based Software Architectures","IETF RFC 9110 — HTTP Semantics","IETF RFC 9457 — Problem Details for HTTP APIs","Official docs: GraphQL specification"],"why":"The moment a client ships against your interface, that interface is frozen. Design decisions made\ncasually on day one, an action-style endpoint named `/getUserOrders2`, an error returned as 200 with\n`{\"ok\": false}`, a delete that is not safe to retry, cost forever. API styles exist to make those\ndecisions by convention instead of by accident.\n\nREST's insight is to lean on HTTP rather than tunnel through it. If `GET` is safe and idempotent,\ncaches can store it and clients can retry it blind. If `PUT` and `DELETE` are idempotent, a timeout\ncan be retried without creating duplicates. If status codes carry meaning, load balancers can count\n5xx responses and retry policies can act on 429 and 503 without reading bodies. The naive alternative,\none `POST /api` endpoint with an `action` field in the body, throws all of that away: nothing can be\ncached, nothing is retry-safe by inspection, and every request looks identical to observability.\n\nGraphQL arose at Facebook (open-sourced in 2015) from a different pain: mobile clients needed many\ndifferent shapes of the same data, and REST either over-fetched (one fat resource) or under-fetched\n(three round trips to assemble a screen), with endpoints multiplying per screen. A typed schema plus\nclient-authored queries fixes that by moving shape selection to the client. The cost is giving up\nHTTP's per-resource semantics, which is why neither style dominates.\n\nFor AI services the question is sharper: model calls are big `POST` bodies with streaming responses,\nand tools exposed to an agent are easiest to describe as REST operations with an OpenAPI schema.","how":"### REST: resources, methods, status codes\nModel nouns, not verbs: `/conversations/{id}/messages`, not `/sendMessage`. Collections and items\nnest at most a level or two. Methods carry semantics defined by HTTP: `GET` is safe and idempotent and\nmay be cached; `PUT` replaces a resource and is idempotent; `DELETE` is idempotent; `PATCH` applies a\npartial change; `POST` creates or triggers and is *not* idempotent, which is why non-trivial APIs\naccept an `Idempotency-Key` header on it. Status codes are the first thing a client branches on:\n`200` and `201 Created`, `202 Accepted` for work that continues after the response, `204` for empty\nsuccess, `400`/`422` for invalid input, `401`/`403` for identity and permission, `404`, `409` for state\nconflicts, `429` for rate limits, `5xx` for your faults. Use one error envelope everywhere; Problem\nDetails (RFC 9457) gives a standard JSON shape with `type`, `title`, `status` and `detail`.\n\nIdempotency is about server state, not identical responses: a second `DELETE` of the same job may\nreturn `204` or `404` and both are idempotent, because the resource ends up gone either way. Pick one\nand document it, since clients write retry logic against it.\n\nConcurrent writers are HTTP's other built-in mechanism. A `GET` returns an `ETag` (a version tag for\nthe representation); a client that wants to update sends `If-Match: <etag>` with its `PUT` or `PATCH`,\nand the server answers `412 Precondition Failed` if someone else changed the resource in between.\nThat is optimistic concurrency without a custom `version` field, and it is what stops two editors (or\ntwo agents) from silently overwriting each other's changes to the same prompt template or config.\nThe same tag sent as `If-None-Match` on a `GET` lets the server answer `304 Not Modified` with no\nbody, which is how polling stays cheap.\n\nFielding's original definition also requires hypermedia (responses carrying links that tell the\nclient what it can do next, often called HATEOAS). Most production \"REST\" APIs skip it; the useful\nremnants are the `Location` header, `next` links in paginated responses and job resources that link\nto their result.\n\nLong-running AI work maps cleanly: `POST /summaries` returns `202` with a `Location` to a job resource\nthat clients `GET` until it is `succeeded`, or the response itself streams as `text/event-stream`.\nBoth keep proxies and timeouts happy *because* no single request has to live as long as the job.\n\n### GraphQL: schema, resolvers, one endpoint\nA schema in SDL declares types and fields and three roots: `Query`, `Mutation`, `Subscription`. A\nclient sends a query naming exactly the fields it wants; the server executes it by calling a resolver\nper field, walking the tree. Because resolvers run per field and per parent object, a list of 100\nconversations each resolving `lastMessage` naively issues 101 database queries; the standard fix is a\nper-request DataLoader that batches and caches lookups. In practice almost everything travels as\n`POST` to one endpoint, so HTTP caching does not apply unless you send queries (typically persisted\nones) over `GET`. Field errors are returned alongside partial data in an `errors` array, conventionally\nwith a 200 status, so clients must inspect the body; the GraphQL-over-HTTP spec lets servers using the\n`application/graphql-response+json` media type answer 4xx when a request fails before execution. Because\nclients author queries, the server must enforce depth and cost limits, *because* an unbounded nested\nquery is a denial-of-service vector. The spec does not fix a transport for subscriptions; most\nservers use WebSockets (the `graphql-ws` protocol), and SSE is a common alternative.\n\nGraphQL also changes how the contract evolves. There are no URL versions: you add fields freely\n(clients only receive what they ask for, so additions never break anyone), mark old fields\n`@deprecated(reason: ...)`, and remove them once field-level usage telemetry shows zero queries\nselecting them. That telemetry is something REST cannot give you per field without extra work, and it\nis one of GraphQL's quieter advantages for a fast-moving product schema.\n\n### The decision\nAsk who the clients are and how many shapes they need. One product UI with screens assembled from a\nrich domain, owned by a team that iterates weekly: GraphQL pays off. Service-to-service calls, public\nAPIs for integrators, anything cached by CDNs, anything exposed to an LLM as a tool: REST with an\nOpenAPI schema, because the schema generates clients and tool definitions directly. Many teams run\nboth: GraphQL in the product's BFF, REST underneath and outward.","example":"A REST design for a long-running summarisation job in FastAPI: `POST` returns `202` with a `Location`,\n`GET` is safe to poll, `DELETE` is idempotent. The GraphQL SDL after it expresses the same domain for a\nclient that wants to pick fields and subscribe to updates.\n\n```python\nfrom uuid import uuid4\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Response, status\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\napp = FastAPI()\nJOBS: dict[str, dict] = {}                          # stand-in for a database\n\nclass SummarizeRequest(BaseModel):\n    document_id: str\n    max_words: int = 200\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/summaries\", status_code=status.HTTP_202_ACCEPTED)\nasync def create_summary(body: SummarizeRequest, response: Response):\n    job_id = f\"sum_{uuid4().hex[:12]}\"\n    JOBS[job_id] = {\"id\": job_id, \"status\": \"queued\", \"input\": body.model_dump(), \"result\": None}\n    # enqueue to a worker here; the HTTP request returns immediately\n    response.headers[\"Location\"] = f\"/v1/summaries/{job_id}\"   # where to poll\n    return JOBS[job_id]\n\n@app.get(\"/v1/summaries/{job_id}\")                  # GET is safe and idempotent: poll freely\nasync def get_summary(job_id: str):\n    job = JOBS.get(job_id)\n    if job is None:\n        # FastAPI wraps this as {\"detail\": {...}}; a real service maps errors to RFC 9457 in a handler\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail={\"type\": \"not_found\", \"job_id\": job_id})\n    return job                                       # 200 whether queued or done; status is in the body\n\n@app.delete(\"/v1/summaries/{job_id}\", status_code=204)   # idempotent: a second DELETE is also 204\nasync def cancel_summary(job_id: str):\n    JOBS.pop(job_id, None)\n    return Response(status_code=204)\n```\n\n```graphql\nenum JobStatus { QUEUED RUNNING SUCCEEDED FAILED }\ntype Summary { id: ID!  status: JobStatus!  result: String }\ntype Query { summary(id: ID!): Summary }\ntype Mutation { createSummary(documentId: ID!, maxWords: Int = 200): Summary! }\ntype Subscription { summaryUpdated(id: ID!): Summary! }\n```","tradeoffs":["REST gets HTTP caching, retry-safety by method and tooling for free, but a screen needing five resources costs five round trips; this flips to GraphQL when client-side shape variety dominates.","GraphQL gives clients exact shapes and one evolving schema, but you give up HTTP caching, must defend against expensive queries and must batch resolvers; it usually flips back to REST for public APIs, where caching and per-endpoint limits matter (GitHub and Shopify are notable GraphQL exceptions).","Returning `202` and a job resource keeps requests short and proxy-friendly, but clients must poll or subscribe, which costs more code than one long request when jobs are reliably under a few seconds.","A single error envelope and strict status codes make clients simple, but they force you to decide the code for every failure up front rather than leaking exceptions as 500s."],"pitfalls":["Verbs in URLs and everything as `POST`; the symptom is that nothing can be cached or safely retried and observability shows one undifferentiated endpoint.","Returning errors with status 200 and a flag in the body; the symptom is retry and alerting policies that never fire and SDKs that treat failures as success.","GraphQL resolvers without DataLoader; the symptom is query count scaling with list length in the database logs (the N+1 pattern).","Holding a request open for a multi-minute AI job; the symptom is intermittent 504s from a proxy whose idle timeout you never configured, and clients retrying work that already ran.","Read-modify-write updates without `ETag`/`If-Match` or a version field; the symptom is \"my change disappeared\" reports when two users or two agents edit the same resource within seconds.","Leaving GraphQL query depth and cost unlimited; the symptom is one client's nested query pinning the database while everything else times out."],"when":"Default to REST with an OpenAPI schema for AI service endpoints, internal service-to-service calls\nand anything an agent will call as a tool; the schema becomes the client, the docs and the tool\ndefinition. Reach for GraphQL when a product team owns a UI that assembles many shapes from a shared\ndomain and is paying for it in round trips and endpoint sprawl. Measure round trips per screen,\ncache hit rate and p99 latency before switching either way. Day to day, the work is naming resources,\nchoosing status codes deliberately and designing the long-running-job pattern. In interviews, expect\n\"design the API for X\" questions that test whether you reach for idempotency, `202` plus polling or\nstreaming, and a consistent error shape without being prompted.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Designs resource paths and status codes, implements `202` plus polling or SSE for model calls, and keeps the OpenAPI schema accurate enough to generate clients and tool definitions.","architect":"Decides which style each boundary uses (GraphQL in the BFF, REST between services and outward), sets the error envelope and idempotency conventions, and reviews breaking changes.","fde":"Maps a customer's existing REST or GraphQL conventions onto the AI service's contract and explains why model endpoints need async job patterns their CRUD APIs never required."},"check":{"q":"You are designing the API for a document-analysis service whose jobs take 30 seconds to 5\nminutes and whose results are read by a web UI, a partner integration and an internal agent. Which\nstyle and request pattern do you choose, and why?","a":"REST with an OpenAPI schema, because three different clients including an agent need a\ncontract that generates clients and tool definitions, and partners expect it. `POST /analyses` returns\n`202 Accepted` with a `Location` and a job resource; clients `GET` it until `status` is terminal, or\nsubscribe to an SSE stream of progress events for the UI. The `POST` accepts an `Idempotency-Key` so a\npartner's retry after a timeout does not run the job twice. Results live at `/analyses/{id}/result`\nand are cacheable once terminal. I would not pick GraphQL here: the shapes are few, the clients are\nmostly machines, and long-running work plus caching favour HTTP semantics."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why return `202 Accepted` with a `Location` header for a five-minute AI job instead of holding the request open until it finishes?","options":["Because `200` cannot carry a JSON body larger than the proxy buffer","Proxies and clients have idle timeouts, and a short request plus a pollable job resource survives them and is retry-safe","Because `202` enables HTTP caching of the eventual result","Because FastAPI cannot await for more than 60 seconds"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Long-held connections hit proxy and client timeouts and make retries ambiguous. A job resource turns the work into state that can be polled, cached when terminal and cancelled."},{"q":"Why can an ordinary HTTP cache not store GraphQL responses by default?","options":["GraphQL responses are always compressed","The GraphQL spec forbids caching","Queries go as `POST` to one endpoint, so the URL does not identify the resource being read","GraphQL responses never include a `Content-Type` header"],"answer":2,"explanation":"HTTP caches key on method and URL. With every query a `POST` to `/graphql`, there is nothing to key on; persisted queries over `GET` are the workaround."},{"q":"A GraphQL list of 100 conversations resolving `lastMessage` issues 101 database queries. What is the standard fix?","options":["Switch the field to a REST endpoint","A per-request DataLoader that batches and caches the lookups","Increase the database connection pool to 101","Mark the field as `@deprecated`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Resolvers run per parent object, so without batching each one queries alone. DataLoader collects the keys requested in one tick and issues a single batched query."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/api-and-sdk-integration","sources":["Official docs: httpx","Official docs: OpenAI Python SDK","Official docs: Anthropic Python SDK","Nygard, 2007 — Release It!"],"why":"A model provider, a vector database, a CRM, a payment processor: each is a remote system that will\nreturn 429 when you are busiest, 503 during its deploys, a half-finished stream when a load balancer\nrecycles, and a slightly different JSON shape after a release you did not read about. Your code must\nplan for every one of those, and the place that plan lives is the integration layer.\n\nThe naive version is `requests.post(url, json=payload)` sprinkled through the codebase. It has no\ntimeout (the `requests` library applies none by default), so a hung provider pins a worker forever.\nIt has no retry, so a transient 503 becomes a user-visible failure. It raises raw exceptions, so\ncallers cannot tell \"retry later\" from \"your request is invalid\". Its API key is read in twelve\nplaces, so rotation is a treasure hunt. The symptom of this design is an incident where every worker\nis blocked waiting on one vendor and the dashboard shows zero errors.\n\nThe opposite naive version is \"the SDK handles it\". It partly does: official SDKs retry on 429 and\n5xx with backoff, parse streams and type the responses. But SDK defaults are generic. Automatic\nretries silently multiply the latency of a slow outage, and the cost too whenever an attempt the\nprovider already processed is abandoned and resent; default timeouts in provider SDKs (on the order of\nten minutes in the major Python LLM SDKs at the time of writing) are long enough to let one stuck call\nhold a request for minutes; and an SDK upgrade can change the API version it speaks. You still need a layer that owns those decisions explicitly.","how":"### Raw HTTP versus SDK\nAn SDK gives you: authentication headers, a base URL, typed request and response models, retry with\nexponential backoff on retryable statuses (honouring `Retry-After`), SSE parsing into events,\nauto-pagination iterators, and matching sync and async clients. It hides: the connection pool, the\ntimeout values, which calls it considers safe to retry, and the mapping from HTTP status to exception\nclass. Read the client constructor for `timeout` and `max_retries` before shipping; those two numbers\ndefine your worst-case latency.\n\n### The adapter: one module owns the vendor\nWrap each external system in one module that exposes your own types and raises your own exceptions.\nThis exists *because* vendor types and errors leak otherwise, and swapping or adding a provider becomes\na rewrite. Inside the adapter, classify failures into a taxonomy:\n\n- **Retryable:** 429, 5xx (most often 502/503/504), connection errors, timeouts, truncated streams.\n- **Terminal:** 400/422 (your request is wrong), 401/403 (credentials), 404, content-policy refusals.\n- **Ambiguous:** a timeout on a non-idempotent call; the request may have completed.\n\n### Timeouts\nSet connect, read and total timeouts separately. In httpx and most HTTP clients the read timeout is\nthe longest allowed gap between received bytes, not a deadline for the whole response: on a stream it\nbounds time-to-first-token and every inter-chunk gap, and on a non-streaming model call (where the\nserver sends nothing until generation finishes) it effectively bounds generation time. A streaming call\nmay legitimately run for minutes while each chunk arrives within a second, so the read timeout is a\nper-chunk budget. A total deadline needs a separate wrapper (`asyncio.timeout` around the call); tie it\nto what the user-facing request can afford, not to what the provider allows.\n\n### Retries, idempotency and budgets\nRetry retryable errors with exponential backoff and full jitter (sleep a uniform random time in\n`[0, min(cap, base × 2^n)]`), and honour `Retry-After` when present, *because* synchronized retries from many clients create a thundering\nherd. Retry only operations that are idempotent or carry an idempotency key; an LLM completion is\nusually safe to re-run (it costs money, not correctness), while a tool call that sends an email is\nnot. Bound retries per request and per time window so an outage does not triple your traffic.\n\nThe arithmetic of a retry policy is worth doing once. With *a* attempts, per-attempt timeout *t* and\ntotal backoff sleep *S*, worst-case latency is `a·t + S`, and during a full outage the load you\nsend the vendor is multiplied by up to *a*. Layering makes it worse: if your adapter makes 3 attempts\naround an SDK that makes 3 per call (its default of 2 retries), one user request can become 9\nupstream calls. Retry in exactly one layer and switch the others off (`max_retries=0` on the SDK client).\n\n### Streams fail differently\nA streaming call can return `200`, send half the answer and then stop: a load balancer recycled the\nconnection, or the provider hit an internal error mid-generation. The status code already said\nsuccess, so the only signal is the missing terminal event (a final `finish_reason`, a stop event or a\n`[DONE]` sentinel, depending on the provider). The adapter must treat a stream that ends without its\nterminal event as a retryable failure, not as a short answer. Retrying a stream is also not free of\nuser impact: tokens already shown cannot be unshown, so the choice is to restart and replace the\npartial text, or to surface the truncation and let the user retry.\n\n### Circuit breakers, fallbacks and connection reuse\nAfter N consecutive failures stop calling the vendor for a cool-down and fail fast or route to a\nfallback model; this protects your own latency and the vendor's recovery. Keep one client per process\nso TLS handshakes and connections are reused; creating a client per request is a common source of\nsocket exhaustion.\n\n### Configuration, observability and tests\nKeys come from a secret store or environment, never source; log the vendor's request id, status,\nlatency, attempt count and token usage on every call with a correlation id. Pin SDK versions and any\nAPI-version header. Test with recorded responses for happy paths and injected 429/timeout/truncation\nfor the failure paths, and run a small contract test against the live sandbox on a schedule.","example":"A minimal adapter around a model endpoint using `httpx`: explicit per-phase timeouts, a shared client,\nan error taxonomy, bounded retries with jitter that honour `Retry-After`, an idempotency key so a\nretried `POST` cannot double-execute, and one total deadline that the retries must fit inside.\n\nWalk the worst case to see why the deadline matters. Without it, three attempts that each hit the\n60-second read timeout plus backoff sleeps can hold the caller for over three minutes. With a\n20-second budget, the first attempt times out at the deadline and the caller gets a `TimeoutError` at\n20 seconds that it can turn into a fallback or a clear error. A `Retry-After: 30` hint on a 429 is\nalso handled correctly: it cannot fit in the remaining budget, so the adapter fails immediately\ninstead of sleeping past the point where the user has given up.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, os, random\nimport httpx\n\nclass RetryableError(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, msg: str, retry_after: float | None = None):\n        super().__init__(msg); self.retry_after = retry_after\n\nclass TerminalError(Exception): ...\n\nTIMEOUT = httpx.Timeout(connect=5.0, read=60.0, write=10.0, pool=5.0)   # explicit, per phase\nclient = httpx.AsyncClient(base_url=\"https://api.example-llm.com\", timeout=TIMEOUT,\n                           headers={\"Authorization\": \"Bearer \" + os.environ[\"LLM_API_KEY\"]})\n\ndef classify(r: httpx.Response) -> None:\n    if r.status_code < 400:\n        return\n    if r.status_code == 429 or r.status_code >= 500:\n        ra = r.headers.get(\"retry-after\")\n        raise RetryableError(f\"HTTP {r.status_code}\", float(ra) if ra and ra.isdigit() else None)\n    raise TerminalError(f\"HTTP {r.status_code}: {r.text[:200]}\")   # 400/401/403/422: retrying won't help\n\nasync def complete(payload: dict, idem_key: str, attempts: int = 3, budget_s: float = 20.0) -> dict:\n    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\n    deadline = loop.time() + budget_s                  # total budget across ALL attempts and sleeps\n    async with asyncio.timeout_at(deadline):           # Python 3.11+; raises TimeoutError at the deadline\n        for n in range(attempts):\n            try:\n                r = await client.post(\"/v1/complete\", json=payload,\n                                      headers={\"Idempotency-Key\": idem_key})   # safe to retry\n                classify(r)\n                return r.json()\n            except (RetryableError, httpx.TimeoutException, httpx.TransportError) as e:\n                hinted = getattr(e, \"retry_after\", None)\n                delay = hinted if hinted else random.uniform(0, min(8.0, 2 ** n))  # full jitter\n                if n == attempts - 1 or loop.time() + delay >= deadline:\n                    raise                              # retrying cannot finish inside the budget\n                await asyncio.sleep(delay)\n    raise RuntimeError(\"unreachable\")\n```","tradeoffs":["An SDK saves weeks of client code and tracks API changes for you, but it chooses retry and timeout defaults you must override, and it couples you to its release cadence; raw HTTP flips to the better choice only for tiny surfaces or unsupported languages.","Automatic retries hide transient failures from users, but each retry costs money and latency and can amplify an outage; the balance flips when the operation is non-idempotent or the budget is tight.","A thin adapter per vendor adds a layer of types and mapping, but it makes provider swaps, fallbacks and tests cheap; for a single-vendor prototype it can feel like ceremony.","Aggressive timeouts protect your latency budget, but they turn slow successes into failures and create ambiguous outcomes on non-idempotent calls."],"pitfalls":["No timeout or an inherited multi-minute default; the symptom is worker pools draining during a vendor incident while error rates stay at zero.","Retrying everything, including 400 and 401; the symptom is the same invalid request hammering the vendor four times and a rate-limit ban on top of the original bug.","Retrying a non-idempotent `POST` after a timeout without an idempotency key; the symptom is duplicate records, double charges or two emails sent.","Retrying in two layers (your wrapper and the SDK, or a gateway and the app); the symptom is vendor-side request counts several times your own request count during an incident.","Treating a stream that ended without its terminal event as complete; the symptom is answers that stop mid-sentence and parsers failing on truncated JSON with no error in the logs.","A new client per request; the symptom is a TLS handshake on every call and \"too many open files\" under load.","Parsing vendor error bodies ad hoc in callers; the symptom is a schema change in the error payload breaking twelve call sites at once."],"when":"Every external call deserves the adapter treatment, scaled to its blast radius: a model provider on\nthe request path gets timeouts, retries, idempotency keys, a breaker and metrics; a nightly report\nAPI can live with a timeout and a retry. Use the official SDK when one exists and you have read its\ntimeout and retry settings; drop to `httpx` when the SDK lacks a feature, blocks the event loop, or\nyou need exact control over streaming. Measure attempt counts, p99 latency including retries, and\nvendor request ids in logs. In interviews, \"how do you call a third-party API in production\" is a\nstandard question; the expected answer walks timeouts, retry policy, idempotency, error taxonomy and\nobservability in that order.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds and tests the adapter: explicit timeouts, retry with jitter, idempotency keys, one shared client, and error mapping that callers can branch on.","architect":"Sets the integration standard (which errors retry, the retry budget, breaker thresholds, fallback routing) and decides where provider abstraction lives.","fde":"Integrates the AI service with a customer's internal APIs whose SDKs may be absent or unreliable, and negotiates sandbox access, rate limits and credentials for them."},"check":{"q":"Your service calls a model provider through its official SDK with defaults. During a provider\nincident, user requests take four minutes to fail and your token spend for the hour jumps well above\nnormal. Explain what happened and how you would configure the integration differently.","a":"The SDK retried each failing call (twice by default in the major LLM SDKs) with backoff, and its\nlong default timeout let each attempt wait minutes, so one user request became several long attempts.\nEach retry resent the full prompt, and attempts the provider had partly or fully processed before the\nconnection died or the client gave up are generally still billed, while users who saw failures retried\non top; that is the cost spike (exactly which failed requests are billed is provider-specific). I would set an explicit total timeout tied to the user-facing\nbudget, cap retries to one or two with jitter and honour `Retry-After`, add a circuit breaker that\nfails fast or routes to a fallback model after consecutive failures, and emit attempt-count and\nlatency metrics so the behaviour is visible. Idempotency keys would make the remaining retries safe\nif the call had side effects."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which failure should an integration layer not retry automatically?","options":["A 503 from the provider's load balancer","A connect timeout","A 422 saying a required field is missing","A 429 with a `Retry-After` header"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retries help only when the next attempt can succeed. A validation error is deterministic, so retrying repeats the same bad request, wastes quota and delays the real fix."},{"q":"Why add an `Idempotency-Key` header when retrying a `POST` after a timeout?","options":["It makes the request faster on the second attempt","The first attempt may have completed, and the key lets the server return that result instead of executing again","It tells the server to skip authentication on retries","It disables the server's rate limiter for the retry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A timeout is ambiguous: the server may have done the work. An idempotency key lets it recognise the repeat and return the stored outcome, preventing duplicate side effects."},{"q":"What is the main reason to keep one HTTP client per process rather than per request?","options":["Per-request clients cannot send custom headers","A single client guarantees ordered delivery of requests","Connection and TLS session reuse, plus one place to configure timeouts and limits","Multiple clients would share the same API key incorrectly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each new client opens fresh connections and TLS handshakes, which cost latency and sockets. A shared client pools connections and centralises the configuration the whole service depends on."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","sources":["IETF RFC 8594 — The Sunset HTTP Header Field","Official docs: Stripe API versioning","Official docs: Anthropic API versions","Winters, Manshreck & Wright, 2020 — Software Engineering at Google (Hyrum's Law)"],"why":"The clients of an API are distributed in time as well as space: a mobile app that users never update,\na partner integration built by a contractor who left, an SDK pinned in a lockfile, an agent whose\nsystem prompt describes your tool's arguments. Rename a field and each of them breaks silently at its\nown pace, and you cannot roll them back. Hyrum's law states the underlying fact: with enough users,\nevery observable behaviour of your interface becomes something somebody depends on, documented or not.\n\nThe first naive response is \"never break anything\", which freezes bad decisions forever and bloats\nthe API with fields kept only for one client. The second is \"bump to `/v2` whenever we change\nsomething\", which produces version sprawl: each version is a fork to maintain, test and secure, and\nclients never migrate because nothing forces them. The third is \"be careful\", which fails the first\ntime a well-meaning engineer makes a required field optional in the response and a strict client\nstarts throwing.\n\nThe working discipline sits between these: make additive, compatible changes the normal path so that\nversions are rare; when a break is unavoidable, version deliberately, announce a deprecation with\ndates, measure who is still on the old version, and remove it on schedule. For AI services the\ndiscipline matters twice over, because a model or prompt change can alter outputs for the same input,\nwhich is also a breaking change from the client's point of view.","how":"### What counts as breaking\nBreaking for clients: removing or renaming a field or endpoint; changing a field's type or format\n(integer to string, seconds to milliseconds); making an optional request field required; tightening\nvalidation; changing a default; changing the meaning of a value; changing status codes or the error\nenvelope; changing authentication. Usually compatible: adding an optional request field, adding a\nresponse field, adding an endpoint, relaxing validation. Contested: adding an enum value, which breaks\nclients that switch exhaustively, so document that enums are open and clients must handle unknowns.\n\n### Tolerant readers\nCompatibility is a two-sided contract. Clients should ignore unknown response fields and treat enums\nas open; servers should accept missing optional fields and ignore unknown request fields unless the\nendpoint is security-sensitive. Pydantic's default of ignoring extra fields is the tolerant choice;\n`extra=\"forbid\"` is stricter and turns a client's harmless new field into a 422, so choose it\nknowingly. This is Postel's principle applied with judgement, *because* total leniency hides bugs.\n\n### Versioning strategies\n- **URL path** (`/v1/...`, as OpenAI does): visible, routable, easy to test and cache; coarse, since\n  the whole API moves together.\n- **Header** (`Accept: application/vnd.acme.v2+json`, or a custom header such as Anthropic's\n  `anthropic-version` date): keeps URLs stable and allows per-request choice; harder to try in a\n  browser and easy to forget in a proxy.\n- **Pinned date per account** (Stripe's approach): each account is pinned to the version current\n  when it first called the API, overridable per request with a `Stripe-Version` header; the server\n  applies a chain of transformations to render new behaviour in old shapes. Maximum compatibility, maximum server-side machinery.\n- **Query parameter** (`?version=2`): simple but mixes with filters and cache keys.\n- **Evolution only** (GraphQL's stance): no versions; add fields, mark old ones `@deprecated`, remove\n  after usage reaches zero.\n\nWhichever you choose, keep one implementation and render versions at the edge as representations,\n*because* two forked code paths drift and the old one stops getting fixes.\n\n### Deprecation lifecycle\nAnnounce in docs and changelog; emit `Sunset` (RFC 8594, an HTTP date) and a `Link` to the migration\nguide on old-version responses, and mark the route deprecated in OpenAPI. RFC 9745 (2025) adds a\n`Deprecation` header whose value is a structured-field date (`@` followed by Unix seconds) and a\n`deprecation` link relation for the migration guide. Measure requests per\nversion per client so you know who is left. Run brownouts (short planned outages of the old version)\nbefore removal. Remove on the announced date.\n\n### Catching breaks before they ship\nReview by eye misses breaks, so make them mechanical. Two complementary checks run in CI. A schema\ndiff compares the OpenAPI document generated from the branch against the one published from `main`\nand fails on any change in the breaking list (removed field, narrowed type, new required input); it\ncatches what the provider changed. Consumer-driven contract tests (the Pact style) go the other way:\neach client publishes the requests it sends and the fields it reads, and the provider's build replays\nthem; this catches the Hyrum's-law dependencies the schema never promised, such as a client relying\non list order.\n\n### SDKs and client libraries\nA published SDK has its own compatibility surface on top of the wire API: method names, exception\nclasses, default timeouts and retries. Version it with semantic versioning, so a breaking change to any\nof those is a major bump even when the HTTP API did not change, and decouple the SDK version from the\nAPI version it speaks (pinning the API version inside the SDK release is common) so upgrading the\nlibrary is not silently also an API migration.\n\n### Data and schema changes: expand and contract\nRename a column or field in three phases. Expand: add the new field and populate both, so old and\nnew readers both work. Migrate: move every consumer to the new field, verified by telemetry rather\nthan by asking. Contract: stop writing the old field, then remove it. The order matters *because* at\nevery point in time each deployed producer and consumer, old or new, still sees a field it\nunderstands, which is what lets the two sides deploy independently. The same pattern applies to message schemas and tool arguments.\n\n### AI-specific contracts\nModel providers retire model versions; pin exact snapshot names and track deprecation notices. A\nprompt revision that changes output format is a contract change for everything downstream, so version\nprompts alongside code. A tool schema change can break an agent that learned the old arguments from\nits prompt or few-shot examples. Re-embedding with a new model invalidates an index built with the old\none, which is versioning applied to data.","example":"Two versioned routers in FastAPI sharing one implementation. The v2 shape renamed `summary` to\n`text` and added citations, a breaking change, so v1 is kept as a thin representation with a `Sunset`\nheader and an OpenAPI deprecation flag rather than as a forked code path.\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import APIRouter, FastAPI, Response\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict\n\nclass SummaryV2(BaseModel):             # current shape: renamed field, structured citations\n    id: str\n    text: str\n    citations: list[dict]\n    model: str\n\nclass SummaryV1(BaseModel):             # frozen shape old clients depend on\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"ignore\")   # Pydantic's default, spelled out: unknown input dropped\n    id: str\n    summary: str                        # renamed to `text` in v2, hence the shim below\n\nasync def summarize(doc_id: str) -> SummaryV2:\n    return SummaryV2(id=\"sum_1\", text=\"...\", citations=[{\"doc\": doc_id, \"span\": [0, 40]}],\n                     model=\"summarizer-2026-03-01\")   # pinned model snapshot is part of the contract\n\nv1, v2 = APIRouter(prefix=\"/v1\"), APIRouter(prefix=\"/v2\")\n\n@v2.post(\"/summaries\", response_model=SummaryV2)\nasync def create_v2(doc_id: str):\n    return await summarize(doc_id)\n\n@v1.post(\"/summaries\", response_model=SummaryV1, deprecated=True)   # flagged in OpenAPI docs\nasync def create_v1(doc_id: str, response: Response):\n    s = await summarize(doc_id)                      # one implementation, two representations\n    response.headers[\"Deprecation\"] = \"@1767225600\"            # RFC 9745: deprecated since 2026-01-01\n    response.headers[\"Sunset\"] = \"Sat, 31 Oct 2026 00:00:00 GMT\"  # RFC 8594: removal date\n    response.headers[\"Link\"] = '</docs/migrate-v2>; rel=\"deprecation\"'\n    return SummaryV1(id=s.id, summary=s.text)        # render the old shape; drop fields v1 never promised\n\napp = FastAPI()\napp.include_router(v1)\napp.include_router(v2)\n```","tradeoffs":["Additive-only evolution keeps every client working with no migration, but the API accumulates legacy fields and awkward names; the balance flips to a versioned break when the old shape actively misleads new integrators.","Path versioning is simple and visible, but it moves the whole API at once; header or date pinning allows fine-grained evolution at the cost of transformation code and harder debugging.","Stripe-style per-account pinning gives clients indefinite stability, but every change adds a transformation layer the server must maintain and test forever.","Strict request validation (`extra=\"forbid\"`) catches client typos early, but it turns harmless additive changes by clients into outages; loosen it on public endpoints, keep it on internal ones."],"pitfalls":["Shipping a field rename as a \"small cleanup\"; the symptom is a partner integration failing days later with `KeyError` on a field you removed.","Forking the codebase per version; the symptom is a bug fixed in v2 that still ships in v1 and a security patch applied to only one of them.","Announcing a deprecation without measuring usage; the symptom is removing v1 on the date and discovering the largest customer was still on it.","Treating model or prompt updates as invisible; the symptom is downstream parsers breaking because the new model stopped emitting the code fence the old one always produced.","Clients that switch exhaustively on an enum; the symptom is a crash the day a new status value is added, even though no field changed."],"when":"Apply the compatibility rules to every change on a published interface: review each diff of a\nresponse or request model for the breaking list above, and treat prompt and model changes with the\nsame review. Introduce a version only when a break is unavoidable, and then introduce exactly one.\nMeasure requests per version per client, error rates after a release, and time-to-migrate for\ndeprecated versions. In day-to-day work this shows up as contract tests in CI that fail on breaking\nschema diffs and as release notes written for integrators. Interviews probe whether you can list\nwhat breaks, pick a strategy with reasons, and describe a deprecation lifecycle with dates and\ntelemetry rather than hope.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps changes additive, renders old versions as representations over one implementation, and wires contract tests that fail on breaking OpenAPI diffs.","architect":"Chooses the versioning strategy and deprecation policy, decides what is a published contract versus internal, and extends the policy to models, prompts and tool schemas.","fde":"Shields customer integrations from provider model deprecations and your own API changes by pinning versions and planning migrations with the customer's release calendar."},"check":{"q":"A product team wants to change the `confidence` field in your extraction API from a 0–100\ninteger to a 0–1 float, and to add a `reasons` array. How do you ship this without breaking the three\nexternal integrators on the endpoint?","a":"The `reasons` array is additive and ships immediately. The `confidence` change is breaking:\nsame name, different type and scale, so a client that thresholds at 80 would silently treat every\nresult as low confidence. Options: add a new field (`confidence_score` as a float), keep `confidence`\nas the integer, and deprecate it with a `Sunset` header and docs, removing it after telemetry shows\nzero use; or, if the API is already versioned, make the change in the next version and render the old\nshape from one implementation. I would avoid a bare `/v2` for one field. Either way, contract tests\nin CI should fail on the type change so it cannot ship by accident."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which change is backward compatible for a typical JSON API client?","options":["Renaming `summary` to `text` in the response","Adding an optional `citations` array to the response","Changing `created_at` from seconds to milliseconds","Making the optional `language` request field required"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tolerant clients ignore fields they do not know, so additions are safe. Renames, unit changes and newly required inputs all change what existing clients send or read."},{"q":"Why does Stripe-style per-account date pinning cost more on the server than path versioning?","options":["Dates are harder to parse than path segments","The server must maintain a chain of transformations that renders current behaviour in every older pinned shape","Pinned accounts cannot use the SDKs","It requires a separate database per version"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each pinned version is a representation the server must keep producing from the current implementation, so every change adds a transformation to maintain and test indefinitely."},{"q":"What should happen before an old API version is removed?","options":["Nothing; the new version has been available for a month","Return 500 from the old version so clients notice","Announce a sunset date, emit deprecation headers, measure remaining usage per client, and run brownouts","Silently redirect old-version requests to the new one"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Removal is safe only when you know who is left and they have been warned in-band and out-of-band. Brownouts surface stragglers before the hard cutoff; silent redirects hide breaking shape changes."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","sources":["IETF RFC 6585 — Additional HTTP Status Codes","IETF RFC 9110 — HTTP Semantics","Winand, 2012 — SQL Performance Explained","Official docs: Stripe API"],"why":"Start with the unbounded list. `GET /documents` for a tenant with two million rows: the database\nmaterialises every row, the server serialises hundreds of megabytes of JSON, the client tries to parse\nit, and somewhere in the middle a 30-second timeout fires, so the client retries and doubles the load.\nPagination exists because response size has to be bounded by the server, not by the client's good\nmanners. The obvious fix, `?offset=1000&limit=50`, works until the set is large or moving: page 20,000\nmakes the engine produce and discard a million rows, and a row inserted while the client is on page 3\nshifts every later page, so items are skipped or shown twice.\n\nRate limits exist because capacity is shared. A connection pool, a CPU, a model provider's\ntokens-per-minute allowance: one client's burst (a retry storm, a batch script, a scraper) takes\ncapacity from everyone else and cascades into timeouts upstream. \"Just scale up\" fails on three counts:\nyou cannot scale a provider's limit, bursts arrive faster than autoscaling reacts, and unlimited\ncapacity is an unlimited bill. A limiter is also a signal. `429` plus `Retry-After` tells a well-behaved\nclient exactly when to come back, which turns overload into orderly queueing instead of a pile-up.\n\nQuotas exist because a rate limit protects the system *this minute* and says nothing about a tenant\nthat runs at the limit continuously for a month. When every call costs money, which is true of every\nLLM call, you need an entitlement: not how fast, but how much. Without one, a free-tier user can run a\nbatch job that costs more than their lifetime revenue, and nobody notices until the invoice.","how":"### Pagination: offset, keyset, cursor\n**Offset** (`ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 50 OFFSET 1000`) forces the engine to produce the first\n1,050 rows in order and throw 1,000 away. Cost grows linearly with depth and the window drifts under\nconcurrent writes. It is acceptable for small, static sets where the UI needs \"page 7 of 12\".\n\n**Keyset** (also called seek) pagination remembers the sort key of the last row and asks for rows after\nit: `WHERE (created_at, id) < (:last_ts, :last_id) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT 50`. With an\nindex on `(created_at, id)` the engine seeks directly to that position, so every page costs roughly\nO(log n + page size) regardless of depth, and inserts do not shift the window *because* position is\ndefined by a value rather than a count. The `id` tie-breaker is there because `created_at` is not\nunique; without it, rows sharing a timestamp at a page boundary get skipped or repeated.\n\n**Cursor** pagination wraps the keyset values in an opaque token (base64 JSON, optionally HMAC-signed)\nand returns `{\"data\": [...], \"next_cursor\": \"...\", \"has_more\": true}`. The token is opaque *because* it\nis part of your contract: if clients can read it they will build it, and you can never change the sort\nor the key again. Bind the cursor to the query that produced it (include the sort and a hash of the\nfilters, or sign it) so a client that reuses a cursor with different filters gets a clear 400 rather\nthan a silently wrong page. Fetch `limit + 1` rows to compute `has_more` without a count. Enforce a default and a\nmaximum page size on the server; clients will ask for 10,000. Omit or estimate totals, because\n`COUNT(*)` over a large table is a full scan on every page. As a consumer, loop until `has_more` is\nfalse and treat an invalid cursor as \"start over\". An agent tool that returns a list needs the same\ndiscipline: one page per call, with `has_more` surfaced to the model, or one tool result eats the\ncontext window.\n\n### Rate limiting: algorithms, placement, signals\n- **Token bucket:** a bucket of capacity *b* refilled at *r* tokens per second; each request takes one\n  token (or *n* for token-metered APIs); an empty bucket means reject or wait. It allows a burst of *b*\n  and a sustained rate of *r*, which is what most APIs want *because* real clients are bursty and\n  shedding a five-request burst from an otherwise idle client helps nobody.\n- **Leaky bucket:** requests queue and drain at a fixed rate; the output is smooth at the cost of\n  queueing delay. Use it in front of a downstream that cannot absorb bursts at all.\n- **Fixed window:** `INCR limit:{key}:{minute}` with a TTL. One command, but a client can send a full\n  window's worth at 11:59:59 and again at 12:00:00, so the real peak is twice the nominal limit.\n- **Sliding window log:** keep every request timestamp in a sorted set and count those inside the\n  trailing window; exact, but memory grows with traffic. **Sliding window counter:** weight the\n  previous window's count by its overlap with the trailing window; approximate, cheap, usually enough.\n- **Concurrency limit:** cap requests *in flight* rather than requests per second. Rate limits alone\n  do not bound concurrency when requests are long: by Little's law, in-flight = arrival rate ×\n  duration, so 10 requests per second of 30-second streaming completions means about 300 open\n  streams, each holding a connection and a slot on the model server. LLM gateways therefore usually\n  enforce both a rate and a max-in-flight per tenant.\n\nA worked trace makes the token bucket concrete. Take *b* = 10 and *r* = 2 per second. An idle client\nfires 15 requests at t = 0: the first 10 drain the bucket and pass, the next 5 are rejected (or wait).\nAt t = 1 s the bucket holds 2 tokens, so 2 more pass. Over any interval of length *T* the bucket admits\nat most `b + r·T` requests, which is the whole contract: burst *b*, long-run rate *r*.\n\nDistributed counters must be atomic: `INCR` followed by `EXPIRE` as two commands leaves a key without\na TTL if the process dies in between, so use a Lua script or `SET NX EX` plus `INCR`. Placement is a\ntrade: a gateway (nginx, Envoy, a cloud API gateway) handles coarse per-IP or per-key limits with no\nbusiness context; application-level limiting in Redis can be per tenant, per model and token-weighted.\nDimensions matter. Model providers limit requests per minute (RPM) and tokens per minute (TPM)\nseparately, often per model and per region, and for LLM workloads TPM binds first *because* each\nrequest carries thousands of tokens. Some providers debit the token budget at admission using your\n`max_tokens` plus an estimate of the prompt rather than the tokens actually generated, so a generous\n`max_tokens` can produce 429s while real usage is low; check your provider's accounting rules.\n\nSignal clearly: respond `429 Too Many Requests` (RFC 6585) with `Retry-After` in seconds or as an HTTP\ndate, and publish remaining budget in headers (`X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`,\n`X-RateLimit-Reset` by convention; an IETF httpapi draft, still evolving, standardises `RateLimit` and\n`RateLimit-Policy` fields, and earlier drafts used `RateLimit-Limit`/`-Remaining`/`-Reset`) so clients\ncan throttle before they hit 429. As a consumer: honour `Retry-After`, back off exponentially with jitter,\ncap concurrency with a semaphore, and keep a client-side token bucket sized to the limit you know you\nhave so you queue locally instead of burning retries.\n\n### Quotas: entitlement and cost\nA quota caps total consumption over a long window (day, month) or a budget (dollars, tokens, jobs) per\naccount, tenant or feature. Rate limit answers \"how fast\"; quota answers \"how much\". Enforce it with a\ncounter per `(tenant, period)` in Redis or the database. For token quotas, reserve an estimate at\nadmission and reconcile from the response's `usage` field *because* the real cost is only known after\nthe call and a streaming call can fail halfway. Distinguish soft limits (alert, degrade to a cheaper\nmodel, lower `max_tokens`, require approval) from hard limits (reject). There is no universal status\ncode for quota exhaustion; what matters is that the error body is machine-distinguishable from a rate\nlimit, since the correct client reaction differs: wait seconds versus stop until the next period or\nupgrade. Finally, you consume a provider's quota and issue quotas to tenants; the sum of allocations\nagainst one provider key must fit the provider's limit, or you overbook with priorities and shed\nlow-priority work when the provider says 429. That mapping is capacity planning, covered in the\ninfra-capacity-economics and reliability modules.","example":"A keyset-paginated endpoint with an opaque cursor, and a client-side token bucket sized to a provider's\ntokens-per-minute limit. The bucket is in-process; with several workers the same logic moves into Redis.\n`db` is an asyncpg pool created at startup.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, base64, json, time\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Query\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef encode_cursor(created_at: datetime, id_: int) -> str:\n    return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(json.dumps([created_at.isoformat(), id_]).encode()).decode()\n\ndef decode_cursor(cursor: str) -> tuple[datetime, int]:\n    try:\n        ts, id_ = json.loads(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(cursor))\n        return datetime.fromisoformat(ts), int(id_)\n    except (ValueError, TypeError):\n        raise HTTPException(400, \"invalid cursor\")             # opaque: clients never build or parse it\n\n@app.get(\"/documents\")\nasync def list_documents(limit: int = Query(20, ge=1, le=100), cursor: str | None = None):\n    sql, args = \"SELECT id, created_at, title FROM documents\", []\n    if cursor:\n        last_ts, last_id = decode_cursor(cursor)\n        sql += \" WHERE (created_at, id) < ($1, $2)\"             # keyset: seek to position, never skip rows\n        args = [last_ts, last_id]\n    sql += f\" ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT {limit + 1}\"  # +1 row answers has_more without COUNT(*)\n    rows = await db.fetch(sql, *args)                           # needs an index on (created_at, id)\n    page, has_more = rows[:limit], len(rows) > limit\n    next_cursor = encode_cursor(page[-1][\"created_at\"], page[-1][\"id\"]) if has_more else None\n    return {\"data\": [dict(r) for r in page], \"next_cursor\": next_cursor, \"has_more\": has_more}\n\nclass TokenBucket:\n    \"\"\"Client-side limiter: queue locally instead of collecting 429s from the provider.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, rate_per_sec: float, capacity: float):\n        self.rate, self.capacity = rate_per_sec, capacity\n        self.tokens, self.last = capacity, time.monotonic()\n    async def acquire(self, n: float) -> None:\n        if n > self.capacity:\n            raise ValueError(\"request larger than bucket capacity would wait forever\")\n        while True:\n            now = time.monotonic()\n            self.tokens = min(self.capacity, self.tokens + (now - self.last) * self.rate); self.last = now\n            if self.tokens >= n:\n                self.tokens -= n; return                        # no await between check and debit: safe in asyncio\n            await asyncio.sleep((n - self.tokens) / self.rate)  # sleep exactly the refill time we need\n\ntpm_bucket = TokenBucket(rate_per_sec=200_000 / 60, capacity=200_000)   # illustrative 200k TPM limit\n# before each model call: await tpm_bucket.acquire(prompt_token_estimate + max_tokens)\n```","tradeoffs":["Keyset and cursor pagination cost the same at page 1 and page 10,000 and stay stable under writes, but they cannot jump to page *N* or sort by an arbitrary unindexed column; offset wins when the set is small, static and the UI genuinely needs page numbers.","A token bucket admits bursts, which keeps latency low for bursty but light clients, yet a burst of size *b* still lands on the downstream at once; when that downstream has no buffer of its own, a leaky bucket's smoothing is worth its queueing delay.","Enforcing at the gateway is uniform and cheap, but the gateway cannot see token counts or tenant tier; application-level limiting can, at the price of a Redis round trip per request and one more component that must stay highly available.","Hard quotas protect the bill and fairness between tenants, but stopping a paying customer dead at 100% creates support load; soft limits with graceful degradation win when the customer's SLA matters more than the marginal cost.","An exact sliding-window log is fair and precise, but its memory grows with traffic; the approximate sliding counter is cheaper and good enough unless the limit itself is contractual."],"pitfalls":["Offset pagination on a growing table; the symptom is page latency that climbs with page number and users reporting duplicated or missing items while the list is being written to.","Sorting on a non-unique column without a tie-breaker; the symptom is rows that vanish or repeat exactly at page boundaries when many rows share a timestamp.","A fixed-window counter guarding a fragile downstream; the symptom is overload incidents clustered in the seconds around minute boundaries, at twice the nominal limit.","Retrying 429 immediately or without jitter; the symptom is a retry storm in which the 429 rate climbs instead of clearing, sometimes ending with the provider disabling the key.","Counting requests when the provider counts tokens; the symptom is 429s at a small fraction of the request limit because each request carries a 40k-token prompt.","Charging quota only after the response arrives; the symptom is tenants exceeding their cap by one burst's worth of concurrent requests, and failed streaming calls that are never accounted for.","Rate-limiting long streaming requests without a concurrency cap; the symptom is the model server or connection pool saturating while every tenant is under its requests-per-minute limit.","Exposing a readable cursor; the symptom is integrations breaking after a release that changed the sort order, because clients had been hand-building cursors."],"when":"Default to cursor pagination for any list that can grow or is ordered by time; keep offset for small\nadmin tables. Rate-limit every endpoint that fronts a scarce resource, per tenant or key, with a token\nbucket in Redis at the application layer and coarse limits at the gateway, and put a client-side bucket\nin front of every provider whose limits you know. Add quotas as soon as a call has a cost, which for LLM\nproducts is day one. Measure the 429 rate per tenant, time spent waiting in client limiters, quota\nutilisation, and page latency by depth. Interviewers ask \"design a rate limiter\" constantly; the\nexpected answer compares token bucket with fixed and sliding windows, handles atomicity in Redis,\nchooses response headers, and separates rate from quota.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements keyset endpoints and opaque cursors, the Redis limiter script, the client-side TPM bucket, and the 429 and `Retry-After` handling inside every SDK wrapper.","architect":"Chooses the limit dimensions (RPM, TPM, concurrency), the tenant quota model and how it overbooks provider capacity, and where enforcement lives (gateway versus service).","fde":"Translates a customer's expected load into provider limits, requests quota increases ahead of launch, and designs the \"you have hit your quota\" experience and dashboards their admins will see."},"check":{"q":"Your model provider allows 500 requests and 200,000 tokens per minute on the model you use. Your\nagent sends prompts of roughly 20,000 tokens. A customer submits 1,000 documents at once. What happens,\nand how do you redesign?","a":"TPM binds: 200,000 / 20,000 is 10 requests per minute, so the 500 RPM figure is irrelevant and\nthe burst produces 429s within seconds. The batch is 20 million tokens, so 100 minutes at the very\nbest. Redesign: queue the batch and drain it through a client-side token bucket sized to TPM (counting\nthe prompt estimate plus `max_tokens`), bound concurrency, honour `Retry-After` with jittered backoff,\ngive each tenant a quota so one customer cannot consume the whole budget, shrink the prompt (retrieval\ninstead of whole documents; prompt caching cuts cost and latency, and on some providers cached tokens\nalso stop counting against the input-token limit), and ask for a quota\nincrease or spread load across regions or providers before launch. If the provider offers an\nasynchronous batch endpoint with separate limits, route offline jobs there. Then watch the 429 rate and\nqueue wait time."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does keyset pagination stay fast at page 10,000 while offset pagination slows down?","options":["Keyset pages are cached in Redis after the first request","The index seek starts at the last-seen key, so each page costs about the page size rather than the offset","Keyset queries return fewer columns, so less data is transferred","The database compresses rows that have already been paginated"],"answer":1,"explanation":"OFFSET forces the engine to produce and discard every earlier row in sort order, so cost grows with depth. A keyset predicate on an indexed column lets the engine jump straight to the position, so the cost is proportional to the page, not the depth."},{"q":"A fixed-window limiter allows 100 requests per minute. What is the most a client can push through in a two-second span that straddles the minute boundary?","options":["100, because the limit is enforced per minute","About 3, because 100 per minute is under two per second","200: the last 100 of one window and the first 100 of the next","Unbounded, because the counter resets to zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The counter is per calendar window, not per trailing minute, so a client can exhaust one window at its end and the next at its start. Sliding-window and token-bucket limiters remove that boundary burst."},{"q":"A tenant's plan allows 60 requests per minute and 100,000 requests per month. They send a steady 30 requests per minute for three days. Which bound stops them?","options":["The rate limit, because 30 per minute sustained for hours exceeds 60 per minute","The monthly quota, which 30 per minute exhausts in a little over two days","Neither, because they never exceed 60 per minute","Both at once, on the third day"],"answer":1,"explanation":"30 per minute is 43,200 per day, so 100,000 is used up after about 2.3 days without ever exceeding the per-minute rate. Rate limits bound speed; quotas bound total consumption over the period."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/webhook-signature-verification-and-replay-protection","sources":["IETF RFC 2104 — HMAC: Keyed-Hashing for Message Authentication","Official docs: Stripe webhooks","Official docs: Standard Webhooks specification","Official docs: Slack API (verifying requests)"],"why":"`POST /webhooks/payments` is reachable from every machine on the internet. If the handler trusts the\nbody, an attacker sends `{\"type\": \"payment.succeeded\", \"customer\": \"them\"}` and gets the product for\nfree. An agent platform that receives Slack events will happily execute instructions from anyone who\ncan craft a request, which is prompt injection with a delivery mechanism. A pipeline waiting for a\nprovider's \"batch job finished\" event will ingest a forged result. The sender's identity has to come\nfrom somewhere, and nothing in plain HTTP provides it: TLS authenticates your server to the sender, not\nthe sender to you.\n\nThe naive defences each fail in a specific way. A secret URL is a bearer token that leaks through access\nlogs, proxies, browser history and a screenshot in a support ticket, and rotating it means touching\nevery integration. An IP allowlist breaks because providers send from large, changing cloud ranges and\nyour own ingress may rewrite source addresses. A `\"source\": \"billing\"` field in the JSON is written by\nwhoever sends the JSON. Mutual TLS would settle the question, but few SaaS providers offer it for\nwebhooks. What\nremains is a shared secret used to compute a message authentication code over the exact bytes\nreceived: cheap, verifiable offline, and proof that whoever produced the message held the secret.\n\nSigning stops forgery but not replay. A captured legitimate delivery is still correctly signed next\nweek, so \"credit 100 units\" can be resent until the attacker is satisfied. And the provider itself\nredelivers on any non-2xx response or timeout, so duplicates are normal operation, not an edge case.\nA timestamp inside the signed content bounds the window, an event id bounds duplicates inside it, and\nan idempotent handler makes whatever slips through harmless.","how":"### What the provider signs\nEach endpoint gets its own secret: Stripe calls it a signing secret, GitHub a webhook secret you type\ninto the settings page. On delivery the provider computes `HMAC-SHA256(secret, signed_content)` and\nsends the result in a header. Schemes differ in what `signed_content` is. Stripe signs\n`\"{timestamp}.{raw_body}\"` and sends `Stripe-Signature: t=<unix>,v1=<hex>`. The Standard Webhooks\nconvention (used by Svix and adopted by several vendors) signs `\"{msg_id}.{timestamp}.{raw_body}\"` and\nsends `webhook-id`, `webhook-timestamp` and `webhook-signature` headers. GitHub signs the body alone in\n`X-Hub-Signature-256: sha256=<hex>` with a separate `X-GitHub-Delivery` id and no timestamp. Slack signs\n`\"v0:{timestamp}:{raw_body}\"` into `X-Slack-Signature`. The common core is an HMAC with a per-endpoint\nsecret over raw bytes. It is an HMAC rather than a bare hash *because* a hash of public bytes can be\ncomputed by anyone; it is in a header rather than the body *because* the body is the thing being\nauthenticated. A few providers use asymmetric signatures with a published public key instead, which lets\nmany parties verify without any of them being able to forge.\n\n### Verifying, in order\n1. Read the raw bytes of the body before any framework parses it. Re-serialising parsed JSON changes\n   whitespace, key order, float formatting and unicode escaping, so the MAC will not match. Cap the\n   body size at the edge first, since an unauthenticated sender can otherwise make you buffer and\n   hash arbitrarily large payloads.\n2. Parse the header into a timestamp and one or more signatures; reject anything malformed.\n3. Check `|now − timestamp| ≤ tolerance`. The tolerance is a few minutes (Stripe's libraries default to\n   300 seconds), not zero, *because* clocks drift and deliveries queue.\n4. Recompute the MAC over the provider's exact signed content and compare with `hmac.compare_digest`,\n   never `==`. An ordinary comparison returns at the first differing byte, so response time leaks how\n   many leading bytes of a guess were right; a constant-time compare removes that side channel. Accept\n   the delivery if *any* supplied signature matches, because during secret rotation providers send one\n   signature per active secret.\n5. Only now parse the JSON and dispatch on the event type.\n\n### Replay protection\nThe timestamp only helps *because* it is inside the signed content: an attacker who edits it breaks\nthe MAC. A timestamp sent in an unsigned header, or no timestamp at all (GitHub's scheme), gives no\nreplay bound, so for those senders deduplication by delivery id is the only replay defence and its\nretention has to be long. Where the timestamp is signed, the window turns \"valid forever\" into\n\"valid for five minutes\", which is still plenty for a double credit. So deduplicate by event id:\n`SET webhook:seen:{id} 1 NX EX <ttl>` in Redis, or an insert into a\n`processed_events(event_id UNIQUE)` table. Retention must cover the provider's retry horizon (often days), not just the tolerance window, *because* legitimate retries arrive later with the same\nid. Check whether your provider re-signs retries with a fresh timestamp; if it does not, the tolerance\nhas to cover its retry schedule as well. The robust version puts the uniqueness insert in the same\ndatabase transaction as the side effect, so either both commit or neither, and a crash between \"marked\nseen\" and \"credited\" cannot lose an event. A Redis flag set before the work is cheaper but can mark an\nevent seen that then fails; that is the exactly-once limitation, and the cure is an idempotent handler\nplus periodic reconciliation against the provider's API.\n\n### Acknowledge fast, process later, trust the API over the payload\nProviders time out deliveries after seconds to tens of seconds, retry on any non-2xx with backoff over\nhours or days, and may disable an endpoint that keeps failing. So: verify, dedupe, enqueue the raw\nevent, return 200, and let a worker do the real work. Deliveries are not ordered (a `completed` can\narrive before `started`) and payload contents can be stale, so for anything that moves money or grants\naccess, fetch the current object from the provider's authenticated API by id and act on that. \"Thin\"\nwebhooks that carry only ids make this pattern the default.\n\n### Secrets\nThe signing secret lives in your secret manager, per endpoint, never in code. It is symmetric: anyone\nholding it can forge deliveries, so do not share one secret across services or environments, and rotate\nwith an overlap window during which both the old and the new secret are accepted.","example":"A FastAPI receiver for a Stripe-style scheme: raw-body HMAC, timestamp tolerance, multiple signatures\nfor rotation, constant-time compare, Redis dedupe keyed by event id with retention longer than the\ntolerance, and a hand-off to a queue so the provider gets its 200 in milliseconds. Test it with three\nfixtures: a recorded good delivery, the same body with one byte changed, and the same delivery twice.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, hmac, json, os, time\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Request\nimport redis.asyncio as redis\n\napp = FastAPI()\nSECRET = os.environ[\"WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET\"].encode()   # per-endpoint secret from the secret manager\nTOLERANCE_S = 300                                        # clock skew + delivery delay we accept\nSEEN_TTL_S = 3 * 24 * 3600                               # must cover the provider's retry horizon\nr = redis.from_url(os.environ[\"REDIS_URL\"])\n\ndef verify(raw: bytes, header: str) -> None:\n    # Stripe-style header: \"t=<unix>,v1=<hex>[,v1=<hex>]\"; two v1 values appear during secret rotation\n    fields = [p.split(\"=\", 1) for p in header.split(\",\") if \"=\" in p]\n    ts = next((v for k, v in fields if k == \"t\"), None)\n    sigs = [v for k, v in fields if k == \"v1\"]\n    if ts is None or not ts.isdigit() or not sigs:\n        raise HTTPException(400, \"malformed signature header\")\n    if abs(time.time() - int(ts)) > TOLERANCE_S:\n        raise HTTPException(400, \"timestamp outside tolerance\")        # bounds replay to a short window\n    expected = hmac.new(SECRET, f\"{ts}.\".encode() + raw, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n    # constant-time compare on bytes: compare_digest raises TypeError on non-ASCII str input\n    if not any(hmac.compare_digest(expected.encode(), s.encode()) for s in sigs):\n        raise HTTPException(400, \"bad signature\")\n\n@app.post(\"/webhooks/provider\")\nasync def receive(request: Request):\n    raw = await request.body()                     # the exact bytes that were signed, never re-serialised JSON\n    verify(raw, request.headers.get(\"Provider-Signature\", \"\"))\n    event_id = json.loads(raw).get(\"id\")\n    if not event_id:\n        raise HTTPException(400, \"missing event id\")\n    key = f\"webhook:seen:{event_id}\"\n    if not await r.set(key, 1, nx=True, ex=SEEN_TTL_S):\n        return {\"status\": \"duplicate\"}             # 2xx: a retry or a replay, either way do nothing again\n    try:\n        await r.lpush(\"webhook:inbox\", raw)        # ack fast; a worker fetches the object by id and acts\n    except Exception:\n        await r.delete(key)                        # un-mark so the provider's retry is not dropped\n        raise\n    return {\"status\": \"queued\"}\n```","tradeoffs":["An HMAC with a shared secret is simple, fast and verifiable offline, but it is symmetric: every holder can forge, so asymmetric signatures become the better choice when many parties must verify or the verifier must never be able to sign.","A tight timestamp tolerance shrinks the replay window, but clock skew and queued deliveries then cause false rejections and more retries; widen it when the provider does not re-sign retries and lean on id deduplication instead.","Dedupe in Redis with a TTL is one cheap command, but it is not transactional with your side effect; a unique constraint inside the same database transaction costs a write per event and buys a real effectively-once guarantee.","Acknowledging immediately and processing in a worker frees you from provider timeouts, but a 200 no longer means \"done\", so monitoring, dead-lettering and reconciliation move to the queue.","Fetching the authoritative object from the provider on every event defeats stale and out-of-order payloads, but it adds an API call per event and is subject to the provider's rate limits."],"pitfalls":["Verifying over parsed-then-re-serialised JSON; the symptom is signatures that fail only for some payloads (non-ASCII text, floats, nested key order) while every local test passes.","Comparing signatures with `==`; there is no functional symptom, which is exactly the danger, and it surfaces only in a pentest or security review.","Checking the signature but neither the timestamp nor the event id; the symptom is double side effects (two credits, two emails) whenever the provider retries, and silent replayability.","A gateway, body-parsing middleware or compression layer rewriting the body before your handler; the symptom is every signature failing after a deployment that changed nothing in your code.","Doing the work inline and returning 200 only at the end; the symptom is provider-side timeouts, escalating retries and duplicate processing under load, sometimes ending with the endpoint disabled.","Assuming ordered delivery; the symptom is a record stuck in `started` because a late `started` event overwrote `completed`.","Rotating the secret without an overlap; the symptom is a burst of 400s and missed events during the switch.","Logging full headers and bodies; the symptom is signatures, tokens and customer data sitting in the log platform for anyone with search access."],"when":"Every inbound webhook, without exception, including events from your own services whenever they cross\na trust boundary; the cost is a few lines and the failure mode is an attacker driving your business\nlogic. If a sender cannot sign, require at least a secret header and prefer polling the provider's API\nfor state over trusting pushes. Measure the verification-failure rate (anything above zero is an attack\nor a misconfiguration), the duplicate rate, the distribution of timestamp age, and processing lag. In\ninterviews, \"how would you secure a webhook endpoint\" is a standard question; a strong answer walks\nraw-body HMAC, constant-time compare, timestamp window, id dedupe, idempotent handler, ack-then-queue\nand fetching the authoritative object, and can say why each exists.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the verification middleware on raw bytes, the dedupe and queue hand-off, and the three fixtures (good, tampered, duplicated) that keep it honest in CI.","architect":"Defines the trust boundary for inbound events: which may trigger side effects, where the idempotency store lives, the retry horizon, and who owns and rotates each secret.","fde":"Wires customer systems (Slack, GitHub, Salesforce, an in-house ERP) to the agent, checks each one's signing scheme, and agrees how secrets are exchanged and rotated inside the customer's security process."},"check":{"q":"A colleague says the billing webhook is already secure: it lives at a long random URL, the\nhandler checks that the JSON contains `\"source\": \"billing\"`, and the site is HTTPS-only. Explain what\neach measure misses and what you would implement instead.","a":"The random URL is a bearer secret that leaks through access logs, proxies and screenshots and\ncannot be rotated without touching every integration. The `source` field is part of the body the\nattacker writes. HTTPS encrypts in transit and proves your identity to the sender; it says nothing\nabout who sent the request. Instead: verify an HMAC-SHA256 over the timestamp and the raw body using a\nper-endpoint secret from the secret manager, compare in constant time, reject timestamps older than a\nfew minutes, deduplicate on event id with retention covering the provider's retry schedule, make the\nhandler idempotent, return 200 quickly and process from a queue, and for anything that grants value,\nfetch the current object from the billing API by id before acting."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must the HMAC be computed over the raw request bytes rather than over the parsed JSON object?","options":["Parsing JSON is too slow to do before authentication","Serialisation is not canonical, so re-encoded JSON can differ byte for byte from what the provider signed","Raw bytes include the headers, which are part of the signature","JSON objects cannot be hashed because they are unordered"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The provider signed a specific byte sequence. Whitespace, key order, escaping and number formatting can all change on a parse-and-dump round trip, producing a different MAC even though the data is \"the same\"."},{"q":"A delivery arrives with a valid signature and a timestamp two minutes old, but its event id is already in your seen-set. What should the handler do?","options":["Return 400 so the provider stops sending it","Process it again, because the signature proves it is genuine","Return 2xx without running the side effect again","Return 500 so the provider retries later with a fresh timestamp"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Whether it is a provider retry or an attacker's replay, the right action is the same: acknowledge and do nothing. A non-2xx would only trigger more retries of an event you have already handled."},{"q":"What does a constant-time comparison such as `hmac.compare_digest` protect against that `==` does not?","options":["SHA-256 hash collisions","Length-extension attacks on the hash","A timing side channel where early exit reveals how many leading bytes of the guess were correct","Replay of a previously valid signature"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ordinary string comparison stops at the first mismatch, so response time correlates with the number of correct leading bytes. Length-extension is already prevented by HMAC's construction, and replay is handled by timestamps and ids, not by the comparison."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/database-design","sources":["Codd, 1970 — A Relational Model of Data for Large Shared Data Banks","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: PostgreSQL"],"why":"Code is rewritten every year; the data it produced is still there a decade later. If the storage layout is\nwhatever the first endpoint happened to need, three things break quickly. First, the same fact ends up\nstored in several rows (a user's email copied into every order), and the copies disagree after the first\nupdate that misses one. That is an *update anomaly*, and no amount of application discipline fixes it,\nbecause the next engineer does not know the copies exist. Second, invariants that live only in code\n(\"a message always belongs to exactly one conversation\") get violated by the one script, migration or\nmanual fix that bypasses the code, leaving orphans that crash reads months later. Third, queries that were\nfast at ten thousand rows become table scans at ten million, and by then the data layout is load-bearing\nfor a dozen services.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"store JSON blobs and figure it out later\", postpones all three problems to the\nmoment the data is largest and the original authors are gone. Database design is the practice of deciding\nup front which facts exist, which entity owns each one, which rules the database itself must enforce, and\nwhich access paths need to be cheap. For AI systems this matters more, not less: conversation history,\ntool-call logs, embeddings and evaluation results are the product's memory, and they are queried by\nhumans, dashboards and retrieval code long after the request that wrote them.","how":"### Entities, keys and relationships\nStart by naming the nouns the business reasons about (tenant, user, conversation, message, tool call, run,\ndocument, chunk) and the relationships between them with their cardinality: a conversation has many\nmessages, a message has zero or more tool calls, a document has many chunks. Each entity gets a table\nand a primary key. A **surrogate key** (bigint identity or UUID) is the default *because* natural keys\n(email, external IDs) change, get reused, or turn out not to be unique. If you use UUIDs, prefer a\ntime-ordered variant (UUIDv7) over random UUIDv4 for primary keys, because a B-tree index on random keys\ninserts into random pages, which defeats cache locality and bloats the index as the table grows.\nRelationships become foreign keys, and the database enforces them so an orphan cannot exist even when\na script bypasses the application.\n\n### Normalization: one fact, one place\nNormal forms are rules for removing redundancy, and they follow from one idea: a table should describe\none kind of thing, and every non-key column should depend on the whole key and nothing else. In practice:\n\n1. **1NF:** no repeating groups or lists packed into a column; `tags = \"a,b,c\"` becomes a `tags` table or\n   an array column with proper indexing.\n2. **2NF:** with a composite key, no column may depend on only part of it; in a\n   `(conversation_id, user_id)` membership table, `user_name` depends on `user_id` alone and belongs in\n   `users`.\n3. **3NF:** no non-key column may depend on another non-key column. If `messages` stores `user_id` and\n   `user_email`, the email depends on `user_id` rather than on the message, so it belongs in `users`.\n   Otherwise changing an email means rewriting every message row.\n\nNormalization is the default *because* writes are where consistency is decided; a normalized schema makes\nthe correct write the only possible write. The cost is joins on read, which is where denormalization comes\nin: a `conversations.last_message_at` column duplicated from `messages` makes the inbox query cheap, but\nnow it must be maintained in the same transaction as the insert. Denormalize when a specific query is\nmeasured to be slow, and write down the invariant you are now responsible for.\n\n### Constraints are invariants the database guarantees\n`NOT NULL`, `UNIQUE`, `CHECK (tokens_used >= 0)`, foreign keys with explicit `ON DELETE` behaviour, and\nenum-like `CHECK (role IN ('user','assistant','tool'))` are the cheapest tests you will ever write: they\nrun on every write path, including the ones you forgot. A partial unique index (`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON\ndocuments (tenant_id, external_id) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`; a plain `UNIQUE` constraint cannot take a\n`WHERE`) encodes \"unique among live rows\", which code alone gets wrong under concurrent inserts.\n\n### Types carry meaning\nUse `timestamptz`, never bare `timestamp`. A `timestamptz` is stored as an absolute instant (UTC\ninternally); a bare `timestamp` stores only a wall-clock reading, so which instant it means depends on\nwhatever zone the writer happened to assume, and rows written from servers in different zones cannot be\ncompared. Use `numeric` for money and token prices, never `float`, because binary floats cannot\nrepresent 0.1 exactly and the rounding errors accumulate in invoices. Use `jsonb` for genuinely\nsemi-structured payloads (raw model responses, tool arguments), and pull out any field you filter or join\non into a real column with a real index. Store a `schema_version` next to any jsonb you will parse later.\n\n### Multi-tenancy and deletion from day one\nEvery tenant-owned row carries `tenant_id`, every index that serves a tenant-scoped query leads with it,\nand every query filters on it; PostgreSQL row-level security can enforce the filter when you do not trust\nevery code path. A plain foreign key on `conversation_id` still lets a message carry tenant A's id while\npointing at tenant B's conversation, because nothing compares the two columns. Make the parent's\n`(tenant_id, id)` unique and have the child reference that pair, so the database itself refuses a\ncross-tenant link. Decide how deletion works before the first user asks for it: a `deleted_at` soft-delete\ncolumn keeps audit history but every query must exclude it; hard deletes with `ON DELETE CASCADE` are\nsimpler but irreversible. Retention and right-to-erasure requests are much easier when data is owned by\none clear parent row.\n\n### Indexes follow queries\nAn index is a sorted copy of some columns that makes one access path cheap and every write slightly more\nexpensive. The design question is never \"which columns are important\" but \"which `WHERE`/`ORDER BY`\npatterns run often\". A composite index on `(conversation_id, created_at)` serves \"messages of this\nconversation in order\" in one range scan; the same two columns in the other order do not. Equality\ncolumns go first, the range or sort column last, *because* the B-tree is sorted by the first column, then\nthe second within ties: equality on the leading column narrows to one contiguous slice, inside which the\nnext column is already in order. Put the range column first and the matching entries for one conversation\nare scattered across the whole range. Foreign-key columns usually need an index because joins\nand cascading deletes look rows up by them, and PostgreSQL does not create one automatically on the\nreferencing side.\n\n### Evolving the schema\nThe schema will change while the old code is still running, so every change is designed to be compatible\nwith both versions for the length of a deploy. That is the **expand/contract** rule: add the new column or\ntable, write to both, backfill in batches, move reads, and only then drop the old one. Renaming a column\nin one step breaks every instance still running the previous release. Some operations are cheap and some\nrewrite or lock the table: since PostgreSQL 11, adding a column with a constant default does not rewrite\nexisting rows, while changing a column's type generally does; `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` builds an index\nwithout blocking writes at the cost of a slower build that can fail and leave an invalid index to drop.\nReview every migration for which locks it takes on a large, hot table.","example":"A minimal schema for an LLM application. Note the tenant column on every row (and the composite foreign\nkey that keeps a message in its conversation's tenant), the constraints that encode\nrules, the jsonb for raw payloads next to the extracted columns (`model`, token counts) that queries\nactually filter and aggregate on, the composite index built for the one hot query (load a conversation's\nmessages in order), and the plain index on the foreign key that cascading deletes need.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE conversations (\n  id           uuid PRIMARY KEY,                 -- UUIDv7 generated by the app\n  tenant_id    uuid NOT NULL,\n  user_id      uuid NOT NULL,\n  title        text,\n  created_at   timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n  deleted_at   timestamptz,\n  UNIQUE (tenant_id, id)                         -- target for tenant-consistent FKs\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE messages (\n  id              bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  tenant_id       uuid NOT NULL,\n  conversation_id uuid NOT NULL,\n  FOREIGN KEY (tenant_id, conversation_id)       -- a message cannot point into another tenant\n    REFERENCES conversations (tenant_id, id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  role            text NOT NULL CHECK (role IN ('system','user','assistant','tool')),\n  content         text NOT NULL,\n  model           text,\n  input_tokens    integer CHECK (input_tokens >= 0),\n  output_tokens   integer CHECK (output_tokens >= 0),\n  raw_response    jsonb,                     -- provider payload, never filtered on directly\n  schema_version  smallint NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n  created_at      timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\n-- the hot path: \"messages of one conversation, oldest first\"\nCREATE INDEX messages_by_conversation ON messages (conversation_id, created_at);\n-- the tenant-scoped dashboard query: spend per tenant per day\nCREATE INDEX messages_by_tenant_time ON messages (tenant_id, created_at);\n\nCREATE TABLE tool_calls (\n  id            bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  tenant_id     uuid NOT NULL,                -- child tables carry the tenant too\n  message_id    bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES messages(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  tool_name     text NOT NULL,\n  arguments     jsonb NOT NULL,\n  status        text NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('pending','succeeded','failed')),\n  idempotency_key text,\n  UNIQUE (tenant_id, idempotency_key)        -- a retried call cannot be recorded twice\n);\n-- FK lookup for joins and for ON DELETE CASCADE from messages\nCREATE INDEX tool_calls_by_message ON tool_calls (message_id);\n```","tradeoffs":["Normalization guarantees one fact in one place at the cost of joins; it flips toward denormalization only when a specific read path is measured to be slow and its duplicate can be maintained transactionally.","Surrogate keys are stable and cheap to join, but you must still add a unique constraint on the natural identifier or duplicates will silently appear.","UUID keys can be generated client-side and merged across systems, but random UUIDs cost index locality and 16 bytes per reference versus 8 for bigint; time-ordered UUIDs recover most of the locality.","`jsonb` absorbs schema changes without migrations, but every field you later filter on without a column and index becomes a sequential scan, and nothing enforces its shape.","Every index speeds one read pattern and taxes every insert and update; on write-heavy tables such as event logs, each extra index is a measurable throughput cost.","Foreign keys and `CHECK`s cost a lookup per write but catch bugs at the only point that sees every path; dropping them \"for speed\" trades a few microseconds for data you can no longer trust."],"pitfalls":["Omitting `tenant_id` from child tables and relying on joins to the parent for scoping; the symptom is a query that returns another tenant's rows the first time someone forgets the join.","Using `float` for money or token prices; the symptom is invoices that are off by a cent and tests that compare with tolerances.","Bare `timestamp` columns; the symptom is rows written before and after a server or container time-zone change that are hours apart in meaning but adjacent in value, and \"yesterday\" reports that disagree.","Indexing columns rather than queries; the symptom is ten single-column indexes, slow writes, and the one hot query still doing a sort because no composite index matches its `WHERE` and `ORDER BY`.","Entity-attribute-value tables (\"one table of key/value pairs for everything\") to avoid migrations; the symptom is five-way self-joins to reconstruct one object and no constraints on anything.","Storing multi-megabyte payloads (full prompts, document text) in the hottest table; the symptom is bloated tables, slow vacuums and a cache that no longer holds the working set.","Shipping a one-step rename or type change on a large table; the symptom is errors from instances still on the previous release, or a migration that holds a lock for minutes while every write queues behind it.","Designing deletion last; the symptom is a right-to-erasure request that takes a week because data is scattered across tables with no ownership chain."],"when":"Reach for deliberate design whenever data will be read by anything other than the code that wrote it:\ndashboards, evaluations, audits, other services, or future you. For a throwaway prototype, a single jsonb\ncolumn is fine; the moment a second query pattern appears, extract real columns. Measure before\ndenormalizing: look at `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on the slow query and at the write rate of the table you would\nduplicate into. In day-to-day work this shows up as migration reviews (\"why is there no index for the\nquery this endpoint runs?\", \"what happens to messages when a conversation is deleted?\"). In interviews,\nexpect to be handed a product description and asked to draw tables, keys and indexes, then defend each\nconstraint and explain which query each index serves.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the migration, picks types and constraints, and adds the composite index that matches the query the new endpoint runs, verified with `EXPLAIN`.","architect":"Owns the ownership model: which service owns which tables, how tenancy is enforced, the retention and deletion policy, and when a read model may be denormalized.","fde":"Maps the customer's existing schema and identifiers onto the product's model, finds the missing tenant or ownership keys, and plans how their history will be imported and later erased."},"check":{"q":"A chat product stores each conversation as one jsonb document containing all its messages. Reads\nare fast today. What breaks as it grows, and how would you restructure it?","a":"Appending a message rewrites the whole document, so write cost grows with conversation length and\nlong conversations create bloat and lock contention. You cannot index or constrain individual messages,\nso \"all messages by this tool across tenants\" or \"token spend per day\" become full scans that parse JSON.\nDeleting one message or one user's data means rewriting documents. The fix is a `messages` table with\n`tenant_id`, `conversation_id`, `role`, token counts and `created_at`, a composite index on\n`(conversation_id, created_at)` for the conversation view, and a second on `(tenant_id, created_at)` for\nreporting; keep the raw provider response in a jsonb column on the message row, where it is not filtered\non. If the inbox view needs `last_message_at`, denormalize that single column and maintain it in the same\ntransaction as the insert."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a composite index on (conversation_id, created_at) better than two single-column indexes for loading a conversation's messages in order?","options":["Composite indexes are stored in memory while single-column indexes are on disk","One range scan over the composite index returns the rows already in the right order, while separate indexes require combining results and then sorting","Single-column indexes cannot be used together in the same query","The composite index takes less space than two single-column indexes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The planner can walk the composite index from the first matching conversation_id in created_at order, so no sort step is needed. With single-column indexes the planner must either fetch the conversation's rows and sort them, or combine indexes in a bitmap scan, which also loses order and needs a separate sort."},{"q":"A team drops foreign keys \"to make inserts faster\". Which failure is this most likely to cause?","options":["Queries become slower because the planner can no longer use joins","The database refuses to create indexes on the former foreign-key columns","Orphan rows appear whenever any script or bug deletes a parent without its children, and reads fail later","Transactions can no longer be rolled back on those tables"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A foreign key is checked on every write path, including the ones the application code does not cover. Without it, orphans accumulate silently and surface as missing-join bugs or crashes much later."},{"q":"When is denormalizing a column such as conversations.last_message_at the right call?","options":["Whenever a join would otherwise be required, because joins are always expensive","Never, because it violates third normal form","When a specific frequent query is measured to be slow and the duplicate can be updated in the same transaction as the source row","Only when the database does not support indexes on the child table"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Denormalization buys read speed by taking on a consistency obligation. It is justified by measurement and made safe by maintaining the copy transactionally; doing it by default reintroduces update anomalies."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/sql","sources":["Codd, 1970 — A Relational Model of Data for Large Shared Data Banks","Official docs: PostgreSQL","Winand — Use The Index, Luke"],"why":"Before the relational model, databases were navigational: a program walked pointers from record to record,\nso every program encoded the physical layout, and changing the layout meant changing every program. Codd's\ninsight was to describe data as relations (tables of tuples) and queries as algebra over them, so the\n*what* could be stated independently of the *how*. SQL is the practical language that came out of that,\nand the planner is the component that turns the what into a how at run time, using statistics about the\nactual data.\n\nThe naive alternative in application code is the loop: fetch the conversations, then for each one fetch\nits messages, then for each message count tokens. That is the N+1 pattern, and it fails for two reasons.\nEach round trip to the database costs a network hop (hundreds of microseconds to milliseconds), so 1,000\nconversations cost 1,001 queries and somewhere between half a second and several seconds of wall time. And the database never sees the whole\nquestion, so it cannot pick a hash join or use an index to do the work in one pass. A single query with a\njoin and an aggregate lets the engine do it in one scan.\n\nAI backends are heavy SQL users even when the model is the star: per-tenant token spend, latency\npercentiles per model, the last N messages of a conversation, eval results joined to prompt versions, and\nhybrid retrieval that mixes a vector similarity with a metadata filter are all one query each when\nwritten well and a slow fan-out of calls when written badly.","how":"### Logical evaluation order\nA `SELECT` statement is written in one order and evaluated in another. The logical order is:\n`FROM` and `JOIN` (build the row set), `WHERE` (filter rows), `GROUP BY` (collapse into groups),\n`HAVING` (filter groups), `SELECT` (compute output columns, including window functions), `DISTINCT`,\n`ORDER BY`, then `LIMIT`/`OFFSET`. This explains the classic confusions: you cannot reference a `SELECT`\nalias in `WHERE` because `WHERE` runs first; `HAVING` exists because aggregates do not exist yet when\n`WHERE` runs; window functions see the filtered, grouped rows because they run during `SELECT`.\n\n### Joins are set operations, not loops\nAn inner join keeps rows that match on both sides; a left join keeps every left row and fills the right\nside with NULLs when nothing matches, which is how you find conversations with no messages\n(`WHERE m.id IS NULL`). `EXISTS`/`NOT EXISTS` express \"has at least one\" without duplicating rows the way\na join would. The engine picks the physical algorithm: a **nested loop** when one side is tiny or has an\nindex on the join key, a **hash join** when both sides are large and unsorted, a **merge join** when both\nsides are already sorted on the key. You do not pick these; you make them possible with indexes and\naccurate statistics.\n\n### Aggregates and window functions\n`GROUP BY` collapses rows; a window function (`SUM(...) OVER (PARTITION BY tenant_id ORDER BY day)`)\ncomputes an aggregate *per row* without collapsing, which is how you get running totals, rankings and\n\"latest row per group\" in a single pass. PostgreSQL's `DISTINCT ON (conversation_id) ... ORDER BY\nconversation_id, created_at DESC` is the idiomatic \"newest message per conversation\".\n\n### How the planner decides\n`EXPLAIN ANALYZE` shows the tree the planner chose and the real row counts and timings. The planner\nestimates cost from table statistics (row counts, value distributions gathered by `ANALYZE`) and chooses\nbetween a sequential scan (read everything; cheapest when most rows qualify), an index scan (walk a B-tree\nto the matching rows; cheapest when few qualify) and a bitmap scan (collect matching pages from one or\nmore indexes, then read them in physical order). A predicate is **sargable** (can use an index) when the\nindexed column appears bare on one side of a comparison: `created_at >= $1` can use an index;\n`date(created_at) = $1` cannot, because the index stores `created_at`, not its date. `LIKE 'abc%'` can use\na B-tree, but in PostgreSQL only if the index uses the `C` collation or the `text_pattern_ops` operator\nclass (a default index under a locale such as `en_US.UTF-8` is not used for `LIKE`); `LIKE '%abc%'` cannot\nuse a B-tree at all and needs a trigram (`pg_trgm`) index. When an index holds every column the query\nreads (for example via `INCLUDE (input_tokens)`), PostgreSQL can answer from the index alone with an\n**index-only scan**, skipping the table, but only for pages the visibility map marks all-visible, which is\none more reason vacuum health shows up in query latency. The planner is only as good as its statistics, so a\ntable that grew 100× since the last `ANALYZE` (autovacuum usually re-analyzes, but can lag after a bulk\nload) gets bad plans.\n\n### NULL is not a value\nSQL uses three-valued logic: a comparison with NULL is neither true nor false but *unknown*, and `WHERE`\nkeeps only rows where the predicate is true. So `WHERE model <> 'gpt-x'` silently drops every row whose\n`model` is NULL, `x = NULL` never matches (use `IS NULL` or `IS DISTINCT FROM`), and\n`NOT IN (subquery)` returns nothing at all once the subquery yields a single NULL, because\n`a NOT IN (b, NULL)` expands to `a <> b AND a <> NULL`, which can never be true. Arithmetic propagates it:\n`input_tokens + output_tokens` is NULL if either side is, and `SUM` skips NULLs, so a row with a missing\ncount vanishes from a spend total rather than raising an error. `COUNT(*)` counts rows; `COUNT(col)` counts\nnon-NULL values; the difference is a common source of reports that disagree.\n\n### Parameters, not string formatting\nQueries take parameters (`$1`, `%s`, `?` depending on the driver) that are sent separately from the SQL\ntext. This is the only reliable defence against SQL injection *because* the database never parses user\ninput as SQL; it also lets the server reuse a prepared plan. String interpolation of user input into a\nquery is a vulnerability regardless of how well you escape.\n\n### Pagination and the OFFSET trap\n`LIMIT 20 OFFSET 100000` still reads and discards 100,000 rows; cost grows linearly with page depth.\nKeyset pagination (`WHERE (created_at, id) < ($last_ts, $last_id) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC\nLIMIT 20`) seeks directly to the next page using the index and costs the same on page 1 and page 5,000.\n\n### Where the ORM fits\nORMs generate SQL from objects, which is convenient for CRUD and dangerous for reporting: lazy-loaded\nrelationships are the usual source of N+1, and complex aggregations are often clearer as SQL. The\nworking rule is to let the ORM handle simple writes and reads by key, and to write (and `EXPLAIN`) the\nhot or analytical queries by hand.","example":"Two queries that an LLM backend runs constantly. The first is a per-tenant spend report with a running\ntotal, done in one pass with a window function over the grouped rows. The second is \"newest message per\nconversation\" for an inbox view using a `LATERAL` subquery (the `DISTINCT ON` form above is the\nalternative), with a keyset cursor so page depth does not change the cost. Each page still evaluates the\nlateral lookup for every conversation the user owns; when users have thousands of conversations, the\nusual next step is a denormalized `conversations.last_message_at` with an index on `(tenant_id, user_id,\nlast_message_at, id)`, so the cursor seeks straight to the page.\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Daily token spend per tenant for the last 7 days, with a running total.\n--    No tenant filter, so a (tenant_id, created_at) index gives no simple range scan;\n--    an index on created_at (or a BRIN index on an append-only table) serves this.\n--    Day boundaries follow the session TimeZone setting.\nSELECT tenant_id,\n       date_trunc('day', created_at) AS day,\n       SUM(COALESCE(input_tokens, 0) + COALESCE(output_tokens, 0))        AS tokens,\n       SUM(SUM(COALESCE(input_tokens, 0) + COALESCE(output_tokens, 0)))   -- window over groups\n         OVER (PARTITION BY tenant_id ORDER BY date_trunc('day', created_at)) AS running_tokens\nFROM   messages\nWHERE  created_at >= now() - interval '7 days'      -- sargable: bare column vs constant\n  AND  role = 'assistant'\nGROUP  BY tenant_id, date_trunc('day', created_at)\nORDER  BY tenant_id, day;\n\n-- 2. Inbox: the newest message of each conversation for one user, 20 at a time.\n--    Keyset pagination: pass the last row's (created_at, id) back instead of an OFFSET.\nSELECT c.id, c.title, m.content, m.created_at\nFROM   conversations c\nJOIN   LATERAL (\n         SELECT content, created_at, id\n         FROM   messages\n         WHERE  conversation_id = c.id\n         ORDER  BY created_at DESC, id DESC           -- id breaks timestamp ties\n         LIMIT  1                                     -- hits (conversation_id, created_at)\n       ) m ON true\nWHERE  c.tenant_id = $1 AND c.user_id = $2 AND c.deleted_at IS NULL\n  AND  (m.created_at, m.id) < ($3, $4)                -- previous page's last row; first page: ('infinity', 0)\nORDER  BY m.created_at DESC, m.id DESC\nLIMIT  20;\n```\n\nRun each with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and check two things: that the scan on `messages` is an index\nscan rather than a sequential scan, and that the estimated row count is within an order of magnitude of\nthe actual one. A large mismatch means stale statistics or a predicate the planner cannot estimate.\nThe `id` tie-breaker in both the `ORDER BY` and the cursor matters: two messages with the same\n`created_at` would otherwise have no defined order, and a page boundary falling between them could skip\nor repeat a row. The `COALESCE` calls stop a single row with a NULL count from dropping out of the total.","tradeoffs":["Declarative queries let the planner adapt to data growth, but you give up control of the algorithm; when the planner guesses wrong you fix statistics or indexes, not the query's \"steps\".","One big joined query minimizes round trips but can produce a huge intermediate result; when a join multiplies rows (one conversation × many messages × many tool calls), two targeted queries are cheaper.","Indexes make predicates cheap but each one slows writes and consumes memory; a reporting query that runs hourly rarely justifies an index that every insert pays for.","ORMs speed up CRUD and keep types in sync, but they hide the SQL; the moment a query touches more than two tables or an aggregate, hand-written SQL is usually both faster and clearer.","Keyset pagination is constant-cost but cannot jump to \"page 37\"; OFFSET can, at linear cost, so pick by whether users actually jump or just scroll.","Common table expressions make queries readable; since PostgreSQL 12 the planner can inline a CTE that is referenced once, so readability no longer forces a materialization cost, but `MATERIALIZED` is there when you need the optimisation fence."],"pitfalls":["The N+1 loop hidden behind an ORM relationship; the symptom is a request that issues hundreds of identical small queries in the log, each fast, together slow.","Wrapping an indexed column in a function (`lower(email) = $1`, `date(created_at) = $1`); the symptom is a sequential scan in `EXPLAIN` despite an index existing. Rewrite as a range or add an expression index; the expression must be immutable, so on a `timestamptz` column index `((created_at AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')::date)`, not `date(created_at)`, which depends on the session time zone.","`NOT IN (subquery)` where the subquery can return NULL; the symptom is a query that silently returns zero rows. Use `NOT EXISTS`.","`SELECT *` in application code; the symptom is a column added for a migration that doubles network payload and breaks positional unpacking in a client.","Deep `OFFSET` pagination; the symptom is page one in milliseconds and page two hundred in seconds, with the database CPU climbing as users scroll.","Building SQL with f-strings or template literals; the symptom is a security finding, or a query that breaks on the first title containing an apostrophe.","Trusting plans from a small dev database; the symptom is a query that was instant in staging and does a sequential scan over 50 million rows in production because the planner's choice flips with size."],"when":"SQL is the right tool whenever the question is relational: filtering, joining, aggregating or ranking\nrows across entities. Write it by hand for anything that joins more than two tables, aggregates, or runs\non a hot path, and let the ORM handle simple key lookups and inserts. Before shipping a new query, run\n`EXPLAIN ANALYZE` against production-sized data and confirm the scan types and row estimates; after\nshipping, watch `pg_stat_statements` for the queries with the highest total time, not the highest mean.\nIn interviews, expect to write a query with a join, a `GROUP BY` and a window function, explain why a\ngiven predicate cannot use an index, and diagnose an N+1 from a description of symptoms.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the spend and inbox queries, replaces ORM loops with joins, and reads `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` to confirm each hot query uses the index it was designed for.","architect":"Decides which questions are answered by the transactional database versus a warehouse or search index, and sets the rule that hot-path SQL is reviewed with its plan.","fde":"Writes the one-off queries that prove value in a customer's data, and reads their slow-query logs to find the N+1 or missing index behind a complaint about latency."},"check":{"q":"A dashboard query filtering `messages` by `tenant_id` and the last 24 hours takes 8 seconds in\nproduction but 20 ms in staging. There is an index on `(tenant_id, created_at)`. What do you check and why?","a":"First run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` in production to see whether the index is used at all. If the\nplan shows a sequential scan, the predicate is probably not sargable (a function wrapped around\n`created_at`, or a type mismatch that makes PostgreSQL cast the column rather than the parameter, such as\nan `integer` column compared with a `numeric` parameter) or the statistics are stale after rapid growth,\nso `ANALYZE messages` and compare. If the\nindex is used but the query is still slow, look at actual versus estimated rows: one huge tenant can make\nthe range scan return millions of rows, in which case the aggregate itself is the cost and a pre-aggregated\ndaily table is the fix. Staging is fast simply because it is small; the planner's choice flips with data\nvolume, which is why plans must be checked against production-sized data."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can WHERE date(created_at) = '2026-10-20' not use a B-tree index on created_at?","options":["Date comparisons are never indexable in PostgreSQL","The index stores created_at values, not the result of date(), so the planner cannot seek on the function's output","The literal must be cast to timestamptz before the index can be used","B-tree indexes only support equality on integer columns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The index is ordered by the raw column. Applying a function hides the column from the planner; rewrite as a range (`created_at >= '2026-10-20' AND created_at < '2026-10-21'`) or create an index on an immutable expression such as `((created_at AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')::date)`."},{"q":"An endpoint loads 500 conversations and then each conversation's last message via an ORM relationship. Which fix addresses the root cause?","options":["Add an index on messages.content to speed up each lookup","Increase the connection pool size so the 500 queries run in parallel","Replace the per-row lookups with one query that joins or uses a lateral subquery to fetch the latest message per conversation","Cache the conversation list in Redis so the loop runs less often"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cost is 501 round trips, not slow individual queries. A single joined or lateral query lets the database do the work in one pass; more connections or a cache only hides the pattern."},{"q":"What is the main advantage of keyset pagination over LIMIT/OFFSET?","options":["It returns rows in a stable order even without an ORDER BY clause","It allows jumping directly to any page number","It seeks to the next page through the index, so deep pages cost the same as the first page","It avoids the need for an index on the sort column"],"answer":2,"explanation":"OFFSET reads and discards every preceding row, so cost grows with page depth. A keyset cursor uses the index to start exactly where the previous page ended; the trade is losing random page jumps."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/nosql","sources":["DeCandia et al., 2007 — Dynamo: Amazon's Highly Available Key-value Store","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Amazon DynamoDB","Official docs: MongoDB"],"why":"A relational database on one machine answers any question you can write in SQL, but it has a ceiling:\none machine's disk, memory and write throughput. Past that you must partition, and the moment rows live\non different machines, a join between them becomes a network operation and a transaction across them\nbecomes a distributed commit. Both are possible, but they are slow and fragile, and they are exactly the\noperations the relational model makes casual. Early web-scale systems hit this wall and responded by\nrestricting the data model: if every operation touches one partition, identified by a key, then each\nmachine can serve its partition independently, nodes can be added without coordination, and a machine\nfailure affects only its share of keys.\n\nThe naive alternative, sharding a relational database by hand, pushes the join and transaction problem\ninto application code where every engineer must know the sharding scheme, and it keeps the schema rigid\nwhile the data is spread across nodes that cannot be migrated atomically. NoSQL systems make the\nconstraint explicit instead: you declare a partition key, the store guarantees cheap operations within a\npartition, and anything across partitions is either unsupported or clearly expensive. A second\nmotivation is the data model itself rather than scale: a document store lets items of one collection carry\ndifferent fields and nests read-together data in one record, which suits event payloads and tool outputs\nwhose shape varies by type, and a graph database makes multi-hop traversals cheap that would be chains\nof self-joins in SQL. \"NoSQL\" is therefore a bundle of different answers to different problems, and the\nfirst design question is which problem you actually have.\n\nThe cost is real. Every query you did not design a key or index for is a full scan or impossible, and\n\"schemaless\" simply means the schema lives in your code, unenforced.","how":"### The families\n- **Key-value** (Redis, DynamoDB used simply, Riak): get and put a value by key. The simplest and the\n  fastest; no queries other than by key.\n- **Document** (MongoDB, Couchbase, DynamoDB with nested attributes): the value is a JSON-like document\n  with nested fields; you can index and query on fields inside it, within a collection.\n- **Wide-column** (Cassandra, ScyllaDB, Bigtable): rows grouped by a partition key and ordered within the\n  partition by clustering columns; built for very high write rates and time-ordered reads within a key.\n- **Graph** (Neo4j): nodes and edges, for queries that are mostly traversals (\"friends of friends\").\nSearch engines and vector databases are specialised stores that behave like document stores with a\ndifferent index at the core; they are covered in their own lessons.\n\n### Partitioning by key\nThe store hashes the partition key to pick a node (or a range of keys maps to a node). Every operation\nthat carries the key is routed to one node and costs one lookup regardless of total data size, which is\nthe whole point. Operations without the key must fan out to every node, so a design question is: \"which\nkey will every hot query have in hand?\" In DynamoDB this is the partition key plus an optional sort key;\nin Cassandra the partition key plus clustering columns; in MongoDB the shard key.\n\n### Replication and consistency\nEach partition is replicated to N nodes for durability. Writes are acknowledged after W replicas accept\nthem and reads consult R replicas; when W + R > N the read set must overlap the write set, so a read\nreaches at least one replica holding the latest acknowledged write (a strict quorum). Edge cases weaken\nthis in practice: sloppy quorums that accept writes on stand-in nodes, concurrent writes resolved by\nlast-write-wins, and writes that failed on some replicas but were not rolled back.\nChoosing W = 1 gives the fastest writes and the possibility that a read a moment later sees the old value:\n**eventual consistency**. Most stores let you choose per operation (Cassandra consistency levels,\nDynamoDB's `ConsistentRead`), and strongly consistent reads cost more and may fail when a replica is\ndown. This is the practical face of the CAP trade-off: during a partition, a store either refuses some\nrequests or serves possibly-stale data.\n\n### Modelling: access patterns first\nRelational design starts from entities and normalizes; NoSQL design starts from the list of queries and\nshapes the data so each one is a single-partition read. Two decisions dominate:\n1. **Embed or reference.** Embed child data inside the parent document when it is read together and\n   bounded in size (a message with its tool calls). Reference by id when the child set is unbounded or\n   shared (all messages of a conversation that may run to thousands). Documents have hard size limits\n   (16 MB in MongoDB, 400 KB per item in DynamoDB), and an unbounded embedded array will hit them.\n2. **Denormalize and duplicate.** Because there are no joins, data needed by two queries is often stored\n   twice, in two key layouts, and the application updates both. DynamoDB's single-table design takes this\n   to its conclusion: every entity type in one table with composite keys like `TENANT#t1#USER#u9` /\n   `SESSION#2026-10-20T10:00`, and global secondary indexes (GSIs) that re-key the same items for a second\n   access pattern. A GSI is maintained asynchronously, so reads through it are eventually consistent.\n\n### Hot partitions\nCapacity is configured per table, but each physical partition has its own throughput ceiling (in\nDynamoDB, documented as roughly 3,000 read units and 1,000 write units per second per partition). If one\ntenant produces 40% of all writes and tenant id is the partition key, that partition throttles while the\nrest of the cluster idles. DynamoDB's adaptive capacity and split-for-heat soften this for some\nworkloads, but they are not something to design around. Fixes are\nto add a high-cardinality component to the key (tenant + conversation, or tenant + a shard suffix) so a\nbusy tenant spreads over several partitions, at the cost of fan-out reads for \"everything for this tenant\".\n\n### Why wide-column stores write so fast\nCassandra, ScyllaDB and Bigtable sit on a log-structured merge tree (LSM) rather than the update-in-place\nB-tree of a relational engine. A write is appended to a commit log and inserted into an in-memory sorted\ntable; when that fills, it is flushed to disk as an immutable sorted file, and background compaction later\nmerges files and discards overwritten and deleted versions. Every write is sequential I/O with no read\nfirst, which is why ingest rates are high. The cost lands on reads, which may consult several files\n(bloom filters skip most), and on compaction, which consumes disk bandwidth in the background. Deletes\nare written as tombstones, so a partition that receives many deletes gets slower to read until compaction\nremoves them, a well-known operational trap with queue-like access patterns.\n\n### Transactions and indexes\nModern document stores support multi-document transactions, and DynamoDB offers transactional reads and\nwrites across a bounded set of items (up to 100 per transaction at the time of writing), but they cost\nmore capacity, are limited in scope and are not the default idiom. Secondary indexes exist in all of them,\nand each one is a copy of the data maintained on every write; in DynamoDB every GSI also consumes its own\nwrite capacity and storage, so \"index everything\" shows up directly on the bill.","example":"Agent sessions and their messages in DynamoDB, designed from two access patterns: \"list a user's recent\nsessions\" and \"load the last 20 messages of a session\". Each is a single-partition `Query`. Messages are\nreferenced, not embedded, because a session can grow without bound. The sort key carries a zero-padded\nsequence so lexical order is chronological (`MSG#00000010` sorts after `MSG#00000009`; unpadded, `MSG#10`\nwould sort before `MSG#9`). The caller assigns `seq` (for example from a counter on the session item), and\nthe conditional put is what makes a collision visible: it distinguishes \"my own retry already landed\"\nfrom \"another writer took this sequence number\", which must not be silently swallowed.\n\n```python\nimport boto3, time\nfrom boto3.dynamodb.conditions import Key\n\ntable = boto3.resource(\"dynamodb\").Table(\"agent-app\")\nDuplicate = table.meta.client.exceptions.ConditionalCheckFailedException\n\ndef create_session(tenant, user, session_id, title):\n    table.put_item(Item={\n        \"pk\": f\"TENANT#{tenant}#USER#{user}\",\n        \"sk\": f\"SESSION#{int(time.time()*1000):013d}#{session_id}\",\n        \"title\": title, \"session_id\": session_id,\n    })\n\ndef append_message(session_id, seq, role, content):\n    key = {\"pk\": f\"SESSION#{session_id}\", \"sk\": f\"MSG#{seq:08d}\"}\n    try:\n        table.put_item(Item={**key, \"role\": role, \"content\": content},\n                       ConditionExpression=\"attribute_not_exists(sk)\")  # never overwrite\n    except Duplicate:\n        existing = table.get_item(Key=key, ConsistentRead=True)[\"Item\"]\n        if existing[\"content\"] != content:    # someone else took this seq: caller must retry\n            raise                             # with the next one, or the message is lost\n        # else: our own earlier attempt landed; the retry is a no-op\n\ndef recent_sessions(tenant, user, limit=10):\n    r = table.query(\n        KeyConditionExpression=Key(\"pk\").eq(f\"TENANT#{tenant}#USER#{user}\")\n                               & Key(\"sk\").begins_with(\"SESSION#\"),\n        ScanIndexForward=False, Limit=limit)              # newest first\n    return r[\"Items\"]\n\ndef last_messages(session_id, limit=20):\n    r = table.query(\n        KeyConditionExpression=Key(\"pk\").eq(f\"SESSION#{session_id}\")\n                               & Key(\"sk\").begins_with(\"MSG#\"),\n        ScanIndexForward=False, Limit=limit,\n        ConsistentRead=True)                              # read-your-own-write after append\n    return list(reversed(r[\"Items\"]))\n```\n\nA query the design does not support, \"all messages mentioning refunds across all tenants\", would be a\nfull table scan; if it matters, the data also goes to a search index.","tradeoffs":["Single-partition operations scale horizontally with no ceiling in practice, but any query that lacks the partition key becomes a scatter-gather or a scan; the trade flips when ad-hoc questions dominate.","Flexible schemas let different record shapes coexist and evolve without migrations, but nothing enforces them, so validation moves into code and old shapes linger in the data forever.","Eventual consistency gives low latency and availability during failures, but a client that writes then reads may see stale data; pay for consistent reads on the paths where users would notice.","Denormalizing for read patterns makes each read one call, but every update must touch every copy, and a missed copy is a silent inconsistency with no foreign key to catch it.","Managed stores like DynamoDB remove operations work and give predictable per-request cost, but a new access pattern can mean a new index or a data migration rather than a new query.","A document store avoids the object-relational mismatch for nested, read-together data, but it loses the database's ability to join, constrain and aggregate across entities."],"pitfalls":["Choosing NoSQL \"for scale\" at tens of gigabytes; the symptom is a team rebuilding joins, aggregates and constraints in application code for data that a single PostgreSQL instance would handle easily.","Partitioning by tenant alone; the symptom is throttling errors on one partition while the cluster's overall utilisation is low.","Embedding an unbounded array (all messages inside the session document); the symptom is writes that get slower as the conversation grows, then a hard document-size error.","Expecting read-your-own-write on default eventually consistent reads; the symptom is a user who posts a message and does not see it on the next refresh, intermittently.","Issuing scans or non-key queries from request paths; the symptom is latency that rises with table size and a bill dominated by read capacity.","Letting document shapes drift (some records with `tokens`, some with `token_count`); the symptom is code full of defensive `get` calls and analytics that undercount.","Relying on secondary indexes as if they were free; the symptom is write cost multiplying with each index and GSI reads that lag the base table."],"when":"Reach for a NoSQL store when you can write down the handful of access patterns up front, when each one\ncan be keyed to a single partition, and when the write rate, data volume or availability requirement\nexceeds what one relational node provides comfortably. Typical fits in AI systems: per-session agent\nstate and message logs keyed by session, high-volume event and trace ingestion, feature or cache lookups\nby id. Stay relational while the data fits one machine and the questions are still changing; add a\nspecialised store (search, vector, key-value cache) beside PostgreSQL rather than replacing it. Measure\npartition heat and consistent-read usage once live. In interviews, expect to design keys for a given set\nof queries and to explain what happens to the query you forgot.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Designs partition and sort keys from the query list, writes conditional puts for idempotency, and chooses consistent reads only on the paths where staleness would be visible.","architect":"Decides which data leaves the relational system of record, how copies stay in sync, and what consistency each read path promises to users and downstream services.","fde":"Reads the customer's existing DynamoDB or Mongo model, spots the hot partition or the unbounded document, and plans how conversation data will be exported for evaluation and compliance."},"check":{"q":"You are storing agent runs in DynamoDB with `tenant_id` as the partition key and `run_id` as the\nsort key. One enterprise tenant generates half of all traffic. What goes wrong and how do you fix it?","a":"All of that tenant's reads and writes land on one partition, which has a fixed throughput ceiling,\nso that tenant sees throttling while the table's total capacity is mostly unused. Raising provisioned\ncapacity does not help because the limit is per partition. The fix is to spread the tenant across\npartitions by adding high-cardinality data to the key, for example `tenant_id#user_id` or\n`tenant_id#shard` where shard is derived from the run id. The cost is that \"all runs for the tenant\" now\nrequires querying each shard and merging, so you keep a GSI or a separate summary item for tenant-wide\nviews, accepting that it is eventually consistent. The general lesson is that the key must distribute\nload, not just identify ownership."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a document store make joins across collections expensive or unsupported?","options":["JSON documents cannot contain foreign keys","Data is partitioned by key across nodes, so a join would require moving matching records between machines","Document stores do not support indexes on nested fields","Joins require a fixed schema that document stores lack"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Horizontal scaling relies on each operation touching one partition. A general join must combine records that live on different nodes, which is the network-heavy operation partitioning was designed to avoid."},{"q":"A session document embeds every message and writes slow down as conversations grow. What is the correct restructuring?","options":["Compress the message array before storing it","Switch the session document to a strongly consistent write","Store messages as separate items keyed by session id with an ordered sort key, and reference them from the session","Add a secondary index on the embedded message array"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Embedding suits bounded, read-together data. An unbounded array makes every append rewrite the whole document and eventually hits the size limit; separate items keyed by session keep appends constant-cost."},{"q":"With N = 3 replicas, which setting guarantees a read sees the latest acknowledged write?","options":["W = 1, R = 1, because the write is replicated eventually","W = 1, R = 2, because two readers will outvote the stale replica","W = 2, R = 2, because the write and read replica sets must overlap","Any setting, because replication is synchronous by default"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Overlap requires W + R > N. With W = 2 and R = 2 (a strict quorum), at least one replica consulted by the read holds the write. W = 1 with R = 2 can read the two replicas that both missed it."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/redis","sources":["Official docs: Redis","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"why":"Every multi-instance backend needs some state that is shared across processes and read far more often\nthan it changes: the cached answer to an expensive call, the count of requests a client made this minute,\nwho currently holds a lock, the session for a logged-in user. Putting that in the primary database works\nuntil it does not: a relational database stores rows on disk with transaction logging and MVCC, so a\nsimple counter increment costs a write-ahead-log append and a row version, and ten thousand increments a\nsecond from a rate limiter compete with real transactions for the same CPU and I/O. Keeping it in process\nmemory is fast but wrong as soon as there are two processes, because each has its own copy and a load\nbalancer sends consecutive requests to different ones.\n\nThe naive middle ground, a plain key-value cache like memcached, solves the shared-lookup problem but\noffers only get and set, so anything that needs structure, such as \"add this item to the user's\nsorted-by-time list and trim it to 100\", becomes a read-modify-write race between two servers. Redis\nexists to make those operations single, atomic commands on rich data structures, executed in memory\nwith sub-millisecond latency, and to add just enough persistence, replication and clustering that it can\nbe trusted with more than a cache. In AI backends it ends up holding response caches keyed by prompt\nhash, per-tenant token-rate limits, semaphores that cap concurrent model calls, and the broker behind\njob queues.","how":"### Data structures, not just values\nEach key maps to one typed value: a **string** (also used for integers, with `INCR`), a **hash** (field\nto value map, good for an object), a **list** (push/pop at either end: a queue), a **set** and **sorted\nset** (members with a score, kept ordered: leaderboards, sliding windows, scheduled jobs), and a\n**stream** (an append-only log with consumer groups, the closest thing to Kafka inside Redis). Separately,\n**pub/sub** broadcasts a message to whoever is subscribed at that instant and stores nothing, so a\nsubscriber that is disconnected for a second misses what was published in that second; it suits live\nnotifications (push a token stream to whichever web node holds the user's socket), never work that must\nbe done. Commands\noperate on the structure in place, so \"push onto the list and trim it\" is two commands rather than a\nround trip to fetch, modify and write back. Keys can carry a TTL, after which they are deleted; TTLs are\nhow caches and rate-limit windows expire without a cleanup job.\n\n### Single-threaded execution is the point\nCommands are executed one at a time on a single thread (newer versions use extra threads for network\nI/O, but command execution stays serialized). That is why every command is atomic with no locking, and\nwhy `INCR`, `SET ... NX`, `ZADD` and friends can be used as the primitives of counters, locks and\nschedulers across many application servers. It is also why one slow command (`KEYS *`, a huge `SMEMBERS`,\na long Lua script) stalls every client. For multi-command atomicity there are `MULTI`/`EXEC` transactions\nand Lua scripts, which run as a single unit on the server.\n\n### Persistence and what \"durable\" means here\nRedis is memory-first; persistence is a configuration choice. **RDB** writes periodic snapshots, cheap\nbut losing everything since the last snapshot on a crash. **AOF** appends every write to a log, and its\n`fsync` policy decides the window of loss (every write, every second, or left to the OS). Neither makes\nRedis a system of record: treat data in Redis as rebuildable, or at least as acceptable to lose a second\nof, and put anything that must survive in a database.\n\n### Memory limits and eviction\n`maxmemory` caps the dataset; when it is reached, the eviction policy decides what happens:\n`noeviction` (writes fail), `allkeys-lru` (evict least recently used anything), `volatile-lru` and\n`volatile-ttl` (evict only keys with a TTL), plus LFU and random variants. LRU is approximated by sampling\na few keys rather than tracking exact order. A cache wants `allkeys-lru` or `allkeys-lfu`; a queue or lock\nstore wants `noeviction`, because evicting a job silently is worse than failing loudly. `maxmemory` and\nthe policy apply to the whole instance, including every numbered logical database, which is why mixing a\ncache and a queue in one instance is a design error.\n\n### Replication, Sentinel and Cluster\nA primary replicates asynchronously to replicas; Sentinel monitors and promotes a replica on failure.\nBecause replication is asynchronous, a failover can lose the last writes, which matters for locks and\nqueues. Redis Cluster shards keys across nodes by hashing the key into 16,384 slots; multi-key commands\nthen only work when all keys hash to the same slot, which you control with hash tags such as\n`{tenant42}:limit`. Managed offerings (ElastiCache, Memorystore, Upstash, Redis Cloud) run these for you.\nNote that Redis changed its licence in 2024 (to source-available terms) and the Linux Foundation fork\nValkey appeared; Redis 8 in 2025 added AGPLv3 as an option. The core commands are the same, but newer\nfeatures diverge, so check which one your platform ships.\n\n### The patterns that matter in AI backends\n- **Cache-aside:** look up `cache:{model}:{sha256(prompt+params)}`; on miss, call the model, `SET` with\n  a TTL. Only safe when the call is deterministic enough for your use (temperature 0, or you accept a\n  stored sample).\n- **Rate limiting:** `INCR` a per-tenant-per-window key and set its TTL on first increment. Atomic, so it\n  is correct across all servers, but a *fixed* window lets a client spend its whole limit in the last\n  second of one window and again in the first second of the next: up to 2× the limit in two seconds.\n  When that burst matters, keep a sorted set of request timestamps per client (`ZREMRANGEBYSCORE` the\n  expired ones, `ZCARD`, `ZADD`, in one script) for an exact sliding window, at one entry of memory per\n  request, or weight the previous window's count to approximate one in constant memory.\n- **Concurrency semaphore:** cap concurrent calls to a model provider with a counter bounded by a Lua\n  script, so a burst does not trip the provider's limits.\n- **Locks:** `SET lock:key token NX PX 30000` acquires only if absent and expires if the holder dies.\n  Release by checking the token in a Lua script, *because* a plain `DEL` from a holder whose lease\n  already expired would delete the next holder's lock. Expiry means a slow holder can lose the lock while\n  still working, so protected resources need a fencing token if correctness matters: a number from `INCR`\n  issued with each acquisition, which the resource stores and uses to reject writes carrying an older one.\n  Redlock (acquiring on a majority of independent nodes) addresses losing the lock on failover, but its\n  safety under clock drift and process pauses is disputed (Kleppmann's critique is the usual reference),\n  so treat Redis locks as an efficiency mechanism, not a correctness guarantee, unless fenced.\n- **Queues and streams:** lists for simple job queues (`LPUSH`/`BRPOP`), streams with consumer groups\n  for acknowledged, replayable delivery; BullMQ, Celery and RQ build on these.","example":"Three primitives in redis-py: an LLM response cache, a fixed-window rate limiter made atomic with a Lua\nscript, and a lock with a safe release. The script runs server-side so the increment and the TTL cannot\nbe separated by a crash: with two client calls, a process that dies after `INCR` and before `EXPIRE`\nleaves a key with no TTL, and that tenant is rate-limited forever once it reaches the limit. The cache key\nhashes every parameter that changes the output, because two requests that differ only in temperature or\nsystem prompt must not share an entry.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, redis, time, uuid\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\n\nr = redis.Redis(host=\"localhost\", port=6379, decode_responses=True)\n\ndef cached_completion(model, prompt, call, **params):\n    spec = json.dumps({\"prompt\": prompt, **params}, sort_keys=True)   # stable across dict order\n    key = f\"cache:{model}:\" + hashlib.sha256(spec.encode()).hexdigest()\n    hit = r.get(key)\n    if hit is not None:\n        return json.loads(hit)\n    out = call(model, prompt, **params)       # the expensive provider call\n    r.set(key, json.dumps(out), ex=3600)      # 1-hour TTL; eviction handles the rest\n    return out\n\nRATE_LUA = \"\"\"\nlocal n = redis.call('INCR', KEYS[1])\nif n == 1 then redis.call('EXPIRE', KEYS[1], ARGV[1]) end\nreturn n\n\"\"\"\nrate_script = r.register_script(RATE_LUA)\n\ndef allow(tenant, limit=60, window_s=60):\n    key = f\"rl:{tenant}:{int(time.time() // window_s)}\"   # e.g. rl:t42:29361600, one key per minute\n    return rate_script(keys=[key], args=[window_s]) <= limit   # 61st call this minute -> False\n\nRELEASE_LUA = \"\"\"\nif redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then return redis.call('DEL', KEYS[1]) end\nreturn 0\n\"\"\"\nrelease_script = r.register_script(RELEASE_LUA)\n\n@contextmanager\ndef with_lock(name, ttl_ms=30000):\n    token = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    if not r.set(f\"lock:{name}\", token, nx=True, px=ttl_ms):\n        raise RuntimeError(\"busy\")\n    try:\n        yield                                  # with with_lock(\"reindex:tenant42\"): ...\n    finally:\n        release_script(keys=[f\"lock:{name}\"], args=[token])   # only deletes if still ours\n```","tradeoffs":["Sub-millisecond operations in memory versus a dataset that must fit in RAM; when the working set exceeds memory, either eviction or a move to a disk-based store is forced.","AOF with fsync every write approaches database durability but cuts throughput; fsync every second is the usual compromise, accepting up to a second of loss on a crash.","Lua scripts and `MULTI` give atomicity across commands, but a long script blocks every other client; the trade flips to application-side logic when the script would touch thousands of keys.","Cluster mode scales memory and throughput across nodes, but multi-key operations require hash tags and cross-slot transactions are unavailable, which complicates scripts written for a single node.","Expiring locks never deadlock after a crash, but they can be lost by a slow holder; adding fencing tokens restores safety at the cost of making the protected resource check the token.","Using Redis as a queue is simple and fast, but asynchronous replication and eviction can drop jobs; when loss is unacceptable, a durable broker or a database-backed queue is the better trade."],"pitfalls":["Running `KEYS *` or a huge `SMEMBERS` in production; the symptom is every client stalling for seconds while the single thread walks the keyspace. Use `SCAN`.","Caching without a TTL or a sensible eviction policy; the symptom is memory growth until `maxmemory` and then either write failures (`noeviction`) or surprising evictions of non-cache keys.","Sharing one instance between cache and queue; the symptom is jobs vanishing under memory pressure because `allkeys-lru` evicted them.","Setting a lock without an expiry; the symptom is a permanent \"busy\" after one worker crashes mid-task.","Treating a lock with expiry as exclusive without fencing; the symptom is two workers writing the same resource after a GC pause or slow model call exceeded the TTL.","Hot keys and large values (a 5 MB cached response read 1,000 times a second); the symptom is one node saturating its network while others idle.","Opening a new connection per request instead of pooling; the symptom is connection-count spikes and latency dominated by handshakes.","Cache stampede when a popular key expires; the symptom is a burst of identical provider calls at the same second. Use a short lock or early refresh on the miss path."],"when":"Use Redis for state that is shared across processes, read-heavy or counter-shaped, and rebuildable or\ntolerable to lose: caches, rate limits, semaphores, sessions, short-lived coordination, and simple job\nqueues where occasional loss is acceptable. Do not use it as the system of record for conversations,\nbilling or anything you cannot recompute; use PostgreSQL for that and put Redis in front. Before adding\nit, confirm that the database genuinely cannot carry the load (a rate limit on a few hundred requests per\nsecond may be fine in PostgreSQL). Watch memory usage, evicted keys, hit ratio and slow-log entries. In\ninterviews, be ready to explain why single-threaded execution makes `INCR` a correct distributed counter,\nand where an expiring lock is unsafe.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements cache-aside, rate limiting and semaphores with atomic commands or Lua, sets TTLs on every key, and pools connections in FastAPI or Node services.","architect":"Decides what may live only in Redis versus the database, the eviction policy and persistence mode per instance, and whether the queue needs a durable broker instead.","fde":"Checks a customer's managed Redis tier, memory ceiling and eviction policy before deploying a cache or limiter, and explains what is lost on failover of their setup."},"check":{"q":"A team uses one Redis instance with `allkeys-lru` for both the LLM response cache and the Celery\njob queue. Jobs occasionally disappear under load. Explain the mechanism and the fix.","a":"Under memory pressure `allkeys-lru` evicts keys that have not been touched recently, regardless of\nwhat they are. Queue libraries keep state in many keys besides the main queue list: Celery keeps\nunacknowledged and scheduled (ETA) messages and task results in separate keys, and libraries such as RQ\nand BullMQ store one hash per job. A job that has been waiting is untouched since it was enqueued, and\na queue for a rarely used task type may be idle as a whole. The cache fills memory with fresh entries,\neviction samples and removes idle keys, and some of those are queue state, so jobs vanish without an\nerror. The fix is separation into two instances: numbered logical databases do not help, because\n`maxmemory` and the eviction policy are instance-wide. The queue instance gets `noeviction` and AOF\npersistence, so running out of memory fails enqueues loudly instead of silently discarding work; the\ncache instance gets `allkeys-lru` and TTLs on every key. If loss is still unacceptable, move the queue to\na durable broker or a database-backed queue."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a plain INCR in Redis be used as a correct rate-limit counter across many application servers?","options":["Redis replicates the increment to every application server's memory","Commands execute one at a time on a single thread, so each INCR is atomic without client-side locking","INCR acquires a row lock like a relational UPDATE","The client library retries INCR until the value is consistent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Serial execution means no two increments interleave, so the returned count is exact. A memcached-style get-then-set would race between servers; INCR's atomicity is a property of Redis's execution model."},{"q":"A lock is acquired with SET key token NX PX 30000. What problem remains if the holder takes longer than 30 seconds?","options":["The lock becomes permanent and other workers block forever","The token is rotated automatically and the holder cannot release it","The key expires, another worker acquires the lock, and two holders can act on the resource at once","Redis returns an error to the original holder on its next command"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Expiry protects against crashed holders but not slow ones. Once the key is gone a second worker acquires it; only a fencing token checked by the protected resource makes this safe."},{"q":"Which persistence and eviction configuration is appropriate for a Redis instance used as a job queue?","options":["RDB snapshots every hour with allkeys-lru","No persistence with volatile-lru, since jobs are short-lived","AOF persistence with noeviction, so a full instance fails writes rather than discarding jobs","AOF persistence with allkeys-lru, so old jobs make room for new ones"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A queue must fail loudly, never drop work silently. noeviction makes memory exhaustion an error the producer sees, and AOF limits what a crash loses; LRU policies would evict idle waiting jobs."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","sources":["Official docs: PostgreSQL","Berenson et al., 1995 — A Critique of ANSI SQL Isolation Levels","Ports and Grittner, 2012 — Serializable Snapshot Isolation in PostgreSQL"],"why":"Backends run many requests at once against the same rows. Without transactions, a crash between \"insert\nthe message\" and \"increment the tenant's token counter\" leaves the two permanently inconsistent, and\nwithout isolation, two requests that both read a balance, compute a new one and write it back will\noverwrite each other and lose one of the updates. These are not rare races: a budget check before an\nLLM call, a counter of concurrent runs, \"create the conversation if it does not exist\", and \"claim the\nnext job\" are all read-then-write sequences that run concurrently in any real service.\n\nThe naive defence, a global lock in the application, serializes everything and does not survive a second\nserver. The classic database answer, two-phase locking, makes readers and writers block each other, so a\nlong report query stalls every insert. PostgreSQL instead keeps multiple versions of each row and gives\neach transaction a consistent snapshot, which lets readers and writers proceed concurrently. The\nremaining question, which anomalies a snapshot still permits, is what isolation levels answer, and it is\nwhere most production data-corruption bugs in otherwise careful code come from. Understanding the\nlevels is cheaper than debugging a tenant whose token usage no longer adds up.","how":"### ACID in one paragraph\nA transaction (`BEGIN` ... `COMMIT`) is **atomic**: all of its writes become visible together or none do,\nvia rollback. **Consistency** means constraints hold at commit. **Isolation** is the subject of this\nlesson. **Durability** means a committed transaction survives a crash, which PostgreSQL achieves by\nwriting to the write-ahead log and fsyncing it before acknowledging the commit. That fsync is the floor\non commit latency, which is why batching many small writes into one transaction is faster than committing\neach, and why `synchronous_commit = off` (per transaction if you like) exists: it acknowledges before the\nflush, risking the loss of the last few commits on a crash, but never corruption.\n\n### MVCC: versions instead of locks\nEvery row version carries the id of the transaction that created it (`xmin`) and, once updated or\ndeleted, the one that ended it (`xmax`). An `UPDATE` does not overwrite; it writes a new version and\nmarks the old one as ended. A transaction takes a **snapshot** (the set of transaction ids that were\ncommitted when it started looking) and sees exactly the versions that were committed in that snapshot.\nThis is why a long `SELECT` never blocks an `UPDATE` and vice versa. Old versions become dead once no\nsnapshot can see them, and `VACUUM` reclaims them, which is why long-running or idle-in-transaction\nsessions cause table bloat: they pin old snapshots and prevent cleanup.\n\n### The isolation levels in PostgreSQL\nPostgreSQL offers three distinct behaviours (READ UNCOMMITTED is accepted but behaves as READ COMMITTED,\nbecause MVCC never exposes uncommitted data):\n\n1. **READ COMMITTED (default).** Each *statement* takes a fresh snapshot, so a transaction can see\n   different data in two consecutive selects if another transaction committed in between\n   (non-repeatable read). An `UPDATE` that finds its target row changed by a concurrent committed\n   transaction waits, then re-evaluates its `WHERE` on the new version and proceeds. This is fast and\n   rarely aborts, but a read-modify-write in application code is not protected at all.\n2. **REPEATABLE READ.** The snapshot is taken at the first statement and held for the whole transaction,\n   so repeated reads agree and, in PostgreSQL, phantoms do not appear either. If the transaction tries to\n   update a row that another transaction modified and committed since the snapshot, it fails with\n   serialization error `40001` and must be retried. It still permits **write skew**: two transactions\n   each read a condition, both see it true, each writes a *different* row, and the combined result\n   violates the condition.\n3. **SERIALIZABLE.** Serializable snapshot isolation (SSI) tracks read/write dependencies between\n   concurrent transactions and aborts one when it sees the \"dangerous structure\" (two consecutive\n   read-write dependencies) that any non-serializable cycle must contain. The check is conservative, so it\n   sometimes aborts transactions that would have been fine. The result is as if transactions ran one at\n   a time, with no explicit locking, at the cost of extra bookkeeping and more `40001` aborts under\n   contention. Any code using it must retry.\n\n### The anomalies, side by side\nNaming the anomalies makes the levels easy to compare (PostgreSQL behaviour, which is stricter than the\nSQL standard's minimums):\n- **Dirty read** (seeing another transaction's uncommitted write): impossible at every level.\n- **Non-repeatable read and phantom** (a re-read returns changed values or new rows): possible at READ\n  COMMITTED; prevented at REPEATABLE READ and SERIALIZABLE.\n- **Lost update** (two read-modify-writes, one overwrites the other): possible at READ COMMITTED; at\n  REPEATABLE READ the second writer gets `40001` instead.\n- **Write skew** (two transactions read an overlapping condition and write *different* rows): possible\n  at READ COMMITTED and REPEATABLE READ; prevented only at SERIALIZABLE.\n\n### The lost update, concretely\nTenant budget 1,000,000 tokens, 999,000 used. Two requests costing 800 each run `SELECT tokens_used`,\nboth read 999,000, both compute 999,800 ≤ 1,000,000, both `UPDATE tokens_used = 999800`. Final value:\n999,800, real usage 1,000,600, and the budget was exceeded without any error. Under READ COMMITTED this is\nfully allowed: the second `UPDATE` does wait for the first's row lock and re-checks its `WHERE` on the\nnew version, but its `SET` assigns the constant 999,800 that the application computed from a stale read,\nso re-evaluation changes nothing. The fixes below work by moving the read inside the protected step. Three fixes, each with a different cost:\n- **Atomic update:** `UPDATE tenants SET tokens_used = tokens_used + 800 WHERE id = $1 AND\n  tokens_used + 800 <= budget RETURNING tokens_used`. The check and the write are one statement that\n  waits for the row lock, re-evaluates, and returns zero rows if the budget is now blown. Cheapest, and\n  needs no retry at READ COMMITTED; at REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE the second statement gets `40001`\n  instead of re-evaluating, so it is still safe but must be retried.\n- **Pessimistic lock:** `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` locks the row for the transaction, so the second request\n  waits until the first commits, then reads the new value. Simple, but the lock is held for the whole\n  transaction, so never hold it across a network call.\n- **Optimistic:** read a `version` column, write with `WHERE version = $old`, and retry when zero rows\n  are affected; or run the transaction at SERIALIZABLE and retry on `40001`.\n\n### Row locks, SKIP LOCKED and deadlocks\n`FOR UPDATE` and the weaker `FOR NO KEY UPDATE` / `FOR SHARE` lock individual rows. `FOR UPDATE SKIP\nLOCKED` returns only rows nobody else holds, which is the primitive behind database-backed job queues:\nmany workers each claim a different row with one statement. `NOWAIT` errors instead of waiting. When two\ntransactions each hold a lock the other wants, PostgreSQL detects the cycle after `deadlock_timeout`\n(one second by default) and aborts one of them with `40P01`; the usual cause is acquiring locks in\ndifferent orders, and the usual fix is a consistent ordering (sort ids before locking).\n\n### Things that surprise people\n- After any error inside a transaction, PostgreSQL rejects every further statement until `ROLLBACK`\n  (or rollback to a `SAVEPOINT`). Catching the exception in code and continuing does not help.\n- Transaction-level connection poolers (PgBouncer in transaction mode) reuse server connections between\n  transactions, so session state such as `SET` and session-level advisory locks leaks or vanishes.\n  Prepared statements were in the same list until PgBouncer 1.21 added protocol-level support\n  (`max_prepared_statements`); check your pooler version and setting.\n- Isolation level is per transaction: `BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE` or `SET TRANSACTION` as the\n  first statement; a `SET default_transaction_isolation` changes the session default.","example":"The budget race above, written with psycopg (3.x). `reserve_atomic` is the single-statement fix.\n`reserve_serializable` shows the SERIALIZABLE-plus-retry pattern for logic that genuinely needs several\nstatements, such as checking a budget and then inserting the run that consumes it.\n\n```python\nimport time, psycopg\nfrom psycopg.errors import SerializationFailure\n\nDSN = \"postgresql://app@localhost/app\"\n\ndef reserve_atomic(conn, tenant_id, cost):\n    row = conn.execute(\n        \"\"\"UPDATE tenants SET tokens_used = tokens_used + %s\n           WHERE id = %s AND tokens_used + %s <= budget\n           RETURNING tokens_used\"\"\", (cost, tenant_id, cost)).fetchone()\n    return row is not None            # None means the budget would be exceeded\n\ndef reserve_serializable(conn, tenant_id, cost, run_id, attempts=5):\n    for attempt in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            with conn.transaction():          # call on an idle connection: SET must come first\n                conn.execute(\"SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE\")\n                used, budget = conn.execute(\n                    \"SELECT tokens_used, budget FROM tenants WHERE id = %s\",\n                    (tenant_id,)).fetchone()\n                if used + cost > budget:\n                    return False\n                conn.execute(\"UPDATE tenants SET tokens_used = %s WHERE id = %s\",\n                             (used + cost, tenant_id))\n                conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO runs (id, tenant_id, cost) VALUES (%s, %s, %s)\",\n                             (run_id, tenant_id, cost))   # unique id makes retries idempotent\n                return True\n        except SerializationFailure:      # SQLSTATE 40001: another txn won; retry\n            time.sleep(0.01 * 2 ** attempt)\n    raise RuntimeError(\"could not reserve budget after retries\")\n\nwith psycopg.connect(DSN) as conn:\n    ok = reserve_atomic(conn, tenant_id=42, cost=800)\n    conn.commit()\n    print(\"reserved\" if ok else \"over budget\")\n```\n\nRun two copies of either function concurrently against a tenant at 999,000 of 1,000,000 with a cost of\n800: each lets exactly one through (the atomic version by re-evaluating its `WHERE`, the serializable one\nby aborting and retrying the loser, which then sees the budget is exhausted). The naive select-then-update\nat READ COMMITTED, the same body as `reserve_serializable` without the isolation level, lets both through.","tradeoffs":["READ COMMITTED gives high throughput and almost no aborts, but every read-modify-write in application code is a race; it flips to a stricter level or explicit locks as soon as money or quotas are involved.","SERIALIZABLE guarantees correctness without reasoning about every race, but aborts rise with contention and every caller must implement retries; on a hot counter row it can thrash.","`FOR UPDATE` is simple and never aborts, but it blocks other writers for the length of the transaction, so a transaction that also calls an LLM holds the row for seconds.","Optimistic version columns avoid locks and work through a pooler, but under heavy contention most attempts fail and the retry loop becomes the bottleneck.","Long transactions give a consistent view for reports, but they pin old row versions, block `VACUUM` and cause bloat; the trade flips toward a replica or a snapshot export for long analytics.","Transaction-mode pooling multiplies the clients one database can serve, but costs session features such as advisory locks and `SET`, which must be moved into the transaction."],"pitfalls":["Select, compute, update in application code under the default level; the symptom is counters and balances that drift from the sum of their events, with no errors in the logs.","Holding a transaction (and its row locks) across an LLM or HTTP call; the symptom is lock waits and idle-in-transaction sessions that scale with provider latency, then pool exhaustion.","Not retrying on `40001` at REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE; the symptom is sporadic 500s under load that disappear when you retry by hand.","Assuming REPEATABLE READ prevents write skew; the symptom is an invariant spanning two rows (for example \"at most one active run per tenant\") violated by concurrent transactions that each saw it satisfied.","Catching an exception mid-transaction and continuing; the symptom is `current transaction is aborted, commands ignored until end of transaction block` on the next statement.","Deadlocks from locking rows in different orders in two code paths; the symptom is intermittent `40P01` errors roughly one second after the conflicting requests start.","Expecting READ UNCOMMITTED to behave as in other databases; in PostgreSQL it is READ COMMITTED.","Forgetting that a pooler in transaction mode breaks `SET` and advisory locks across statements; the symptom is settings that seem to apply randomly to other requests."],"when":"Start from the default level and make every write path safe by construction: atomic single-statement\nupdates for counters and quotas, `FOR UPDATE` when a multi-statement decision must be exclusive and the\ntransaction stays short, `SKIP LOCKED` for job claiming, and SERIALIZABLE with retries for genuinely\nmulti-row invariants that are hard to lock explicitly. Keep transactions short and never span a network\ncall. Watch `pg_stat_activity` for idle-in-transaction sessions, lock waits, and serialization failures\nper second. In interviews, expect to be given a read-modify-write and asked which level or lock fixes it,\nto explain write skew, and to say why a long transaction slows down a table it never wrote to.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the atomic update or the `FOR UPDATE` block, adds the `40001` retry loop, and keeps the transaction boundary inside the request rather than around the model call.","architect":"Sets the policy: which invariants are enforced by the database versus the application, the default isolation level, and the rule that transactions never cross service or provider calls.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's drifting usage counters or lock pileups from `pg_stat_activity`, and explains why their pooler configuration broke session-level settings."},"check":{"q":"A service enforces \"at most three concurrent runs per tenant\" by counting active runs in a\nREPEATABLE READ transaction, then inserting a new run if the count is below three. Under load, tenants\nend up with five active runs. Why, and what are two fixes?","a":"This is write skew. Each transaction's snapshot shows two active runs, each inserts a *new* row\nrather than updating a row the other touched, so neither conflicts with the other at REPEATABLE READ and\nboth commit, giving four or five runs. Fix one: run the transaction at SERIALIZABLE and retry on `40001`;\nSSI detects the read-write dependency between the count and the inserts and aborts one. Fix two: make\nthe conflict explicit by locking something shared, for example `SELECT ... FROM tenants WHERE id = $1\nFOR UPDATE` before counting, or a per-tenant `pg_advisory_xact_lock`, and drop to READ COMMITTED, so the\nsecond transaction waits and its count statement takes a fresh snapshot after the first commits. At\nREPEATABLE READ the lock alone is not enough: the snapshot was taken by the locking statement before it\nwaited, so the recount still cannot see the other transaction's insert. A third option is to keep an `active_runs` counter on the tenant row and use a\nsingle conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE active_runs < 3`, which turns the invariant into one atomic\nstatement and avoids retries entirely."},"quiz":[{"q":"Under PostgreSQL's default READ COMMITTED level, why can two transactions both pass a \"balance is sufficient\" check and overspend?","options":["READ COMMITTED allows reading uncommitted data from the other transaction","Each transaction reads the committed balance, computes a new value, and writes it; neither read locks the row, so both writes succeed and one update is lost","The database merges both updates by adding them together","READ COMMITTED serializes writes but not reads, so the second write is silently dropped"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A plain SELECT takes no lock and the UPDATE only waits for a concurrent writer of the same row. Both transactions read the same committed value, so the later write overwrites the earlier one."},{"q":"What does SERIALIZABLE in PostgreSQL require from application code that READ COMMITTED does not?","options":["Explicit FOR UPDATE locks on every row that will be modified","A single connection per transaction without pooling","A retry loop, because the database may abort a transaction with a serialization failure when a conflict is detected","Disabling MVCC so that writers block readers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"SSI resolves dangerous dependency cycles by aborting one transaction with SQLSTATE 40001. The outcome is correct only if the caller retries; code that treats the abort as a fatal error turns safety into 500s."},{"q":"Why does an idle session that opened a transaction an hour ago slow down tables it never touched?","options":["It holds an exclusive lock on the whole database","Its snapshot keeps old row versions alive, so VACUUM cannot reclaim dead tuples and tables bloat","It consumes all available connections in the pool","PostgreSQL pauses checkpoints while any transaction is open"],"answer":1,"explanation":"MVCC keeps a row version until no snapshot can see it. An old open snapshot (or an old assigned transaction id) holds back the cleanup horizon for the whole database, so dead versions accumulate in every table, indexes grow and scans read more pages; this is why idle-in-transaction is monitored."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/message-queues","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Apache Kafka","Official docs: Amazon SQS","Official docs: RabbitMQ"],"why":"A request that triggers five downstream actions (store the upload, extract text, embed chunks, update\nthe index, notify the user) cannot do them inline: the HTTP call would take minutes, any one failure\nfails the whole request, and a burst of uploads would hit every downstream service at the burst rate.\nCalling the services synchronously couples their availability (if the embedder is down, uploads fail),\ntheir capacity (the slowest service sets the pace), and their deployment (a change in one means retries\nacross all). The naive fix, \"retry in a background thread\", loses the work when the process restarts and\nstill has no buffer when the downstream is slower than the upstream.\n\nA queue solves the three couplings at once. Producers write a message and return; the broker stores it\ndurably; consumers pull at their own rate; if a consumer is down the messages wait. The same mechanism\ngives fan-out (several consumer groups read the same event), load levelling (a burst becomes a backlog\nthat drains), and a natural place to put retries and dead-letter handling. In AI systems the pattern\nappears constantly: document ingestion pipelines, batch embedding, webhook buffering from providers,\ndecoupling an agent's tool calls from the services that execute them, and emitting events (run started,\ntool failed, tokens consumed) that observability and billing consume independently.\n\nThe price is that the system becomes asynchronous and eventually consistent, and that every consumer\nmust tolerate seeing the same message twice.","how":"### The core loop\nA producer publishes a message to a named destination (queue, topic, exchange). The broker persists it\nand makes it available. A consumer receives it, does the work, and **acknowledges** it; only then does the\nbroker consider it done. If the consumer crashes before acknowledging, the broker redelivers to another\nconsumer. This ack-after-work protocol is what makes delivery **at-least-once**: a consumer that finishes\nthe work and dies before the ack causes a duplicate. Acknowledging *before* the work gives\n**at-most-once**: no duplicates, but a crash loses the message. \"Exactly-once\" across arbitrary side\neffects is not achievable; what systems offer is at-least-once delivery plus **idempotent** consumers\n(deduplicate by message id or business key), which is effectively-once from the outside. Kafka's\n\"exactly-once semantics\" is real but narrower than the name: idempotent producers plus transactions make\na read-from-Kafka, write-to-Kafka pipeline commit its outputs and its consumer offsets atomically, so\nnothing inside Kafka is duplicated. The guarantee ends at Kafka's edge; a consumer that calls an email\nAPI or writes to a database is back to at-least-once plus idempotency.\n\n### Publishing reliably\nThe producer side has its own failure, the **dual write**. A handler that commits an order to the database\nand then publishes `order.created` can crash between the two: the order exists and no event ever does.\nPublishing first is worse: consumers act on an order the database then rolls back. Retrying the publish\ndoes not help when the process is gone. The standard fix is the **transactional outbox**: write the event\ninto an `outbox` table in the same database transaction as the business change, and let a separate relay\n(a poller, or change-data capture reading the database log) publish outbox rows and mark them sent. The\nrelay can itself crash after publishing and before marking, so it too is at-least-once, and consumers\nstill deduplicate.\n\n### Ordering, partitions and parallelism\nA single queue consumed by one worker is ordered; the moment you add workers, messages complete out of\norder. Kafka's answer is **partitions**: a topic is split into N ordered logs, a message's key decides its\npartition (same key, same partition, strict order), and a **consumer group** assigns each partition to\nexactly one consumer in the group. Parallelism is therefore capped by partition count, and a skewed key\n(one huge tenant) makes one partition hot. SQS FIFO queues offer the same idea with message group ids.\nSQS standard queues give best-effort ordering only. A RabbitMQ queue is FIFO as stored, but competing\nconsumers and requeued redeliveries mean messages are not *processed* in order once there is more than\none consumer or any failure.\n\n### Backpressure and retries\nA queue only levels load if someone watches the **lag** (messages waiting, or the age of the oldest\nmessage); an unbounded backlog is a silent outage. The arithmetic is simple and worth doing: if producers\nadd λ messages per second and consumers finish μ, the backlog grows at λ − μ while λ > μ and drains at\nμ − λ afterwards. A burst that leaves 120,000 messages waiting, with consumers at 500/s against a steady\n400/s arrival, drains in 120,000 ÷ 100 = 1,200 s, twenty minutes during which every new message waits\nbehind it. Doubling consumers to 1,000/s cuts that to 200 s, which is why consumer autoscaling keys off\nlag or oldest-message age rather than CPU. If λ exceeds the maximum μ for long, the queue is not levelling\nload, it is accumulating debt, and the producer must be slowed or work shed. Consumers limit in-flight messages (RabbitMQ\n`prefetch`, SQS `MaxNumberOfMessages`, Kafka `max.poll.records`). Failed messages are retried with\nbackoff, and after N attempts go to a **dead-letter queue** (DLQ) so one poison message cannot block the\nrest. A message whose payload is large (a PDF, a transcript) should not travel through the broker:\nstore it in object storage and send the reference (the claim-check pattern).\n\n### Kafka: a distributed, replayable log\nKafka keeps messages on disk for a configured retention (time or size), regardless of whether they were\nconsumed. Consumers track their own **offset** per partition and commit it; replaying means resetting the\noffset. Durability comes from replicating each partition to several brokers and, for producers,\n`acks=all` plus the idempotent producer setting, which deduplicates retries within a session. Log\ncompaction keeps the latest message per key, turning a topic into a changelog. Kafka is built for high\nthroughput, many consumers of the same stream, and event sourcing; it costs an operations burden (or a\nmanaged service) and a rigid partition-based parallelism model. It is a poor fit for a small job queue\nwhere each job needs individual retry and priority. Recent Kafka releases add share groups (KIP-932),\nqueue-style consumption with per-message acknowledgement beyond the partition count; check its maturity\non your version before relying on it.\n\n### SQS: a managed queue without replay\nSQS is a fully managed queue: no brokers to run, near-unlimited throughput on standard queues, and\npay-per-request. A consumer receives messages with a **visibility timeout**; the message is hidden from\nothers until the consumer deletes it or the timeout expires, at which point it reappears. Long polling\nwaits up to 20 seconds for messages instead of returning empty. Retention is bounded (days, not forever),\nmessages are deleted when acknowledged, so there is no replay; a redrive policy moves messages to a DLQ\nafter `maxReceiveCount` attempts. Standard queues are at-least-once with best-effort ordering; FIFO\nqueues add ordering per message group and a deduplication id honoured for a five-minute window. Message\nsize is bounded (256 KiB for most of SQS's history, raised to 1 MiB in 2025; check current limits), so\nlarge payloads go to S3.\n\n### RabbitMQ: a routing broker\nRabbitMQ implements AMQP: producers publish to an **exchange**, bindings route messages to **queues** by\nrouting key (direct), pattern (topic), to all bound queues (fanout), or by headers. This makes complex\nrouting and per-consumer queues easy. Consumers ack or nack; `prefetch` bounds in-flight messages per\nconsumer; dead-letter exchanges handle rejects and TTL expiry. Quorum queues replicate via Raft for\ndurability; the newer streams feature adds a Kafka-like replayable log. It is a good fit for task\ndistribution with rich routing and moderate throughput, and it needs operating like any stateful system.\n\n### Choosing\nNeed replay, many independent consumers of the same events, or very high throughput: Kafka (or a managed\nequivalent). Need a simple durable buffer with minimal operations on AWS: SQS. Need routing rules,\nper-message priorities or AMQP interop: RabbitMQ. Need a small job queue and already run PostgreSQL: a\ntable with `SKIP LOCKED` is often enough, and Redis-backed queues sit between that and a real broker.","example":"An SQS consumer for an embedding job. It long-polls, processes each message idempotently (the job id is\nthe dedup key stored with the result), extends the visibility timeout of every message in the batch while\nthey wait and while the slow model call runs, so none is redelivered mid-flight, and deletes only after\nsuccess. Failures are left to expire and\nretry; the queue's redrive policy sends repeat offenders to a DLQ.\n\n```python\nimport json, threading, boto3\n\nsqs = boto3.client(\"sqs\")\nQUEUE = \"https://sqs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/embed-jobs\"\n\ndef extend_visibility(receipt, stop, every=30, extra=60):\n    while not stop.wait(every):                        # heartbeat while work is in flight\n        sqs.change_message_visibility(QueueUrl=QUEUE, ReceiptHandle=receipt,\n                                      VisibilityTimeout=extra)\n\ndef process(job):\n    if results.exists(job[\"job_id\"]):                  # idempotent: seen this job already\n        return\n    vectors = embed(load_chunks(job[\"document_id\"]))   # the slow call\n    results.store(job[\"job_id\"], vectors)              # write result keyed by job id\n\nwhile True:\n    resp = sqs.receive_message(QueueUrl=QUEUE, MaxNumberOfMessages=5,\n                               WaitTimeSeconds=20, VisibilityTimeout=60)\n    batch = resp.get(\"Messages\", [])\n    stops = [threading.Event() for _ in batch]          # heartbeat all, not just the current one\n    for msg, stop in zip(batch, stops):\n        threading.Thread(target=extend_visibility,\n                         args=(msg[\"ReceiptHandle\"], stop), daemon=True).start()\n    for msg, stop in zip(batch, stops):\n        try:\n            process(json.loads(msg[\"Body\"]))\n            sqs.delete_message(QueueUrl=QUEUE, ReceiptHandle=msg[\"ReceiptHandle\"])\n        except Exception as e:\n            log.warning(\"job failed, will be redelivered: %s\", e)   # no delete: retry later\n        finally:\n            stop.set()\n```\n\n`results`, `embed`, `load_chunks` and `log` are the application's own; the shape of the loop is what\nmatters. Without the batch-wide heartbeat, messages two to five would sit unprocessed while message one\nran, their 60-second timeout would lapse, and another worker would receive them too. With Kafka the same\nloop would poll, process, then commit the offset; with RabbitMQ it would set `basic_qos(prefetch_count=5)`,\n`basic_consume`, and `basic_ack` after success.","tradeoffs":["Decoupling producers from consumers gains availability and burst tolerance, but the caller no longer knows when (or whether) the work finished, so every flow needs a status path or an event back.","At-least-once delivery never loses acknowledged work, but it forces idempotent consumers; at-most-once is simpler and acceptable only for data you can afford to drop, such as metrics samples.","Kafka's retained log allows replay and many consumer groups, but operations cost, partition planning and the lack of per-message retry make it heavy for a plain job queue.","SQS removes all broker operations and scales without planning, but no replay, bounded retention and a small message size push payloads to S3 and audit history to a database.","RabbitMQ's exchanges give fine-grained routing and priorities, but a single very hot queue is harder to scale than Kafka partitions, and the cluster must be run and upgraded.","Partition-keyed ordering preserves per-entity sequence, but parallelism is capped by partition count and a skewed key concentrates load; the trade flips toward unordered queues when order does not matter."],"pitfalls":["Non-idempotent consumers; the symptom is duplicate emails, double charges or two embeddings per chunk after any consumer restart or timeout.","A visibility timeout or ack deadline shorter than the work (a 90-second model call under a 30-second timeout); the symptom is the same job running on two workers and redelivery counts climbing under load.","No dead-letter queue; the symptom is one malformed message redelivered forever, consuming a worker and hiding behind it a growing backlog.","Committing the Kafka offset (or deleting the SQS message) before processing; the symptom is work that silently disappears whenever a worker crashes.","Nobody alerting on queue lag or oldest-message age; the symptom is a backlog discovered hours later by a user asking why their document never got indexed.","Choosing Kafka for a few messages per second of job traffic; the symptom is a team spending its time on partition rebalances and consumer-group tuning instead of the product.","Committing to the database and then publishing the event as a second step; the symptom is rows that exist with no downstream effect (never indexed, never notified) after a deploy or crash, with no error anywhere because the publish was simply never attempted.","Assuming order on SQS standard queues or across Kafka partitions; the symptom is \"updated\" events processed before \"created\" events and intermittent not-found errors.","Sending multi-megabyte payloads through the broker; the symptom is size errors, slow brokers and a broker that has become an accidental blob store."],"when":"Introduce a queue when work must outlive the request, when a downstream is slower or less available than\nthe caller, when several consumers need the same events, or when bursts must be smoothed. Do not\nintroduce one for a synchronous read the user is waiting on, or when a database table with `SKIP LOCKED`\nwould carry the load without a new system. Measure lag, oldest-message age, redelivery rate and DLQ\ndepth from day one. Day to day this shows up as \"should this endpoint enqueue or call directly\", choosing\na partition key, and tuning timeouts to the real duration of an LLM call. In interviews, expect to\nexplain at-least-once, design a partition key for a tenant-skewed workload, and justify Kafka versus SQS\nfor a given scenario.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes idempotent consumers, sets visibility or ack timeouts to match model latency, wires the DLQ and alerts on lag before the first production backlog.","architect":"Chooses the broker per workload (log versus queue), defines topics, keys and retention, and decides where the status of asynchronous work is recorded for callers.","fde":"Integrates with the customer's existing Kafka or SQS, negotiates message schemas and ordering guarantees, and sizes consumers against their throughput and quota limits."},"check":{"q":"A document pipeline on SQS standard occasionally embeds the same chunk twice and sometimes\nprocesses a document's \"deleted\" event before its \"created\" event. What is happening and how would you\nchange the design?","a":"Both are expected properties of a standard queue. Duplicate embeddings come from at-least-once\ndelivery: a worker finished the model call but crashed or exceeded its visibility timeout before deleting\nthe message, so it was redelivered. The fix is an idempotent consumer: key each result by chunk id or\njob id and skip or overwrite when it already exists, and set the visibility timeout above the real\nprocessing time with heartbeat extension for long calls. The ordering problem comes from standard queues\noffering only best-effort order across many workers. Either move to a FIFO queue with the document id as\nthe message group id, so events for one document are serialized while different documents proceed in\nparallel, or make the consumer order-independent by storing a version per document and ignoring events\nolder than the current state. Add a DLQ so poison messages do not block either path."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is \"exactly-once delivery\" not something a broker can promise for a consumer that calls an external API?","options":["Brokers lose messages under load, so at-least-once is the best achievable","The consumer can complete the external call and then fail before acknowledging, so the broker cannot know whether the side effect happened and must redeliver","External APIs do not support message acknowledgements","Exactly-once requires FIFO queues, which most brokers lack"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The acknowledgement and the side effect are separate operations that cannot be made atomic across systems. Redelivery is the safe default, so idempotency on the consumer side is what makes the outcome effectively once."},{"q":"A Kafka topic has 4 partitions and a consumer group has 8 consumers. What happens?","options":["Each partition is read by two consumers for redundancy","Kafka automatically increases the partition count to 8","Four consumers sit idle, because each partition is assigned to at most one consumer in a group","Messages are round-robined across all 8 consumers regardless of partition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Parallelism within a classic consumer group is bounded by partition count. Extra consumers are standby only, which is why partition count must be planned for peak consumer parallelism (newer share groups are a separate mechanism with different rules)."},{"q":"Which situation makes SQS a better choice than Kafka?","options":["Multiple independent teams need to replay the same event history","A team needs a durable buffer for background jobs with minimal operations and no need to re-read past messages","Strict ordering of every message across the whole topic is required","Messages must be retained indefinitely as the system of record"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SQS offers a managed, pay-per-use queue with retries and dead-lettering but deletes messages on acknowledgement and bounds retention. Replay, long retention and many consumer groups are Kafka's strengths."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: PostgreSQL (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED)","Official docs: Celery","Official docs: Temporal"],"why":"The forcing problem is any request whose real work takes longer than a client will wait: embedding a\n300-page PDF, running a twenty-step agent, sending ten thousand notifications, re-indexing a tenant.\nDoing it inline fails three ways at once. Load balancers and browsers cut idle connections (commonly\naround a minute by default; check your own), so the client sees an error while the server keeps working.\nThe web process's capacity is consumed by slow work, so fast requests queue behind it. And a failure at\nminute nine leaves partial side effects with no record of how far it got.\n\nThe first naive fix is \"fire and forget\": start a thread, an `asyncio` task or a framework background\ntask and return. This fails because the job now exists only in one process's memory. The next deploy,\nout-of-memory kill or autoscaler scale-in silently destroys every in-flight and waiting job, and nobody\nis told. There is no retry, no visibility, no backpressure (a burst of uploads becomes a burst of\nthreads) and no way to scale the workers separately from the web tier. Teams discover this the day a\ncustomer asks why their document never got indexed.\n\nA durable worker fixes the root cause by making the job a *record* rather than a *call*. Once the record\nis committed, three invariants hold: the job is not lost; at most one worker runs it at a time; and if\nthat worker dies, the job becomes runnable again. That is the same at-least-once contract a message\nqueue gives, seen from the worker's side and extended with state the worker owns: attempts, next run\ntime, progress, result. In AI systems nearly every expensive operation is such a job, and \"durable\" is\nwhat lets you ship five deploys a day without aborting the agent runs in progress.","how":"### The job is a row\nA job record carries an id, a type (which handler runs it), a payload (arguments, small; large inputs go\nto object storage by reference), a state (`queued`, `running`, `succeeded`, `failed`, `dead`), an attempt\ncounter, a `run_at` time, a lease (`locked_by`, `lease_until`), and an error or result. Each field exists\nfor a reason: `run_at` gives delayed and scheduled jobs for free; `attempts` makes retry policy a function\nof the row instead of worker memory; the lease is what makes crash recovery possible; and the row itself\nis the audit trail and the status endpoint for the caller.\n\n### Enqueue is a write, not a call\nBecause the job is a row, enqueueing is an insert. If the job table lives in the same database as the\nbusiness data, insert it in the *same transaction* as the change that caused it: the job exists if and\nonly if the business change committed. If the queue is a separate system (Redis, SQS, a broker) you\nhave a dual-write problem: \"commit, then enqueue\" loses the job if the process dies between the two;\n\"enqueue, then commit\" runs a job for data that may have been rolled back. The transactional outbox\npattern is the general cure: write the job to a table in the business transaction and let a relay\nforward it to the external queue.\n\n### Claim with a lease, not a lock\nA worker claims a job by atomically flipping `queued` to `running` and setting `lease_until = now + T`\nand `locked_by = me`. In PostgreSQL the idiom is a single `UPDATE ... WHERE id = (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE\nSKIP LOCKED LIMIT 1) RETURNING *`. `SKIP LOCKED` exists because without it N workers block on the same\nhot row and serialize; with it each worker skips rows another worker is examining and takes the next.\nThe claim is a *lease* rather than a transaction-long row lock because a lock held by a dead process is\nheld until the connection drops, and holding a transaction open for a ten-minute job pins a database\nconnection. A lease simply expires: the claim query also matches `running` rows whose `lease_until` is\nin the past, which is exactly how an orphaned job from a crashed worker gets picked up. SQS calls the\nsame thing a visibility timeout; Redis-based libraries call it a lock with a duration the worker renews.\n\n### Heartbeats and the long-job dilemma\nIf the lease is shorter than the job, a healthy worker loses its job to a colleague mid-run, and both\nrun it. If the lease is very long, recovery from a real crash waits that long. The usual answer is a\nshort lease plus a heartbeat that extends it while work is in progress, paired with a hard maximum\nduration so a worker that is alive but stuck cannot hold a job forever.\n\n### Finish, retry, dead-letter\nOn success the worker sets `succeeded` and stores the result. On failure it classifies the error.\nRetryable errors (timeouts, 429s, 5xxs from a model provider) set `queued` again with `run_at = now +\nbackoff(attempts)`, exponential with jitter because a dependency recovering from an outage would\notherwise be hit by every retry at the same instant. Permanent errors (validation, 4xx, a missing\ndocument) go straight to `dead`: burning five retries on them only delays the moment a human sees the\nproblem. After `max_attempts` the job is `dead` and stays inspectable, which is the dead-letter queue in\ntable form. Nothing is ever deleted on failure.\n\n### Idempotency is the price of durability\nThe worker can finish the side effect, then die before marking the row done. The lease expires, the job\nruns again. There is no way to make \"call the external API\" and \"update the row\" atomic, so the handler\nmust tolerate a second run: upsert keyed by job id, pass an idempotency key to payment and email\nproviders, check the store before acting. For multi-step jobs, checkpoint progress (a step index, or a\nper-chunk `done` marker) so a retry *resumes* instead of restarting; a 40-minute job that fails at minute\n39 should cost one more minute, not forty. Taken to its conclusion, recording every step and replaying\non failure is what durable execution engines such as Temporal do, and what agent runtimes need for long\ntool-calling loops; the lessons on durable workflow execution and checkpointing go deeper.\n\n### Where the store lives\nA PostgreSQL table with `SKIP LOCKED` needs no new infrastructure, gives transactional enqueue, and is\ntrivially inspectable; it polls, so idle workers cost queries, and finished rows must be archived or the\ntable bloats. Redis-backed libraries (Celery with Redis, RQ, BullMQ, Sidekiq) give blocking pops and low\nlatency, but inherit Redis's weaker durability and the dual-write problem. Broker-backed workers (Celery\nwith RabbitMQ, SQS consumers) are the message-queue lesson from the consumer's chair. Choose by what\nlosing a job costs and by throughput; a few hundred jobs per second (illustrative) is well within a\nPostgres table on modest hardware.\n\n### Sizing, fairness and shutdown\nWorker count follows from Little's law: jobs in progress = arrival rate × average duration. If uploads\narrive at 0.5 per second and an embedding job averages 4 minutes, 0.5 × 240 = 120 jobs are running at\nsteady state, so fewer than 120 worker slots means the backlog grows without bound, however fast each\nworker is. Size for the peak arrival rate you must absorb within your latency target, and remember that\nthe model provider's rate limit, not your worker count, may be the real ceiling.\n\nA single shared queue in run order is unfair: one tenant that enqueues 10,000 jobs puts every other\ntenant's job behind them. The usual fixes are a per-tenant concurrency cap enforced in the claim (skip\ntenants already at their limit), a claim that rotates across tenants rather than taking the globally\noldest row, or separate queues per priority class so interactive work is never behind bulk backfills.\n\nDeploys are the most frequent \"crash\". A worker that receives SIGTERM should stop claiming, finish or\ncheckpoint the current job within the platform's grace period, and exit; anything it cannot finish is\nrecovered by lease expiry, which is why the lease, not the shutdown handler, is the correctness mechanism.\n\n### What to measure\nQueue depth, age of the oldest queued job, run duration per type, attempts histogram, and dead count.\nThe first two detect a stalled worker fleet; the last two detect a failing dependency.","example":"A PostgreSQL-backed worker in Python using psycopg 3. The claim reclaims expired leases, the handler is\nrequired to be idempotent, retryable failures are rescheduled with jittered backoff, and permanent\nfailures go to `dead` without burning attempts. Every finishing update is guarded by `locked_by`, so a\nworker whose lease expired and was taken over cannot overwrite the new holder's outcome. Heartbeating is\nomitted for brevity; for jobs longer than the five-minute lease, extend `lease_until` from a timer thread.\n\n```python\nimport random, time, uuid, psycopg\n\nWORKER = str(uuid.uuid4())\nCLAIM = \"\"\"\nUPDATE jobs SET state='running', locked_by=%s, attempts=attempts+1,\n                lease_until=now() + interval '5 minutes'\nWHERE id = (SELECT id FROM jobs\n            WHERE (state='queued' AND run_at <= now())\n               OR (state='running' AND lease_until < now()    -- orphaned by a dead worker\n                   AND attempts < 5)                          -- crash-loopers left for a sweeper\n            ORDER BY run_at LIMIT 1 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED)\nRETURNING id, type, payload, attempts\"\"\"\nDONE = \" WHERE id=%s AND locked_by=%s\"     # fence: only the current lease holder may finish\n\ndef backoff(attempt):                      # 2, 4, 8 ... s, capped, with jitter\n    return min(2 ** attempt, 300) * (0.5 + random.random())\n\ndef embed_document(job_id, payload):       # idempotent: keyed writes, safe to re-run\n    for chunk in load_chunks(payload[\"document_id\"]):\n        if not vectors.exists(chunk.id):   # resume: skip chunks a previous attempt finished\n            vectors.upsert(chunk.id, embed(chunk.text))\n\nHANDLERS = {\"embed_document\": embed_document}\n\ndef run_one(conn):\n    with conn.transaction():\n        row = conn.execute(CLAIM, (WORKER,)).fetchone()\n    if row is None:\n        return False\n    job_id, kind, payload, attempts = row\n    try:\n        HANDLERS[kind](job_id, payload)\n        conn.execute(\"UPDATE jobs SET state='succeeded', finished_at=now()\" + DONE, (job_id, WORKER))\n    except RetryableError as e:\n        if attempts >= 5:\n            conn.execute(\"UPDATE jobs SET state='dead', error=%s\" + DONE, (str(e), job_id, WORKER))\n        else:\n            conn.execute(\"UPDATE jobs SET state='queued', error=%s, \"\n                         \"run_at=now() + %s * interval '1 second'\" + DONE,\n                         (str(e), backoff(attempts), job_id, WORKER))\n    except Exception as e:                 # permanent: straight to dead, keep it inspectable\n        conn.execute(\"UPDATE jobs SET state='dead', error=%s\" + DONE, (str(e), job_id, WORKER))\n    conn.commit()\n    return True\n\nwith psycopg.connect(DSN) as conn:\n    while True:\n        if not run_one(conn):\n            time.sleep(1)                  # idle poll; LISTEN/NOTIFY can wake workers instead\n```\n\n`load_chunks`, `embed`, `vectors`, `RetryableError` and `DSN` belong to the application. A partial index\non `(run_at) WHERE state IN ('queued','running')` keeps the claim fast as the table grows. The\n`attempts < 5` guard on orphan reclaim matters because a job that kills its worker (an out-of-memory\ncrash on one huge document) never reaches the `except` branches; without the guard it is reclaimed\nforever, and a small periodic sweeper should move such rows to `dead`.","tradeoffs":["A job table in the main database gains transactional enqueue and zero new infrastructure, but pays polling and row churn; it flips toward Redis or a broker when throughput reaches (as an order of magnitude) thousands of jobs per second, or when hundreds of idle workers poll.","Redis-backed queues gain blocking pops and millisecond pickup, but lose jobs if persistence is off or lagging; acceptable for cache warming, unacceptable for billing or customer-visible work.","A long lease simplifies the worker, but delays recovery after a crash; a short lease with heartbeats recovers fast but needs a maximum-duration guard so a stuck worker cannot renew forever.","Retrying every failure is easy to write, but hides permanent errors behind five slow attempts; classifying errors costs code and flips to worthwhile the moment a model provider returns 4xx.","Restarting a failed multi-step job is simple, but repeats side effects and wasted minutes; checkpointing adds state to manage and pays off once jobs exceed a few minutes or several steps.","A durable execution engine gains resume-from-step and full history, but costs a runtime to operate and a programming model to learn; a table and a loop are enough until workflows become long and branching."],"pitfalls":["Using in-process background tasks for work that matters; the symptom is jobs that vanish after every deploy, discovered weeks later by a customer rather than by an alert.","A non-idempotent handler; the symptom is duplicate emails, double charges or two embeddings per chunk after a worker is OOM-killed or a lease expires.","Enqueueing outside the business transaction; the symptom is jobs that fail on \"row not found\" because the insert was rolled back, or committed changes with no job because the process died first.","A lease shorter than the job and no heartbeat; the symptom is the same job id running on two workers and `attempts` climbing with no recorded failure.","No maximum attempts or dead state; the symptom is one poison job retried forever, a worker permanently busy and a backlog growing behind it.","One shared run-order queue for all tenants; the symptom is a bulk import by one customer turning every other customer's two-second job into a two-hour wait, with workers fully busy and no errors.","Storing large payloads in the job row; the symptom is a bloated table, slow claims and a database that has become a blob store.","Never archiving finished rows and no partial index; the symptom is a claim query that gets slower every week even though the backlog is small.","Assuming the library acknowledges after the work; some default to acknowledging before execution (Celery's `acks_late` is off by default), and the symptom is lost jobs on worker crash despite a \"durable\" broker."],"when":"Reach for a durable worker whenever work must survive the request: anything longer than a few seconds,\nanything with an external side effect that must happen exactly once from the user's view, anything that\nneeds retry, scheduling or a status the caller can poll. Stay inline for fast reads the user is waiting\non, and use in-process tasks only for best-effort work you can afford to lose, such as a metrics sample.\nStart with a table in the database you already run and move to a broker or an execution engine when\nthroughput, fan-out or workflow length demands it. Measure oldest-job age and dead count from day one.\nDay to day this shows up as \"should this endpoint enqueue or call\", choosing lease length against real\nLLM latency, and deciding what is retryable. In interviews, expect to design the job table, explain\n`SKIP LOCKED` and leases, and justify idempotency.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the claim query, idempotent handlers and error classification, sizes leases to model latency, and adds the partial index and archival job before the table grows.","architect":"Decides where job state lives (database, Redis, broker, execution engine), the retry and dead-letter policy, and how callers learn the status of asynchronous work.","fde":"Fits the pipeline to the customer's deploy cadence and infrastructure, proves that a mid-run deploy loses nothing, and explains duplicate-safe design when their downstream systems are not idempotent."},"check":{"q":"Your API accepts document uploads that take two to twenty minutes to chunk and embed, and the\nteam deploys five times a day. Design the job pipeline so a deploy in the middle of an embedding never\nloses or duplicates work, and explain each mechanism's role.","a":"Make the job a row inserted in the same transaction as the upload record, so a job exists exactly\nwhen a document does. Workers claim with an atomic update using `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` that sets a\nlease; on deploy, workers receive SIGTERM, stop claiming and either finish or let the lease expire, after\nwhich a new worker reclaims the row, so nothing is lost. Guard every completion update with the lease\nholder's id so a pre-empted worker cannot overwrite the new holder's result. Duplication is handled inside the handler: each\nchunk's vector is upserted by chunk id and skipped if present, so a re-run after an expired lease resumes\nrather than repeats, and the twenty-minute job costs only the unfinished chunks. Heartbeat the lease\nwhile embedding so a slow but healthy worker is not pre-empted, with a maximum duration as a guard.\nClassify provider errors: 429 and 5xx reschedule with jittered backoff, 4xx go to `dead`. Alert on the age\nof the oldest queued job and the dead count."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a durable worker claim a job with an expiring lease instead of holding a row lock for the duration of the job?","options":["Row locks are not supported for UPDATE statements in most databases","A lease expires if the worker dies, letting another worker reclaim the job, whereas a lock would require keeping a transaction and connection open for the whole job","Leases allow several workers to run the same job in parallel for speed","Locks cannot be combined with SKIP LOCKED"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Crash recovery depends on the claim timing out. A transaction-long lock either pins a database connection for minutes or, if the worker dies, disappears without any record of what was in progress."},{"q":"A worker finishes sending an email through a provider, then crashes before marking the job succeeded. What happens next in a correctly built durable system?","options":["The job is marked failed because the worker's connection dropped","The broker detects the completed side effect and marks the job done","The lease expires, another worker re-runs the job, and the handler's idempotency key stops the provider from sending a second email","The job is lost, which is why email should be sent inline"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Side effect and acknowledgement cannot be made atomic, so re-running is the only safe default. The handler must therefore be idempotent, for example by passing a key the provider deduplicates on."},{"q":"Which enqueue strategy guarantees that a job exists if and only if the business change was committed?","options":["Push to Redis first, then commit the database transaction","Commit the transaction, then push to the queue inside a try/except","Insert the job row in the same database transaction as the business change, or write it to an outbox table relayed to the queue","Enqueue from a background thread after the response is sent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only a write in the same transaction is atomic with the business change. Any order of two separate writes can be interrupted between them, producing either an orphan job or a change with no job."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","sources":["OWASP, 2021 — Top 10: A01 Broken Access Control and A07 Identification and Authentication Failures","OWASP Cheat Sheet Series — Authentication and Authorization Cheat Sheets","Hardt, 2012 — RFC 6749: The OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework","Pang et al., 2019 — Zanzibar: Google's Consistent, Global Authorization System"],"why":"The forcing problem is the second user. With one user, \"logged in\" and \"allowed\" are the same thing.\nWith two, a request for `/documents/42` from a logged-in user says nothing about whether document 42 is\ntheirs, and the moment the server stops asking, user A reads user B's data by editing a number in the\nURL. That single mistake, a valid identity treated as sufficient permission, is what OWASP ranks as the\ntop web application risk in its 2021 Top 10 (broken access control), and it is a staple finding of\npenetration tests.\n\nThe naive alternative is to fold the two together: check at login, then trust everything after. It\nfails because the questions have different inputs and change at different rates. Identity is\nestablished once per session from a credential; permission depends on the specific resource, the\naction, the tenant, the user's current role and sometimes the time of day, and must be re-evaluated on\nevery request because any of those can change between two calls. Folding them also pushes authorization\ninto the user interface (hide the button), which any client that is not your UI simply ignores.\n\nKeeping them separate buys three things. Several authentication methods (password, single sign-on, API\nkey, service identity) can feed one authorization model. Permission policy can change without touching\nlogin. And audit becomes possible: *who* did *what* is only answerable when the principal is explicit.\nFor AI systems the separation is sharper still: an agent calling tools acts *on behalf of* a user, so\nauthentication must say which user, and authorization must be evaluated per tool call against that\nuser's permissions rather than the agent's own, broad service account.","how":"### Authentication: credential to principal\nA credential is evidence: something the caller knows (password), has (hardware key, phone, signed\ntoken), or is (biometric). The authentication layer verifies the evidence and produces a **principal**,\na small object such as `{user_id, tenant_id, roles, auth_method, auth_time}`, attached to the request\ncontext. Everything downstream trusts that object and never re-examines the credential, which is why\nthis layer must be a single, well-tested chokepoint (middleware or a dependency), not code repeated in\neach handler.\n\nThe common mechanisms differ in what is verified and where state lives. Passwords are checked against a\nslow, salted hash (bcrypt, scrypt or Argon2) *because* a leaked database must not be crackable offline\nat GPU speed. After login the browser holds a **session** cookie: an opaque id looked up in a server-side\nstore, which makes revocation instant but requires every server to reach the store. Because the browser\nattaches cookies automatically, the cookie needs `HttpOnly` (script cannot read it, so an XSS bug cannot\nexfiltrate it), `Secure` (never sent over plain HTTP) and `SameSite=Lax` or `Strict` (not sent on most\ncross-site requests), and state-changing endpoints still need CSRF protection where `SameSite` alone does\nnot cover the case. Issue a fresh session id at login rather than upgrading the anonymous one, or an\nattacker who planted a known id before login inherits the authenticated session (session fixation). A\nsecond factor (TOTP, or a phishing-resistant WebAuthn key) raises the bar from \"knows the password\" to\n\"also holds the device\", which matters because reused and phished passwords are the common way in. API clients send a\n**bearer token**: either opaque (looked up like a session) or a self-contained **JWT**, which the server\nverifies locally with the issuer's public key. A JWT check must cover four things: signature, issuer,\naudience and expiry. Signature verification includes pinning the accepted algorithm rather than trusting\nthe token's own `alg` header, which is how `none`-algorithm and key-confusion attacks get in. Audience is\nthe one people skip, and skipping it means a token minted for another service is accepted by yours. Because a JWT cannot be revoked before it expires without server state,\naccess tokens are short-lived and paired with a refresh token. **API keys** identify machines and are\nstored only as a hash. Unlike passwords they are long random strings, so a fast hash such as SHA-256 or\nan HMAC is enough (a slow password hash would add latency to every request for no gain); compare in\nconstant time, and give keys a recognisable prefix so secret scanners can spot a leaked key and you can\ntell which key it is without exposing the secret. Service-to-service calls use mutual TLS or platform\nidentities. Single sign-on delegates all of this to an identity provider through OIDC or SAML; the\nOAuth/OIDC lesson covers the flows.\n\n### Authorization: (principal, action, resource) to allow or deny\nAuthorization is a function of three inputs plus context, evaluated with **default deny**. The simplest\nform is an ownership check, `resource.owner_id == principal.user_id`, and its absence is the bug above.\nBeyond ownership there are three families. **RBAC** maps principals to roles and roles to permissions,\nand is what most products start with. **ABAC** evaluates policies over attributes of the principal, the\nresource and the environment (\"analysts may read documents whose classification is at most internal,\nfrom the corporate network\"). **ReBAC** answers through relationships (\"user is an editor of this\ndocument via membership of this group\"), which is the Zanzibar model behind systems such as OpenFGA and\nsuits deeply shared, hierarchical data.\n\nCollections need the same care as single resources. `GET /documents/42` is authorized by loading 42 and\nchecking it; `GET /documents?q=refund` must put the permission into the query itself\n(`WHERE tenant_id = $1 AND (owner_id = $2 OR ...)`), because fetching a page and filtering afterwards\nreturns short or empty pages, leaks totals through counts, and on a vector search lets disallowed chunks\ncrowd out allowed ones in the top-k.\n\nTwo placement rules matter more than the model. First, enforce on the server, as close to the data as\npossible, on every request; the UI hiding a button is not authorization. In multi-tenant systems every\nrow carries a `tenant_id`, every query filters by it, and database row-level security acts as a backstop\nfor the query someone forgets. Second, separate the **enforcement point** (the handler or middleware that\nasks) from the **decision point** (the library or policy service that answers: Casbin, OPA, Cedar,\nOpenFGA, or your own module), so policy is written once and testable in isolation.\n\nOAuth **scopes** are often mistaken for permissions. A scope bounds what a *client application* was\nallowed to request on the user's behalf; a permission is what the *user* actually holds. The effective\nright is the intersection: a `documents:write` scope on a token for a user who can only read grants\nnothing.\n\n### Order inside a request\nAuthenticate first, in middleware: invalid or missing credential, respond 401 with a `WWW-Authenticate`\nheader saying how to authenticate. Then, in the handler, load the resource and authorize: denied,\nrespond 403, or 404 when revealing that the resource exists would itself leak information (a document id\nbelonging to another tenant). Never reverse the order; an authorization check cannot run without a\nprincipal, and an unauthenticated caller must not learn which ids exist.\n\n### Agents are principals too\nAn agent runtime introduces a new principal (the agent, or the tool server) that acts for an existing one\n(the user). If the runtime calls tools with its own powerful credentials and relies on the prompt to stay\nwithin the user's permissions, it is a **confused deputy**: the model can be talked into fetching what\nthe user may not see. The fix is to carry the user's identity into every tool call (token exchange, or\npassing the principal to the tool) and let the tool's authorization layer decide, which also makes\nretrieval permission-aware. Human approval gates for consequential actions are authorization decisions\nmade at a different time, by a different decider.","example":"A FastAPI endpoint with the two concerns in two places. `current_principal` is the authentication\nchokepoint: it verifies signature, issuer, audience and expiry of a JWT via the identity provider's\npublished keys (PyJWT). PyJWT checks `exp` only if the claim is present, so `require` makes its absence\nan error too. `authorize` is the decision function: tenant match first (returning 404 so other\ntenants' ids are not discoverable), then ownership or an explicit permission.\n\n```python\nimport jwt                                   # PyJWT\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, HTTPException, Request\n\napp = FastAPI()\nJWKS = jwt.PyJWKClient(\"https://idp.example.com/.well-known/jwks.json\")\n\nclass Principal:\n    def __init__(self, claims):\n        self.user_id = claims[\"sub\"]\n        self.tenant_id = claims[\"tenant\"]\n        self.permissions = set(claims.get(\"permissions\", []))\n\ndef current_principal(request: Request) -> Principal:        # AUTHENTICATION\n    auth = request.headers.get(\"authorization\", \"\")\n    if not auth.startswith(\"Bearer \"):\n        raise HTTPException(401, headers={\"WWW-Authenticate\": \"Bearer\"})\n    token = auth.removeprefix(\"Bearer \")\n    try:\n        key = JWKS.get_signing_key_from_jwt(token).key\n        claims = jwt.decode(token, key, algorithms=[\"RS256\"],     # pinned, never from the header\n                            issuer=\"https://idp.example.com\", audience=\"docs-api\",\n                            options={\"require\": [\"exp\", \"sub\", \"tenant\"]})\n    except jwt.PyJWTError:                                   # bad signature, issuer, audience or expiry\n        raise HTTPException(401, headers={\"WWW-Authenticate\": \"Bearer\"})\n    return Principal(claims)\n\ndef authorize(p: Principal, action: str, doc) -> None:       # AUTHORIZATION, default deny\n    if doc.tenant_id != p.tenant_id:\n        raise HTTPException(404)                             # do not reveal cross-tenant existence\n    if doc.owner_id == p.user_id or f\"documents:{action}\" in p.permissions:\n        return\n    raise HTTPException(403)\n\n@app.delete(\"/documents/{doc_id}\")\ndef delete_document(doc_id: int, p: Principal = Depends(current_principal)):\n    doc = db.get_document(doc_id)\n    if doc is None:\n        raise HTTPException(404)\n    authorize(p, \"delete\", doc)\n    db.delete_document(doc_id, actor=p.user_id)              # audit records who, not just what\n    return {\"deleted\": doc_id}\n```\n\n`db` is the application's data layer. Note what is *not* here: no permission logic in the token parsing,\nno identity logic in the handler, and the tenant check runs before any content-dependent decision.","tradeoffs":["Stateless JWTs avoid a store lookup per request, but cannot be revoked before expiry; the trade flips toward server-side sessions when you must log a user out instantly, as in offboarding or compromise.","A central policy service gives one consistent, auditable model across services, but adds latency and a dependency on every request; inline checks are fine until a second service needs the same rules.","Fine-grained permissions give precision and least privilege, but the policy surface grows and becomes hard to review; coarse roles are enough until customers demand custom roles or per-resource sharing.","Returning 404 instead of 403 for cross-tenant resources hides existence from attackers, but confuses your own debugging and support unless the server logs the real reason.","Long-lived API keys are simple for machine clients, but never expire on their own; the trade flips toward short-lived, scoped tokens once keys are shared across customer systems or appear in logs."],"pitfalls":["Insecure direct object reference: authentication present, ownership check absent; the symptom is that changing an id in a URL returns another user's record, and it is the first thing a pen tester tries.","Skipping the audience check on JWTs; the symptom is a token issued for one API being accepted by another, so a compromise of a low-value service unlocks a high-value one.","Authorization only in the front end; the symptom is that `curl` with a valid token bypasses every restriction the UI shows.","A single agent service account holding the union of all users' permissions; the symptom is a user asking the agent for a document they cannot open in the app and receiving it.","Caching authorization decisions beyond revocation; the symptom is a removed employee or deleted share still working minutes or hours later.","Treating \"valid API key\" as \"known tenant\" without binding keys to tenants and filtering every query; the symptom is cross-tenant data appearing in search or RAG results.","Storing API keys or passwords in plaintext, or passwords under a fast unsalted hash; the symptom is that one database dump exposes every customer's credentials.","Returning 403 for an unauthenticated request; the symptom is clients that never retry with credentials because the status told them they were already identified."],"when":"Every multi-user system needs both, so the decisions are about shape. Use server-side sessions for\nbrowser logins where instant logout matters; use short-lived JWTs with refresh tokens for APIs and\nmobile clients; use hashed, prefixed, tenant-bound API keys for machine access; delegate login to an\nidentity provider as soon as an enterprise customer appears. Start authorization with ownership plus\nRBAC, and introduce ABAC or ReBAC only when sharing rules or attribute policies outgrow roles. Measure\n401 and 403 rates per endpoint, failed-login rates per account, and authorization decision latency. In\ndaily work this is the dependency that produces the principal and the check at the top of every\nmutating handler. In interviews, expect to explain 401 versus 403, design the checks for a multi-tenant\nendpoint, and describe how an agent should act on behalf of a user without a confused-deputy hole.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the authentication dependency once, verifies all four JWT properties, writes a default-deny `authorize` function with tenant and ownership checks, and tests it with another tenant's ids.","architect":"Owns the identity and permission model: session versus token, where policy is decided, how tenant isolation is enforced at the data layer, and how agents carry user identity into tools.","fde":"Maps the customer's identity provider, groups and roles onto the product's principal and permission model, and proves to their security team that retrieval and tool calls respect existing ACLs."},"check":{"q":"An agent platform executes tools with one service account that can read every customer's\ndocuments, and the system prompt instructs the model to \"only access the current user's documents\". The\ncustomer's security team asks whether this is acceptable. What do you tell them and what would you change?","a":"It is not acceptable, because the prompt is doing authorization and a prompt is not a security\nboundary: the model can be induced through injection or error to request another user's document, and\nthe service account will be allowed to fetch it. Authentication is fine (the user is known at the API\nedge) but that identity is dropped before the tool call, which is the confused-deputy pattern. The fix\nis to carry the user's principal into every tool invocation, by token exchange for a user-scoped token\nor by passing the principal explicitly, and to have the document service authorize each read against\nthat user's tenant and permissions with default deny. Retrieval should filter by the user's ACLs at\nquery time, and the agent's own account should hold only the minimal rights it needs for itself. Audit\nlogs then record the user, not the agent, as the actor."},"quiz":[{"q":"A request arrives with no credential at all. Which response is correct and why?","options":["403 Forbidden, because the caller is not allowed to access the resource","401 Unauthorized with a WWW-Authenticate header, because the server cannot establish who is calling","404 Not Found, to avoid revealing that the endpoint exists","400 Bad Request, because the Authorization header is required"],"answer":1,"explanation":"401 signals an authentication problem and tells the client how to supply credentials. 403 would claim identity was established and permission denied, which is false and stops clients from retrying with a token."},{"q":"A JWT presented to your API has a valid signature from your identity provider and has not expired, but its audience claim names a different service. Should it be accepted?","options":["Yes, because the signature proves the identity provider issued it","Yes, provided the subject exists in your user table","No, because a token minted for another service must not grant access to yours, even from the same issuer","Only if it also carries the correct scopes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Signature and expiry prove origin and freshness, not intended recipient. Accepting tokens regardless of audience lets a compromise of any service that shares the issuer become a compromise of all of them."},{"q":"A logged-in user changes the id in `/invoices/118` to `/invoices/119` and sees another customer's invoice. Which layer failed?","options":["Authentication, because the session should have been bound to the invoice","Authorization, because the handler never checked that the principal may read that resource","Transport security, because the id was visible in the URL","Rate limiting, because enumeration of ids should have been blocked"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The user was correctly identified; the server then assumed identity implied permission. This is an insecure direct object reference, the classic broken-access-control bug, fixed by an ownership or tenant check."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","sources":["Hardt (ed.), 2012 — The OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework (RFC 6749)","Sakimura et al., 2014 — OpenID Connect Core 1.0","Sakimura, Bradley, Agarwal, 2015 — Proof Key for Code Exchange by OAuth Public Clients (RFC 7636)","Lodderstedt, Bradley, Labunets, Fett, 2025 — Best Current Practice for OAuth 2.0 Security (RFC 9700)"],"why":"Before OAuth, a third-party app that wanted to read your calendar asked for your Google password. That\nhands over everything, not just the calendar; it cannot be revoked per app (only by changing the\npassword, which breaks every app at once); it stops working the moment the account has MFA; and it\nmeans dozens of apps store a credential that unlocks the whole account. The naive fix, a static per-user\nAPI key, removes password sharing but keeps the rest: keys are long-lived, rarely scoped, hard to\nrotate, and the user still copies a secret between systems by hand.\n\nOAuth 2.0 (2012) restructured the problem into four roles: the **resource owner** (the user), the\n**client** (the app), the **authorization server** (which authenticates the user, records consent and\nissues tokens) and the **resource server** (the API, which accepts tokens). The user authenticates only\nto the authorization server. The client receives a token that is scoped (`calendar.readonly`),\nshort-lived (minutes to an hour), revocable, and tied to that one client.\n\nThen people started using OAuth for login: \"if the app can call the profile API with this token, the\nuser must be who the profile says\". That inference is wrong. An access token proves the *client was\nauthorized*, not *who is present at the keyboard*. An attacker can obtain a perfectly valid access token\nfor a victim through a malicious app and replay it against another app that trusts the profile endpoint\n(token substitution). OIDC (2014) exists to close exactly that hole: it defines an **ID token**, a JWT\nsigned by the identity provider with the client's own id in the audience and a nonce bound to this login\nattempt, so authentication becomes something you verify rather than something you infer.","how":"### Two channels, one principle\nThe **front channel** is the user's browser: untrusted, logged, visible in history and referrers. The\n**back channel** is a TLS connection from the client's server to the authorization server. The design\nrule that explains most of the protocol is *nothing valuable travels the front channel*. The browser\nonly ever carries a short-lived, single-use authorization code; tokens move on the back channel.\n\n### Authorization code flow with PKCE, step by step\n1. The client generates three random values: `state` (binds the callback to this browser session,\n   defeating login CSRF), `nonce` (OIDC: binds the ID token to this attempt) and a PKCE pair, a\n   `code_verifier` and `code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier))`. It stores all three in the\n   user's server-side session.\n2. It redirects the browser to the `/authorize` endpoint with `response_type=code`, `client_id`,\n   `redirect_uri`, `scope=openid profile email …`, `state`, `nonce`, `code_challenge` and\n   `code_challenge_method=S256`.\n3. The authorization server authenticates the user however it likes (password, passkey, MFA, corporate\n   SSO); the client sees none of it. It shows a consent screen listing the scopes.\n4. It redirects back to `redirect_uri?code=…&state=…`. The client checks `state` matches the session.\n5. **Back channel.** The client POSTs `code`, `redirect_uri`, `code_verifier` and, for a confidential\n   client, its `client_secret` to `/token`. The server checks the code is unused and unexpired, that\n   `SHA256(code_verifier)` equals the challenge from step 2, and that `redirect_uri` is identical to the\n   one sent in step 2 (which `/authorize` already matched exactly against the registered list; exact\n   matching matters *because* the redirect URI is where the code is delivered, so a wildcard or prefix\n   match lets an attacker steer codes to a page they control). PKCE exists *because* public clients (SPAs, mobile apps, CLIs) cannot keep a secret and a\n   code can be intercepted (a malicious app registering the same custom URL scheme, a proxy log). Binding\n   the code to a value the interceptor never saw makes the stolen code worthless. The IETF security\n   best current practice (RFC 9700) makes PKCE mandatory for public clients and recommends it for\n   confidential clients too, since it also stops code injection.\n6. The response carries `access_token`, `token_type`, `expires_in`, optionally `refresh_token`, and,\n   when the `openid` scope was requested, `id_token`.\n\n### Three tokens, three audiences\n- **Access token.** For the resource server; its audience is the API. It may be opaque or a JWT, and\n  the client must treat it as opaque either way. Sent as `Authorization: Bearer …`. The API validates it:\n  for a JWT, signature against the issuer's JWKS plus `iss`, `aud`, `exp` and the required scopes; for an\n  opaque token, a call to the introspection endpoint (RFC 7662).\n- **ID token.** For the client; always a JWT. Claims: `iss`, `sub` (the stable user identifier, unique\n  only *within* that issuer, so key your user table on the pair `(iss, sub)`, not on email, which can\n  change or be re-issued), `aud` (must equal your\n  `client_id`), `exp`, `iat`, plus `nonce` (present when the request sent one) and `auth_time` (when requested), and\n  optionally `email`, `name`. Never send it to an\n  API as a credential; its audience is you.\n- **Refresh token.** Lets the client get fresh access tokens without the user present. It is the\n  longest-lived secret in the system and therefore the most valuable to steal. Mitigations: rotation\n  (each use returns a new one and reuse of an old one revokes the whole family), sender-constraining\n  (DPoP or mTLS), and keeping it server-side only.\n\n### Discovery and key rotation\nAn OIDC provider publishes `/.well-known/openid-configuration` listing its endpoints and a `jwks_uri`.\nThe JWKS holds public signing keys with a `kid`. Clients cache the set and refetch on an unknown `kid`,\nwhich is how a provider rotates signing keys without breaking anyone.\n\n### The other grants\n- **Client credentials.** No user. A service authenticates as itself (secret, private-key JWT or mTLS)\n  and gets a token carrying its own identity. Use it for batch jobs and for agents acting as themselves.\n- **Device code.** For TVs and CLIs that cannot host a browser: the device polls while the user\n  approves on a phone.\n- **Token exchange (RFC 8693).** A service holding a user's token trades it at the authorization server\n  for a new token with a different audience and usually narrower scope (\"on-behalf-of\"). This is the\n  clean way for an API to call a downstream API, or for an agent's tool runtime to obtain a token for\n  one tool, *because* forwarding the original token sends a credential whose audience is wrong and\n  whose scope is wider than the downstream call needs.\n- **Deprecated.** The implicit grant (token in the URL fragment, front channel) and the resource-owner\n  password grant (the client sees the password) are both removed in the OAuth 2.1 draft. Do not build\n  anything new on them.\n\n### Scopes are not permissions\nA scope is the upper bound the *user* granted the *client*. The resource server still applies the user's\nown authorization underneath: `scope=repo` never lets an app delete a repository the user could not\ndelete themselves. Both checks run on every request (see RBAC).","example":"A confidential web client (server-side) doing the authorization code flow with PKCE and validating\nthe ID token against the provider's JWKS. The pieces that matter are the three random values, the\nback-channel exchange, and the claims that are checked before trusting `sub`.\n\n```python\nimport base64, hashlib, secrets, urllib.parse, requests, jwt   # pip install requests pyjwt[crypto]\n\nISSUER, CLIENT_ID, CLIENT_SECRET = \"https://login.example.com\", \"my-web-app\", \"<from secrets manager>\"\nREDIRECT_URI = \"https://app.example.com/callback\"\ndisc = requests.get(f\"{ISSUER}/.well-known/openid-configuration\").json()   # endpoints + jwks_uri\njwks = jwt.PyJWKClient(disc[\"jwks_uri\"])   # caches keys; refetches when it meets an unknown kid\n\ndef start_login(session):            # 1. PKCE pair, state, nonce -> stored server-side, never in the URL\n    verifier = secrets.token_urlsafe(64)\n    challenge = base64.urlsafe_b64encode(hashlib.sha256(verifier.encode()).digest()).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n    session.update(verifier=verifier, state=secrets.token_urlsafe(16), nonce=secrets.token_urlsafe(16))\n    return disc[\"authorization_endpoint\"] + \"?\" + urllib.parse.urlencode({\n        \"response_type\": \"code\", \"client_id\": CLIENT_ID, \"redirect_uri\": REDIRECT_URI,\n        \"scope\": \"openid profile email\", \"state\": session[\"state\"], \"nonce\": session[\"nonce\"],\n        \"code_challenge\": challenge, \"code_challenge_method\": \"S256\"})\n\ndef handle_callback(code, returned_state, session):   # 2. browser returns ?code=...&state=...\n    if returned_state != session[\"state\"]:\n        raise PermissionError(\"state mismatch: login CSRF or stale session\")\n    tok = requests.post(disc[\"token_endpoint\"], data={             # back channel, TLS, server-to-server\n        \"grant_type\": \"authorization_code\", \"code\": code, \"redirect_uri\": REDIRECT_URI,\n        \"client_id\": CLIENT_ID, \"client_secret\": CLIENT_SECRET,\n        \"code_verifier\": session[\"verifier\"]})\n    tok.raise_for_status(); tok = tok.json()                   # error responses carry no tokens\n    # 3. Validate the ID token: signature via JWKS, pinned algorithm, issuer, audience = our client_id, expiry\n    key = jwks.get_signing_key_from_jwt(tok[\"id_token\"])\n    claims = jwt.decode(tok[\"id_token\"], key.key, algorithms=[\"RS256\"],\n                        audience=CLIENT_ID, issuer=disc[\"issuer\"])\n    if claims.get(\"nonce\") != session[\"nonce\"]:\n        raise PermissionError(\"nonce mismatch: token not minted for this login\")\n    user_key = (claims[\"iss\"], claims[\"sub\"])     # sub is unique per issuer; email is a display attribute\n    return user_key, tok[\"access_token\"], tok.get(\"refresh_token\")   # keep tokens server-side\n```\n\nAfter `handle_callback` the app issues its own HttpOnly session cookie; the access token is used only\nwhen the backend calls the provider's APIs on the user's behalf, and the refresh token never leaves\nthe server.","tradeoffs":["Delegation without password sharing, paid for with redirect complexity, more moving parts and an authorization server on your login path; it flips to \"a session cookie is enough\" for an internal tool that talks only to its own backend and never to third parties.","JWT access tokens let a resource server validate locally with no network call, but cannot be revoked before `exp` without adding a denylist lookup, which gives back the network call; opaque tokens with introspection revoke instantly at the cost of a call (or short cache) per request. Choose by how long a stolen token may stay valid.","Short-lived access tokens shrink the damage from a leak, but move the risk onto the refresh token and the session store; without rotation and server-side storage you have only relocated the problem.","An external identity provider (Okta, Entra ID, Google, Auth0) takes passwords, MFA and recovery out of your codebase, but adds a vendor on the critical path and provider-specific quirks in claim names and refresh policies. For B2B customers SSO is a requirement, so this is rarely a real choice."],"pitfalls":["Treating an access token as proof of identity by calling `/userinfo` and trusting the result; the symptom is that a token issued to app A logs the same user into app B. Require an ID token and check `aud` and `nonce`.","Omitting or not checking `state`; the symptom is login CSRF, where a victim's browser completes the attacker's login and the victim then uploads data into the attacker's account.","Keying users on `email` instead of `(iss, sub)`; the symptom is duplicate accounts after a rename, or account takeover at providers that re-issue addresses, or when a second, multi-tenant issuer lets anyone assert an email they do not own.","Registering wildcard or prefix-matched redirect URIs (\"any path under app.example.com\"); the symptom is codes delivered to an open-redirect or user-content page on your own domain, from where an attacker reads them out of the URL. Register exact URIs only.","Decoding the JWT without pinning `algorithms`, `audience` and `issuer`; the symptom is that any token from the same provider for any other app is accepted, or that an `alg: none` token passes.","Storing tokens in browser `localStorage`; the symptom is that one XSS exfiltrates every user's tokens. Use a backend-for-frontend with an HttpOnly session cookie and keep tokens server-side.","Handing an agent or integration a broad, long-lived refresh token \"to be safe\"; the symptom is one compromised worker that can act as every user. Request minimal scopes, and use client credentials for work the agent does as itself."],"when":"Reach for OIDC whenever humans log in and you do not want to own passwords, MFA and account recovery,\nwhich for B2B products is nearly always: \"do you support SSO\" appears in the first security\nquestionnaire. Reach for the OAuth authorization code grant when your product must call APIs as the user\n(Google Drive, GitHub, Slack, a CRM for an agent), and for client credentials when a service calls\nanother service. Skip it for a single internal app on a session cookie, and never use it as a substitute\nfor \"the cron job needs a credential\". Day to day the work is token validation middleware, mapping\nprovider claims to your roles, token storage and rotation, and provider quirks. In interviews, be able to\ndraw the code flow, explain why the exchange happens on the back channel, what PKCE prevents, and what\neach of the three tokens is for and who validates it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the callback, validates ID tokens against the JWKS, stores refresh tokens server-side, and writes the bearer middleware that checks issuer, audience, expiry and scope on every API request.","architect":"Decides client types and grants per surface (backend-for-frontend for SPAs, client credentials for workers, delegated user tokens for agents), picks JWT versus opaque access tokens by revocation needs, and owns the identity-provider boundary and the claim-to-role mapping.","fde":"Wires the product into the customer's Okta or Entra tenant, debugs redirect-URI and claim mismatches, gets the agent's tool integrations consented with minimal scopes, and explains why the agent acts with the user's token rather than a shared admin key."},"check":{"q":"A single-page app logs users in with Google by obtaining an access token in the browser, calling\nthe userinfo endpoint, and posting the returned email to the backend, which trusts it. What is wrong,\nand how would you redesign it?","a":"Three independent failures. First, identity is inferred from an access token: a token minted for\nany other app for the same user, or an attacker's own token, is accepted, because nothing ties it to this\nclient; the fix is to require an ID token and validate signature, `iss`, `aud` equal to your client id,\n`exp` and `nonce`. Second, tokens live in JavaScript, so a single XSS steals them. Third, the backend\ntrusts a client-asserted email. Redesign as a backend-for-frontend: the browser hits `/login`, the server\nbuilds the authorize URL with `state`, `nonce` and PKCE, handles the callback on the back channel,\nvalidates the ID token, keys the user on `(iss, sub)`, and sets an HttpOnly session cookie. Access and refresh\ntokens stay server-side and are used only for calls to Google on the user's behalf, with refresh-token\nrotation enabled."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the authorization code flow send a code through the browser and exchange it server-side instead of returning the access token directly?","options":["Codes are shorter than tokens and fit more reliably in a redirect URL","The browser is an untrusted channel; a single-use code bound to PKCE or a client secret is useless if intercepted, whereas a token would be directly usable","Access tokens can only be issued over a connection authenticated with mTLS","It lets the authorization server rate-limit how many tokens a client receives"],"answer":1,"explanation":"URLs leak through history, logs and referrers. A code that can only be redeemed with the verifier or secret the interceptor lacks limits the damage; the implicit grant that put tokens in the URL was deprecated for precisely this reason."},{"q":"A mobile app calls its own backend API and also needs to know who the user is. Which token goes where?","options":["The ID token to the API, and the access token to establish identity","The access token to the API, and the ID token to establish identity; each has a different audience","The refresh token to the API, and the ID token to establish identity","Either token works for both purposes because both are signed JWTs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The ID token's audience is the client, the access token's audience is the API. Sending the ID token to an API or using an access token as identity proof breaks the audience check that prevents token substitution between applications."},{"q":"What does PKCE protect against that a client secret alone cannot?","options":["Brute-force guessing of the user's password at the authorization server","Replay of an access token after it has expired","Interception of the authorization code by or on behalf of a public client that cannot hold a secret, and code injection generally","Forging the signature of an ID token"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A public client ships its code to users, so any embedded secret is public. PKCE binds the code to a per-request verifier that never travels the front channel, so an intercepted code cannot be redeemed; the IETF best-practice now recommends it for all clients."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/rbac","sources":["Sandhu, Coyne, Feinstein, Youman, 1996 — Role-Based Access Control Models","Ferraiolo, Sandhu, Gavrila, Kuhn, Chandramouli, 2001 — Proposed NIST Standard for Role-Based Access Control","Pang et al., 2019 — Zanzibar: Google's Consistent, Global Authorization System","Official docs: Kubernetes (Using RBAC Authorization)"],"why":"Authentication answers \"who is calling\"; every request after that has to answer \"may they do this\".\nThe obvious implementation is a direct grant: a table of (user, permission) rows, or an access-control\nlist on each resource naming the users allowed. It works for ten users and dies at a few hundred.\n\nDo the arithmetic. 2,000 employees and 300 distinct permissions (\"read invoices\", \"delete customer\",\n\"run eval job\") can produce up to 600,000 direct grants. Onboarding a support engineer means someone\ndeciding, grant by grant, which 40 of the 300 they need; when they move to another team, someone must\nremember to revoke those 40 and add 35 others. An auditor asking \"who can delete customer records?\"\ngets an answer only by scanning the whole table, and \"is this person's access appropriate?\" has no\nreference to compare against. Each grant is a bespoke decision, so the set drifts toward \"whatever\nwas needed once\" and never shrinks. This is how a departed contractor keeps production access.\n\nThe structure RBAC exploits is that organisations are made of jobs, and jobs need bundles of\npermissions. Name the bundle (the role), attach permissions to it once, and assign people to roles.\nAssignments drop from users × permissions to users → roles plus roles → permissions: with twelve roles\naveraging forty permissions that is 2,000 + 480 rows instead of up to 600,000, and both reviews become\ntractable. \"Is the editor role's permission set right?\" is a question one owner can answer; \"is Alice\nin the right roles?\" is a question her manager can answer.\n\nFor AI systems the stakes are sharper. An agent acting for a user must not be able to do more than\nthat user; a retrieval system must not surface chunks from documents the user cannot open; a tool\ncall must be checked at the moment it runs, not at login. All three need a decision that is cheap to\nevaluate on every request and every retrieved item, which is what a role lookup gives you.","how":"### The model\nFour sets and two relations. Subjects (human users, service accounts, agents), roles, permissions\n(an operation on an object type, written `documents:read`), and sessions, in which a subject activates\nsome of its roles. User assignment `UA ⊆ subjects × roles` and permission assignment `PA ⊆ roles ×\npermissions`. The decision is: allow `(u, p)` iff there exists a role `r` with `(u, r) ∈ UA` and\n`(r, p) ∈ PA`. Anything not explicitly allowed is denied, *because* a default-allow system cannot be\nreasoned about: every missing rule is a hole rather than a lock.\n\nThe NIST model layers refinements on this core. **Hierarchical** RBAC lets a senior role inherit a\njunior role's permissions (`admin ⊇ editor ⊇ viewer`), because without inheritance the admin's\npermission list duplicates the editor's and the two drift apart. **Constrained** RBAC adds separation\nof duty: nobody may hold both \"submit payment\" and \"approve payment\", because fraud should require\ncollusion. *Static* separation forbids the two roles being assigned to one person at all; *dynamic*\nseparation allows both assignments but forbids activating them in the same session, which is where the\nsession set earns its place. These exist as named levels because they are the two extensions every real\ndeployment reinvents.\n\nSessions also carry least privilege in time. An operator assigned `admin` should normally act as\n`viewer` and elevate for a bounded window, with a reason and an audit record, *because* standing\nprivilege is what a stolen session or a prompt-injected agent inherits. Cloud IAM systems add a second\nrefinement, explicit deny: a deny statement overrides any allow, so a guardrail (\"nobody outside the\nsecurity team may delete audit logs\") holds regardless of which roles are later granted. Order of\nevaluation is then deny, then allow, then the implicit default deny.\n\n### Scope: where a role applies\nFlat RBAC answers \"can this user read documents?\", but the production question is \"can this user read\n*this tenant's* documents?\". The fix is to make assignments three-part: (subject, role, scope), where\nscope is a node in a resource tree such as `org → project → resource`. A role granted at a scope\napplies to everything beneath it, because that is how delegation works in organisations: the org\nadmin administers every project. Kubernetes implements exactly this split: a `Role` and `RoleBinding`\nare namespaced, while `ClusterRole` and `ClusterRoleBinding` apply cluster-wide, and binding a\nClusterRole inside a namespace restricts it to that namespace.\n\n### Where the decision runs\nSeparate the *decision* (given subject, permission, scope, return allow/deny) from *enforcement*\n(middleware at the API boundary, a filter in a database query, a guard in front of a tool call). The\ndecision needs the caller's role assignments, and there are two places to get them:\n\n1. **From the token.** OIDC providers can put `groups` or `roles` claims in the ID or access token, so\n   the check is a local set lookup with no I/O. The cost is staleness: the token says what was true\n   when it was issued, so a revoked role stays usable until expiry. This is acceptable for coarse,\n   rarely-changing org roles with short token lifetimes.\n2. **From the authorization store.** Look the assignments up per request (cached with a short TTL).\n   One extra read, but revocation takes effect in seconds, and scoped assignments at project or\n   resource level, which would bloat a token, live naturally here.\n\nMost systems do both: coarse roles in the token, scoped assignments resolved server-side.\n\n### Enforce at every boundary, not just HTTP\nA permission check in the request handler does nothing for the retrieval query that runs inside it,\nthe background job that re-indexes documents, or the tool the agent calls. The same decision function\nhas to be applied as a row or chunk filter at data access and as a guard at tool invocation, and it\nmust run as the *end user's* identity, not as the service's. An agent that runs under a privileged\nservice account has effectively every role at once; a prompt injection then inherits all of them.\n\n### When roles are not enough\nRoles describe job functions, not relationships. \"The owner of a document can edit it\" or \"share this\nwith Bob\" cannot be expressed without a role per document, which is role explosion by another name.\nTwo extensions handle this. Attribute-based access control (ABAC) adds conditions on attributes of the\nsubject, resource and context: `resource.classification <= subject.clearance`, `request.time in\nbusiness_hours`. Relationship-based access control (ReBAC), of which Google's Zanzibar is the\ncanonical design, derives permissions from a graph of tuples such as `doc:7#viewer@group:eng#member`\nand is what sharing in Google Docs and Drive runs on. In practice you layer: RBAC for organisation-level\ncapabilities, ownership or relationship checks for per-object access, and ABAC conditions where the\ncustomer's policy demands them.","example":"A scoped, hierarchical RBAC check with permission-named guards. Roles inherit downward, assignments\ncarry a scope, and a role at any ancestor scope applies to the resource. `current_user` stands in for the\nauthentication dependency from the OAuth lesson. Two points carry the lesson: handlers name\n*permissions*, never roles, so adding a role touches one table instead of every endpoint; and the scope\nis derived from the resource's stored owner, never from the URL, because checking `project:{id}` from\nthe path while deleting `doc_id` lets an editor of project 42 delete project 7's documents by sending\n`/projects/42/documents/<a project-7 doc>` (an insecure direct object reference).\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, HTTPException\n\napp = FastAPI()\ndef current_user() -> str: return \"alice\"   # stub for the bearer-token auth dependency\nPERMS = {                                   # role -> permissions granted directly\n    \"viewer\": {\"documents:read\"},\n    \"editor\": {\"documents:write\"},\n    \"admin\":  {\"documents:delete\", \"members:manage\"},\n}\nINHERITS = {\"admin\": \"editor\", \"editor\": \"viewer\"}      # senior -> junior\n\ndef permissions_of(role: str) -> set[str]:\n    out: set[str] = set()\n    while role:                             # walk down the hierarchy\n        out |= PERMS[role]; role = INHERITS.get(role, \"\")\n    return out\n\n# (subject, scope) -> roles. Scopes form a tree: project -> org.\nASSIGNMENTS = {(\"alice\", \"org:acme\"): {\"viewer\"}, (\"alice\", \"project:42\"): {\"editor\"}}\nPARENT = {\"project:42\": \"org:acme\", \"project:7\": \"org:acme\"}\nDOC_SCOPE = {101: \"project:42\", 202: \"project:7\"}      # each document's real owning scope\n\ndef allowed(subject: str, perm: str, scope: str) -> bool:\n    while scope:                            # a role at any ancestor scope applies here\n        if any(perm in permissions_of(r) for r in ASSIGNMENTS.get((subject, scope), ())):\n            return True\n        scope = PARENT.get(scope, \"\")\n    return False                            # deny by default\n\ndef require_on_doc(perm: str):\n    def guard(doc_id: int, user: str = Depends(current_user)) -> str:\n        scope = DOC_SCOPE.get(doc_id)       # scope from the resource, not from the path\n        if scope is None or not allowed(user, perm, scope):\n            raise HTTPException(404)        # 404, not 403: do not confirm the doc exists\n        return user\n    return guard\n\n@app.delete(\"/documents/{doc_id}\")\ndef delete_doc(doc_id: int, user: str = Depends(require_on_doc(\"documents:delete\"))):\n    return {\"deleted_by\": user}\n\nassert allowed(\"alice\", \"documents:write\", \"project:42\")       # editor at the project\nassert allowed(\"alice\", \"documents:read\", \"project:7\")         # viewer inherited from the org\nassert not allowed(\"alice\", \"documents:delete\", \"project:42\")  # admin nowhere\nassert not allowed(\"alice\", \"documents:write\", DOC_SCOPE[202]) # doc 202 lives in project 7\n```","tradeoffs":["Indirection shrinks the assignment problem from users × permissions to user-role plus role-permission rows, but the role catalogue needs an owner; once roles are minted per team per exception (\"role explosion\") you are back to per-user lists with an extra hop.","Roles carried in the token make every check a local set lookup with no database round trip, but revocation lags by the token lifetime; the moment you need removal to bite within seconds (offboarding, a compromised account) the balance flips to a server-side lookup.","Hierarchies remove duplication and mirror the org chart, but a senior role silently inherits every new junior permission, which is how admins accumulate access nobody reviewed; for high-impact permissions (payments, deletion, key management) flat, explicitly granted roles are safer.","Coarse roles are simple to audit, but cannot express ownership or sharing; as soon as the product needs \"share this document with Bob\" you add a relationship layer and keep RBAC only for organisation-level capabilities."],"pitfalls":["Comparing role strings in code (`if user.role == \"admin\"`) instead of checking permissions; the symptom is that a new \"support\" role works nowhere until twenty endpoints are edited, and some are missed.","Forgetting scope, so an editor in one tenant can edit in every tenant; the symptom is a cross-tenant finding from a penetration test or, worse, a customer.","Taking the scope from the request (a path or body `project_id`) instead of from the stored resource; the symptom is that changing one ID in a URL reaches another project's object while every permission check still passes.","Enforcing only at the HTTP layer while the retrieval query or background job reads the index unfiltered; the symptom is the assistant quoting a document the user cannot open in the source system.","Running the agent under a privileged service account instead of the user's delegated roles; the symptom is a prompt-injected agent deleting or reading what the asking user never could.","Role explosion: hundreds of near-duplicate roles; the symptom is that \"who can do X?\" needs a script and quarterly access reviews are rubber-stamped.","Stale claims after a role change; the symptom is \"I removed their access an hour ago and they are still deleting things\", because the token has not expired."],"when":"RBAC is the default for any multi-user system with a stable set of job functions: admin panels,\nSaaS workspaces, Kubernetes, cloud IAM. Add ABAC conditions when decisions depend on attributes of the\nresource or context (classification, region, time of day) and ReBAC when the product has per-object\nsharing. Watch the ratio of roles to users (creeping up signals explosion), time-to-revoke, the share\nof checks that are permission-named rather than role-string comparisons, and whether every retrieval\nand tool call carries the end user's identity. Day to day you write `require(\"x:y\")` guards, map\nidentity-provider groups to roles and filter queries by scope. In interviews, explain users → roles →\npermissions with scope, the token-versus-store freshness trade, and name the case RBAC cannot express.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements permission-named guards as middleware or dependencies, stores scoped assignments, and applies the same decision as a chunk filter in retrieval and a check before each tool call.","architect":"Owns the role catalogue and scope hierarchy, decides what rides in the token versus the authorization service, and whether a central policy engine (OPA or a Zanzibar-style system) is justified.","fde":"Maps the customer's IdP groups from Okta or Entra ID to product roles, wires SCIM-driven assignment, and proves to their security team that the assistant cannot see what the user cannot."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants an AI assistant over a shared document store where every document has its own\nsharing list, and they also have org-level roles (admin, member, guest). Design authorization so the\nassistant never leaks a document.","a":"Separate two layers. Org-level RBAC handles coarse capabilities: who may use the assistant, who\nmanages members, which features guests get; carry these as roles in the token and check them with\npermission-named guards at the API boundary. Document access is per-object sharing, which is\nrelationship-based, so do not model it as roles. Record each document's allowed principals (users and\ngroups) as metadata on every indexed chunk at ingestion, resolve the caller's identity and group\nmemberships at request time, and apply them as a filter *inside* the vector or keyword query so\nunauthorized chunks never reach the prompt. Sync sharing changes incrementally and treat a stale ACL\nas a leak, so re-check the top-k hits against the source system when freshness matters. Run the\nassistant under the user's delegated identity, never a privileged service account, so a prompt\ninjection cannot widen its reach."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do permissions attach to roles rather than directly to users in RBAC?","options":["Because users cannot be identified reliably until after authorization has run","Because it replaces a users × permissions assignment problem with users → roles plus roles → permissions, which is far smaller to manage, review and revoke","Because roles are stored in the identity provider while permissions are stored in the application","Because direct permission grants cannot be represented as JWT claims"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The indirection collapses the number of assignments and makes reviews about a handful of roles. Where roles are stored is an implementation choice, and JWTs can carry any claim, so neither explains the design."},{"q":"You removed a user's only role in your application's authorization service ten minutes ago, but they can still call a protected endpoint. Access tokens live for an hour. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The role hierarchy still grants the permission through a junior role","Roles are carried in the access token, which has not expired, so the check is using stale claims","The permission check is scoped to the wrong tenant","The identity provider has not propagated the change through SCIM"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Token claims are a snapshot at issue time, so revocation lags by the token lifetime. With no other role left there is no junior role to inherit from, and SCIM carries changes from the identity provider into the app, whereas this change was made in the app's own store; neither touches a token that is already issued and valid."},{"q":"Which requirement is RBAC alone poorly suited to express?","options":["Administrators can manage the members of their own organisation","Viewers can read documents in the projects they are assigned to","A document's owner can edit it and share it with specific colleagues","Only staff in the billing role can issue refunds"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ownership and sharing are relationships between a subject and one object; expressing them with roles needs a role per document. The other three are role-at-scope statements that core or hierarchical RBAC handles directly."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","sources":["Official docs: HashiCorp Vault","Official docs: AWS Secrets Manager","Official docs: Kubernetes Secrets","OWASP — Secrets Management Cheat Sheet"],"why":"Secrets leak through boring paths: a `.env` committed \"just once\", an `ENV API_KEY=` line in a\nDockerfile, a build argument captured in image history, a key pasted into a ticket or a chat with an\nLLM, an exception trace that logs the request headers, a notebook checked into git, a CI log that echoed\nthe environment. For AI teams the stakes are specific: a model-provider key is a billing key, and keys\npushed to public repositories are routinely found and abused by scanners within minutes (illustrative;\nthe exact window varies), turning into unexpected bills, exhausted quotas and rate limits for your real\ntraffic. Unless you deliberately create narrower project- or workspace-scoped keys, a provider key often\nreaches a whole organisation's quota and data, so one leak exposes every project.\n\nThe naive answers each fail in a specific way. Environment variables are a delivery mechanism, not a\nstore: they are inherited by every child process, readable from `/proc/<pid>/environ`, and dumped into\ncrash reports. Encrypted files in the repository (sops, git-crypt) are a reasonable middle ground, but\nthe decryption key is itself a secret that must be bootstrapped somewhere, and rotation means\nre-encrypting in every environment. \"We will be careful\" fails at the first contractor laptop.\n\nThe deeper problem is rotation. If a secret has been copied into fourteen places, nobody can rotate it\nwithout an outage, so nobody does, and a three-year-old key sits in a dozen laptops. The first goal of\nsecrets management is therefore one source of truth referenced by name everywhere else, and the second\nis to need as few long-lived secrets as possible in the first place.","how":"### 1. Inventory and minimise\nList every credential and ask whether it needs to exist. Platform identity (an IAM role on the VM or\npod, a managed identity) replaces a static cloud key; OIDC federation replaces the cloud key stored in\nCI; short-lived tokens replace static API keys where the provider supports them. Every secret you do not\nhave is one you never rotate or leak.\n\n### 2. Store in a manager backed by a KMS\nVault, AWS Secrets Manager or Parameter Store, GCP Secret Manager and Azure Key Vault all do the same\ncore job: values are encrypted with a data key, the data key is encrypted by a root key the service\nnever exposes (envelope encryption; the cloud services hold it in an HSM-backed KMS, while Vault\nprotects it through its seal, which can itself be a cloud KMS or HSM), access is an IAM policy per\nidentity per secret path, recent versions are kept for rollback, and every read is audited. Kubernetes `Secret` objects are *not* this:\nthey are base64-encoded, stored in etcd unencrypted unless an `EncryptionConfiguration` with a KMS\nprovider is enabled, and readable by anyone with `get secrets` in the namespace, and in effect by\nanyone who can create a Pod there, since a Pod can mount any Secret in its namespace. Treat them as the last\nhop, populated from a real manager by the External Secrets Operator, the Secrets Store CSI driver or a\nVault agent sidecar.\n\nA key-management service (AWS KMS, Cloud KMS, Key Vault keys, Vault's transit engine) is a different\ntool from a secrets store, and the distinction matters for signing and encryption keys. A secrets\nstore *returns* the value to the caller; a KMS never does: the caller sends data and asks the service to\nsign, encrypt or decrypt it, and the key stays non-exportable inside the service. Use it for JWT\nsigning keys, webhook signing and field-level encryption, *because* a key that never enters application\nmemory cannot leak through a heap dump, a log line or a compromised dependency; the attacker who owns\nthe process can still call `sign` while they hold it, but loses that power the moment access is\nrevoked.\n\n### 3. Authenticate the workload: the secret-zero problem\nTo fetch secrets a workload needs a credential, and if that credential is a static key you have gained\nnothing. The way out is identity that the platform can attest without a stored secret: an EC2 instance\nprofile; on EKS, IAM Roles for Service Accounts (the pod's projected service-account token is\nexchanged for a role via OIDC federation) or EKS Pod Identity (a node agent exchanges the same token\nthrough the EKS Auth API); GKE Workload Identity; Azure managed identity; Vault's Kubernetes\nand JWT auth methods; and in CI, GitHub Actions' `id-token: write`, which lets a job present a short-lived\nOIDC token that a cloud role trusts for a specific repository and branch. Trust now lives in\nconfiguration (`sub = repo:org/app:ref:refs/heads/main`) instead of in a key, so keep those conditions\nnarrow.\n\n### 4. Deliver to the process\nThree options. Fetch through the SDK at startup: simple, needs network and identity, cache with a TTL.\nMount as files on a tmpfs volume: preferred on Kubernetes because the manager can rewrite the file and\nthe app re-reads it without a restart. Inject as environment variables: universal, but frozen for the\nlife of the process and leaky as above. Never bake into the image; `docker build --secret` mounts a value\nduring one build step without writing it to any layer.\n\n### 5. Rotate, and prefer dynamic\nStatic secrets rotate on a schedule and immediately after any suspected exposure, using two-key overlap,\n*because* an atomic swap across many consumers is impossible. For a database password the sequence is:\n(1) create the new credential at the database while the old one still works; (2) store it in the manager\nas the pending version; (3) verify a login with it; (4) promote it to current, so consumers pick it up on\ntheir next refresh; (5) wait at least one consumer refresh interval (the cache TTL, plus restart time for\nanything that reads only at boot); (6) revoke the old credential. AWS Secrets Manager models exactly this\nwith the staging labels `AWSPENDING`, `AWSCURRENT` and `AWSPREVIOUS`. Revoking at step 4 instead of 6 is\nthe classic rotation outage. Dynamic secrets remove the schedule: Vault's database engine creates a\nper-workload database user with a TTL and drops it on expiry; cloud STS tokens expire within hours.\nRotation only works if consumers re-read, so cache with a TTL and refetch on an authentication failure.\n\n### 6. Detect and contain\nPre-commit and CI scanning (gitleaks, trufflehog) and GitHub push protection catch the commit before it\nlands. A secret that reached git history is compromised even after the commit is removed: rotate it.\nRedact known secret values from logs, allowlist logged fields, and alert when a secret is read by an\nidentity that never read it before.\n\n### 7. Rules specific to AI systems\nUse one model-provider key per service and environment with a spend cap, kept server-side only and\nideally behind a gateway so rotation happens in one place and spend is attributable. Never place a secret\nin the prompt or in a tool result: a model can be induced to repeat its context, and traces in your\nobservability tool would store it. Tools should receive opaque references and resolve credentials in the\ntool runtime, outside the model's context. Agents that reach into customer systems hold per-customer\ncredentials isolated by tenant; the agent's own identity is never the customer's key.","example":"A small resolver that reads a secret from a mounted file when one exists (Vault agent or CSI driver),\notherwise from AWS Secrets Manager using the pod's or instance's ambient IAM identity, re-reads after a\nTTL so rotations take effect without restarts, and installs a logging filter that redacts the value.\n\n```python\nimport logging, os, time, boto3\nfrom requests import post\n\nclass Secrets:\n    \"\"\"No static credential anywhere: boto3 picks up the IAM role from the environment (IRSA / instance profile).\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, mount=\"/var/run/secrets/app\", ttl_s=300):\n        self.mount, self.ttl_s, self._cache = mount, ttl_s, {}\n        self._sm = boto3.client(\"secretsmanager\")\n\n    def get(self, name: str, force=False) -> str:\n        hit = self._cache.get(name)\n        if hit and not force and time.monotonic() - hit[1] < self.ttl_s:\n            return hit[0]\n        path = os.path.join(self.mount, name.replace(\"/\", \"_\"))\n        if os.path.exists(path):                                   # file mount rotates in place\n            value = open(path).read().strip()\n        else:                                                      # manager call, audited per identity\n            value = self._sm.get_secret_value(SecretId=name)[\"SecretString\"]\n        self._cache[name] = (value, time.monotonic())\n        return value\n\nclass Redact(logging.Filter):\n    def __init__(self, values): super().__init__(); self.values = [v for v in values if v]\n    def filter(self, record):\n        msg = record.getMessage()\n        if any(v in msg for v in self.values):\n            for v in self.values: msg = msg.replace(v, \"[REDACTED]\")\n            record.msg, record.args = msg, ()\n        return True\n\nsecrets = Secrets()\nLLM_KEY = secrets.get(\"prod/llm-gateway/api-key\")        # per-service key with a spend cap\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\nfor h in logging.getLogger().handlers:                   # on handlers: logger filters skip propagated records\n    h.addFilter(Redact([LLM_KEY]))                       # re-register with the new value after a rotation\n\ndef call_provider(payload):\n    key = secrets.get(\"prod/llm-gateway/api-key\")        # cheap: served from cache until ttl\n    r = post(\"https://llm-gateway.internal/v1/chat\", json=payload, headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\"})\n    if r.status_code == 401:                             # rotated under us: refetch once, then retry\n        key = secrets.get(\"prod/llm-gateway/api-key\", force=True)\n        r = post(\"https://llm-gateway.internal/v1/chat\", json=payload, headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\"})\n    return r\n```\n\nWhen the key is rotated in the manager, the file mount updates or the next TTL expiry refetches; the\n401 branch covers the window in between. Nothing in the repository, image or environment contains the\nvalue.","tradeoffs":["A central manager gives one place to rotate and audit but is a runtime dependency on startup; cache with a TTL and fail fast at boot. For a two-person project without infrastructure, encrypted files in the repo are an acceptable step up from nothing.","Dynamic, short-lived credentials shrink the blast radius but require every consumer to handle expiry and refresh, and create churn in database users; static secrets with scheduled rotation are simpler where consumers cannot refresh.","File mounts allow hot rotation but need the application to re-read; environment variables work with every runtime but are frozen for the process lifetime and inherited by children.","Platform identity removes secret zero but ties you to that platform's IAM model; in air-gapped or on-premises deployments you may need Vault with AppRole and a bootstrap token delivered out of band."],"pitfalls":["`ENV` or `ARG` with a secret in a Dockerfile; the symptom is that `docker history` or any pull of the image reveals it. Use build secrets.","A `.env` deleted from the working tree but still in git history; the symptom is a scanner alert or a surprise provider bill weeks later. Rotate, do not just rewrite history.","Logging whole request objects or headers; the symptom is `Authorization: Bearer …` lines in the log store. Allowlist fields and add redaction filters.","One shared production key used by twelve services; the symptom is that rotation causes an outage and spend cannot be attributed. Issue per-service keys.","Secrets placed in prompts, tool arguments or tool outputs; the symptom is the model echoing a key in chat or a trace containing it. Resolve credentials inside the tool runtime.","Consumers that read secrets only at startup; the symptom is authentication failures after every rotation until a restart. Re-read on a TTL and on 401.","Assuming Kubernetes Secrets are encrypted; the symptom is any namespace reader dumping them. Enable encryption at rest, restrict RBAC on secrets, and source them from a manager.","Mounting a Kubernetes Secret with `subPath`, or reading it through `env`; the symptom is that the manager and the Secret show the new value but the Pod keeps the old one until it restarts, because neither path receives in-place updates (whole-volume mounts do, after a short sync delay).","Revoking the old credential as soon as the new one is written; the symptom is a burst of authentication failures across every consumer whose cache has not yet refreshed."],"when":"There is no setting where this does not apply; what varies is the tooling. Day to day, adding a secret\nmeans creating it in the manager, referencing it by name in configuration, granting one identity access,\nand never pasting it anywhere. Measure the number of static long-lived secrets, the age of the oldest,\nthe time it takes to rotate one end to end, and scanner findings per month. In interviews, expect to\nexplain the secret-zero problem and how workload identity solves it, how to rotate without downtime, and,\nfor AI roles, where model-provider keys live, why they never reach the browser or the prompt, and how a\nprompt-injected agent could exfiltrate a credential that was left in its context.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Reads secrets through a resolver with TTL and refetch-on-401, adds log redaction, uses build secrets in Dockerfiles, and keeps provider keys out of tool arguments and traces.","architect":"Chooses the manager and the identity model per environment, defines the rotation policy and key-per-service boundaries, and sets the rule that credentials resolve in the tool runtime rather than in the model's context.","fde":"Fits the product into the customer's Vault or cloud KMS, gets workload identity granted in their cluster, and answers the security questionnaire's questions on storage, rotation and audit."},"check":{"q":"A deployment on Kubernetes stores the model-provider key in a Kubernetes Secret created by hand,\nreads it into an environment variable, and the same key is used by the API, the batch evaluator and a\ndeveloper's notebook. What are the risks, and how would you fix it in order of impact?","a":"First, the key is shared, so a leak from the notebook exposes production and rotation would\nbreak all three consumers at once; issue a per-service key with its own spend cap, kept behind a\ngateway. Second, the Kubernetes Secret is base64 in etcd, unencrypted unless at-rest encryption is\nconfigured, and readable by anyone with namespace access; source it from a managed secrets store through\nthe External Secrets Operator or CSI driver, enable etcd encryption, and restrict RBAC on secrets. Third,\nthe environment variable is frozen and inherited by child processes; mount it as a file and re-read on a\nTTL so rotation needs no restart. Fourth, no identity story exists for the workload; use IRSA or workload\nidentity so the pod authenticates to the manager without a stored credential. Finally, add scanning,\nredaction and a read-audit alert, and rotate the current key now because it has lived in a notebook."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the \"secret zero\" problem and what solves it?","options":["The first secret created in a vault is unencrypted until the vault is sealed; sealing solves it","A workload needs a credential to fetch its credentials; platform-attested identity (IAM roles, workload identity, OIDC federation) solves it without a stored secret","Environment variables are numbered from zero and the first one is visible to all users","The root token of a secrets manager cannot be rotated; a hardware module solves it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the bootstrap credential is a static key, the manager only relocates the problem. Identity that the platform asserts about the running workload breaks the chain, because nothing durable exists to steal."},{"q":"Why are Kubernetes Secret objects not a sufficient secrets store on their own?","options":["They can hold at most one value per object","They are only available to pods on the control-plane nodes","They are base64-encoded, unencrypted in etcd unless at-rest encryption is configured, and readable by any identity with namespace secret access","They cannot be mounted as files, only as environment variables"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Base64 is an encoding, not encryption. Without an EncryptionConfiguration and tight RBAC, a namespace reader or an etcd backup exposes every value; a managed store feeding Secrets fixes the storage and audit gap."},{"q":"An agent's tool for querying a customer's CRM needs an API token. Where should the token be handled?","options":["Passed to the model in the system prompt so it can include it in tool calls","Returned to the model inside the tool's JSON result for subsequent calls","Resolved inside the tool runtime from the secrets store, keyed by tenant, so it never enters the model's context","Stored in the conversation memory so multi-turn sessions can reuse it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Anything in the model's context can be echoed back through prompt injection or captured in traces. The tool runtime looks the credential up by the tenant and the tool, uses it, and returns only data."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/docker","sources":["Official docs: Docker","Open Container Initiative — Image Format Specification","Official docs: NVIDIA Container Toolkit"],"why":"A Python service depends on far more than its `requirements.txt`: the interpreter version, system\nlibraries such as glibc and OpenSSL, compiled wheels built against a specific CUDA version, locale\nsettings, environment variables. Installing all of that by hand or by script onto a VM drifts: two\nservers provisioned three months apart differ in ways nobody recorded, \"works on my machine\" is a\nliteral description of the problem, and rolling back means uninstalling. Virtual machines fix\nisolation, but a VM image is gigabytes with its own kernel, boots in tens of seconds to minutes,\nand running one per service wastes memory and makes dev/prod parity painful, so people share VMs\nand the drift returns.\n\nThe insight behind containers is that a process does not need its own kernel to be isolated. The Linux\nkernel already has namespaces (what a process can *see*: its own process tree, network interfaces,\nmount table) and cgroups (what it can *use*: CPU, memory, I/O). Add a filesystem built from a\ndeclarative recipe, make it immutable and addressable by content hash, and add a registry to move it\nbetween machines, and the artifact itself becomes the deployment unit: no install step on the target,\nno drift, rollback is pointing at the previous image.\n\nFor AI work this is the difference between a day of \"which torch wheel matches which CUDA\" and an\nimage that runs identically on a laptop GPU, a CI runner and a Kubernetes GPU node. The naive\nalternative, a virtualenv plus a setup script, pins Python packages but nothing beneath them, and the\nthings beneath them (CUDA libraries, system codecs, build toolchains) are exactly what breaks.","how":"### Images: layers and content addressing\nA Dockerfile is a sequence of instructions. Each `RUN`, `COPY` and `ADD` produces a layer: a tarball\nof the filesystem changes relative to the previous layer, identified by the hash of its contents. An\nimage is an ordered list of layer digests plus a config (entrypoint, environment, exposed ports,\nuser). Layers are immutable and shared: twenty services built on the same `python:3.12-slim` base hold\none copy of those layers on disk and in the registry, and a push uploads only layers the registry\nlacks. At run time a union filesystem (overlay2 on most hosts) stacks the layers read-only and adds a\nthin writable layer per container. Writes to that layer disappear with the container, which is *why*\ndurable state goes in volumes and not in the container filesystem.\n\nThe build cache follows from this. Each instruction's result is cached, keyed on the instruction and\nits inputs (for `COPY`, the content of the copied files). The first instruction whose inputs changed\ninvalidates every later layer, so ordering is a performance decision: copy the dependency manifest,\ninstall, then copy source, because source changes every commit and dependencies change every few\nweeks. Get it backwards and every commit reinstalls torch.\n\n### Containers: namespaces and cgroups\n`docker run` starts a process with its own PID namespace (it sees itself as PID 1), network namespace\n(own interfaces and port space), mount namespace (its root is the image), UTS and IPC namespaces, and\noptionally a user namespace. A cgroup caps CPU and memory: exceeding `--memory` triggers the kernel's\nOOM killer for that container, which exits with code 137 and no application traceback. By default a\ncontainer also drops most Linux capabilities and runs under a seccomp profile that filters syscalls.\nIt remains a normal process: `ps` on the host shows it, a kernel vulnerability escapes it, and it\nstarts in milliseconds. Isolation is therefore good enough for your own services and not enough for\nrunning untrusted code, which is why agent sandboxes use stronger boundaries such as gVisor or\nmicroVMs.\n\n### PID 1 and signals\nThe container's main process is PID 1 in its namespace, and the kernel treats PID 1 specially: default\nsignal dispositions do not apply, so a process without an explicit SIGTERM handler ignores\n`docker stop` and is SIGKILLed when the grace period (10 seconds by default) expires. Two consequences:\nuse the exec form `CMD [\"uvicorn\", ...]` rather than the shell form `CMD uvicorn ...`, because the\nshell form wraps the command in `/bin/sh -c`, and unless that shell happens to `exec` the command\n(behaviour varies by shell and command), the shell is PID 1 and does not forward the signal;\nand if the app spawns children, run under `--init` or `tini` so zombies are reaped. This plumbing is\nwhat makes graceful draining of an in-flight streaming response possible later in Kubernetes.\n\n### Tags, digests and registries\nA tag such as `:1.4.2` or `:latest` is a mutable pointer; the digest `sha256:…` is the immutable\nidentity. Deploy by digest or by an immutable tag like the git SHA, because `:latest` on two nodes can\nmean two different images.\n\n### Multi-stage builds\nA build stage with compilers, headers and dev tools produces artifacts; the final stage copies only\nthose artifacts into a slim runtime base. The toolchain never ships, the image shrinks, and the attack\nsurface with it.\n\n### GPUs\nContainers see no GPU by default. The NVIDIA Container Toolkit injects the device nodes and the host\ndriver's user-space libraries when you run with `--gpus all`. The split is: the image carries the CUDA\nruntime and torch, the host carries the kernel driver, and the host driver must be new enough for the\nCUDA version in the image. ML images are large (several gigabytes is typical, as an order of\nmagnitude), so base-image choice and layer ordering directly set pull time, which becomes cold-start\ntime when autoscaling.\n\n### Compose\n`docker compose` runs several containers (API, Redis, Postgres, a vector database) on one network with\nnamed volumes from a single file: the local development stack that mirrors production topology.\n\n### What \"Docker\" actually is, and the alternatives\n\"Docker\" names a stack, and the pieces are separable *because* they were standardised by the Open\nContainer Initiative. The `docker` CLI talks to the `dockerd` daemon; builds go through BuildKit; the\ndaemon hands containers to containerd, which uses runc to set up the namespaces and cgroups. The image\nformat and the runtime contract are OCI specifications, so an image built by Docker runs under any\nOCI runtime. Kubernetes nodes run containerd or CRI-O directly through the Container Runtime Interface\nand have not used Docker as the runtime since dockershim was removed (v1.24); Docker-built images run\nthere unchanged, but `docker ps` on a node shows nothing. Podman offers a largely compatible CLI\nwithout a central root daemon and is common where rootless operation is required; Buildah and kaniko\nbuild images without a daemon, which suits CI runners that must not expose a privileged Docker socket.\nThe choice rarely changes the Dockerfile; it changes who needs root on the build machine.","example":"A multi-stage Dockerfile for a FastAPI inference service using `uv`. Dependencies are installed from\nthe lockfile in their own layer before the source is copied, the runtime stage contains only the\nvirtualenv and code, the process runs as a non-root user, and the exec-form `CMD` makes uvicorn PID 1\nso it receives SIGTERM. Pin the `uv` image to a specific version in a real repository.\n\n```dockerfile\n# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\nFROM python:3.12-slim AS builder\nCOPY --from=ghcr.io/astral-sh/uv:latest /uv /bin/uv\nWORKDIR /app\n# Dependencies first: this layer is rebuilt only when the lockfile changes.\nCOPY pyproject.toml uv.lock ./\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.cache/uv \\\n    uv sync --frozen --no-dev --no-install-project\nCOPY src/ ./src/\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.cache/uv uv sync --frozen --no-dev\n\nFROM python:3.12-slim AS runtime\nRUN useradd --create-home --uid 10001 app\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=builder /app/.venv /app/.venv\nCOPY --from=builder /app/src /app/src\nENV PATH=\"/app/.venv/bin:$PATH\" PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1\nUSER app\nEXPOSE 8000\nHEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s \\\n  CMD python -c \"import urllib.request as u; u.urlopen('http://127.0.0.1:8000/health')\" || exit 1\n# Exec form: uvicorn is PID 1 and receives SIGTERM from `docker stop`.\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"src.app:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]\n```\n\nBuild and run it with an immutable tag and a memory cap; a `.dockerignore` listing `.git`, `.venv`,\n`data/` and model weights keeps the build context small and the `COPY` cache stable:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t llm-api:$(git rev-parse --short HEAD) .\ndocker run --rm -p 8000:8000 --memory=2g -e LLM_SECRET_ID=prod/llm llm-api:$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\ndocker history llm-api:$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)   # where did the bytes go?\n```","tradeoffs":["Images make deploys reproducible and rollback a pointer change, but a multi-gigabyte CUDA image takes minutes to pull onto a fresh node, so autoscaling latency becomes an image-size problem; the fix flips toward runtime bases, pre-pulled nodes and weights stored outside the image.","Sharing the host kernel gives near-native performance and millisecond startup compared with a VM, but weaker isolation; for code you did not write, such as agent-generated scripts, the balance flips to gVisor or microVM sandboxes.","Layer caching makes rebuilds fast when instructions are ordered well, but the cache only knows the inputs the Dockerfile shows it; an `apt-get update` cached from last month or a `pip install` without a lockfile yields different images on different days.","One container holding the API and the model is simple to run, but splitting the model server from the API adds a network hop in exchange for scaling and restarting the two independently."],"pitfalls":["Shell-form `CMD` or an application without a SIGTERM handler; the symptom is `docker stop` taking exactly ten seconds and in-flight requests dying, and later, rolling updates dropping streams.","Running as root; the symptom is every security review flagging it, and any code-execution bug becoming root inside the container. Add a `USER`.","No `.dockerignore`; the symptom is builds that send gigabytes of context (`.git`, `.venv`, datasets, weights) and miss the cache on every build because a `COPY` input changed.","Copying source before installing dependencies; the symptom is every one-line change reinstalling torch for several minutes.","A `:latest` base image or unpinned dependencies; the symptom is a rebuild of the \"same\" Dockerfile producing a different image that fails, usually on the day of an incident.","Writing state inside the container; the symptom is data vanishing on restart, plus slow writes through the overlay filesystem.","CUDA runtime newer than the host driver supports; the symptom is a \"driver version is insufficient for CUDA runtime version\" error on one node pool and not another.","A memory limit below real peak usage; the symptom is silent restarts with exit code 137 and no Python traceback, typically during model load or a large batch.","Mounting `/var/run/docker.sock` into a CI or agent container \"so it can build\"; the symptom is that anything running in that container can start a privileged container and own the host. Use a rootless or daemonless builder instead."],"when":"Containerize anything you deploy more than once or onto more than one machine: inference APIs,\nworkers, eval jobs, fine-tuning runs. Skip it for a notebook on your laptop, and note that serverless\nplatforms that accept source or a zip build the image for you. Measure image size, cached and uncached\nbuild time, pull time, and time from container start to ready, and watch each of them when a deploy\nfeels slow. Day to day you write Dockerfiles, debug with `docker exec`, `docker logs` and\n`docker history`, and run the local stack with Compose. In interviews, be ready to explain layers\nversus containers, namespaces versus cgroups, why PID 1 matters, and why instruction order changes\nbuild time.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the multi-stage Dockerfile, pins the base image and lockfile, handles signals for graceful shutdown, keeps the CUDA and torch versions consistent, and keeps the image small.","architect":"Decides what lives in the image versus mounted at runtime (weights, config), sets the base-image and registry-scanning policy, and chooses the isolation level for untrusted execution.","fde":"Ships the product as images into the customer's registry or air-gapped environment, and works around their driver versions, proxies and approved-base-image restrictions."},"check":{"q":"Your inference image is about 9 GB and each new node takes six minutes to become ready during\nautoscaling. Walk through what you would investigate and change.","a":"Start with `docker history` to see where the bytes are; it is usually CUDA toolkit layers, torch\nwheels and model weights copied into the image. In order of payoff: move the weights out of the image\nonto object storage or a persistent volume with a node-local cache, so the image is only code and\nruntime; switch to a CUDA runtime base instead of a devel base and use a multi-stage build so compilers\nnever ship; drop unused dependencies; and order layers so the heavy, stable ones (base plus\ndependencies) are shared across versions, so a node that pulled last week's image already has most of\nthis week's. Then attack the platform side: pre-pull images with a DaemonSet, keep a warm node pool,\nand use a registry in the same region. Finally separate pull time from model-load time in the\nmeasurements, because the second needs a startup probe and a weight cache rather than a smaller image."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should a Dockerfile copy the dependency manifest and install before copying the application source?","options":["Because the source files need the installed packages present at `COPY` time","Because the first changed layer invalidates every later cached layer, so putting rarely-changing dependency installation first lets most rebuilds skip it","Because Docker requires all `COPY` instructions to appear at the end of a build","Because installing dependencies after the source makes the final image larger"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cache is keyed on each instruction and its inputs, in order. Source changes on every commit, dependencies rarely, so installing first means a code change rebuilds only the cheap final layers. Swapping these two steps leaves the final image essentially the same size; only rebuild time changes."},{"q":"A container ignores `docker stop` and is killed after ten seconds. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The container's memory limit is set too low","The image was built without a `HEALTHCHECK`","The main process is PID 1 and does not handle SIGTERM, for example because `CMD` used shell form so `/bin/sh` received the signal","The container is running as root"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PID 1 has no default signal handlers, so SIGTERM is dropped unless the process installs one, and the shell form typically leaves a shell as PID 1 that does not forward it. Memory limits produce OOM kills, not stop timeouts, and health checks do not affect signal delivery."},{"q":"What does a container share with its host that a virtual machine does not?","options":["The root filesystem","The environment variables of the host shell","The kernel","The network namespace"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A container is a process isolated by namespaces and cgroups on the host's kernel, which is why it starts in milliseconds and why a kernel exploit escapes it. Its root filesystem comes from the image and it gets its own network namespace by default."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","sources":["Official docs: Kubernetes","Burns, Grant, Oppenheimer, Brewer, Wilkes, 2016 — Borg, Omega, and Kubernetes","Verma et al., 2015 — Large-scale cluster management at Google with Borg"],"why":"Docker gives you a reproducible unit. It does not answer the questions that appear the moment you have\nmore than one: which machine should run this container, given its CPU, memory and GPU needs; what\nhappens when it crashes at 3 a.m. or its node dies; how a new version replaces the old without\ndowntime; how service A finds the current IP of service B when replicas come and go; how to go from\ntwo replicas to twenty when traffic arrives; how configuration and secrets reach the process.\n\nThe naive answer is SSH plus `docker run` plus a hand-edited load balancer, or Compose on a few large\nhosts. It fails in a predictable order. Nothing reconciles: a dead container stays dead unless you\nwrote the watchdog yourself. The real state of the system lives in people's heads and shell history,\nso no one can say what is running where. Placement is manual, so expensive GPU nodes sit half empty\nwhile others are oversubscribed; bin-packing is what makes GPUs affordable, and humans do not bin-pack.\nEvery scaling action, update and rollback is a bespoke procedure that differs the second time it runs.\n\nGoogle's Borg solved this at scale with one idea: operators declare the desired state of jobs, and\ncontrollers converge the cluster toward it, forever. Kubernetes is the open-source generalisation of\nthat model. The important shift is from *commands* (\"start this here\") to *declarations* (\"this should\nexist\"), because declarations can be reconciled after failures, versioned in git, and reasoned about\nwithout knowing the current state of every machine.","how":"### Control plane and reconciliation\nThe API server is the only component that reads and writes etcd, where every object lives. Controllers\neach watch one kind of object and act to make reality match its spec. The scheduler assigns Pods that\nhave no node to a node whose free resources satisfy their requests. On every node, the kubelet starts\ncontainers through the container runtime and reports status; kube-proxy and the CNI plugin provide\nnetworking. When you `kubectl apply` a manifest, nothing executes a command: you changed desired state\nin the API server, and the controllers converge toward it, repeatedly. That is why a deleted Pod comes\nback and why a `docker run` on a node is invisible to the cluster.\n\nEvery controller is the same loop, run on every change event and on a periodic resync:\n\n```\nloop:\n    desired = read spec from API server        # e.g. replicas: 3, image: v2\n    actual  = observe status                   # e.g. 2 Pods running v1, 1 Pending\n    diff    = desired - actual\n    act on diff (create, delete, update)       # one step toward desired, not a script to the end\n    write status back\n```\n\nThe loop is *level-triggered*, acting on the current difference rather than on the event that caused\nit, *because* events are lost when components restart; a controller that wakes after a crash simply\nrecomputes the diff. It also means you never get a \"done\" signal from `apply`; you watch status\n(`kubectl rollout status`) to learn when reality has caught up.\n\n### The objects you actually use\n- **Pod**: one or more containers sharing a network namespace and volumes; the unit of scheduling;\n  ephemeral, and almost never created directly in production (a controller owns it).\n- **Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods**: a replica count, a Pod template and a rollout strategy. Changing\n  the image creates a new ReplicaSet and scales it up while the old one scales down, bounded by\n  `maxSurge` and `maxUnavailable`. Old ReplicaSets are kept at zero replicas, which is what makes\n  `kubectl rollout undo` fast. Kubernetes does not roll back on its own: a rollout whose new Pods never\n  become ready simply stalls (with `maxUnavailable: 0`, harmlessly) and is marked failed after\n  `progressDeadlineSeconds`; reverting is a human, pipeline or progressive-delivery tool (Argo Rollouts,\n  Flagger) decision.\n- **Service**: a stable virtual IP and DNS name that load-balances to Pods matching a label selector,\n  with membership gated by readiness. An Ingress or Gateway brings HTTP in from outside.\n- **ConfigMap** and **Secret**: configuration injected as environment variables or mounted files.\n- **Job** and **CronJob** for run-to-completion work (evals, batch embedding, fine-tuning);\n  **StatefulSet** for stable identity and storage (vector databases); **DaemonSet** for one Pod per\n  node (the GPU device plugin, a log shipper).\n- **Namespace**, RBAC and **ResourceQuota** to share a cluster across teams.\n\n### Scheduling: requests, limits and GPUs\nRequests are what the scheduler reserves on a node; limits are what the cgroup enforces at run time.\nExceeding a CPU limit throttles the container; exceeding a memory limit gets it OOM-killed (exit code\n137). GPUs are exposed by the NVIDIA device plugin as the extended resource `nvidia.com/gpu`: whole\nintegers, request equal to limit, no overcommit, because a GPU cannot be time-shared transparently the\nway CPU can. GPU node pools are usually tainted so that only Pods with a matching toleration land on\nthem, keeping CPU-only work off expensive hardware. Fractional sharing needs MIG partitions or\ntime-slicing configured on the node.\n\nRequests and limits together also set a Pod's quality-of-service class, which decides who dies first\nwhen a node runs short of memory. **Guaranteed**: every container has CPU and memory requests equal to\nlimits. **BestEffort**: no requests or limits at all. **Burstable**: anything in between. Under memory\npressure the kubelet evicts BestEffort Pods first, then Burstable Pods using more than they requested,\nand Guaranteed Pods last, *because* a Pod that reserved exactly what it uses has made no claim on\nanyone else's memory. The trade is that Guaranteed requires a CPU limit, which reintroduces\nthrottling; the example below is Burstable on purpose.\n\n### Probes: the contract that makes rollouts safe\n- **Startup probe**: for slow starts such as loading model weights; while it has not passed, the other\n  probes are paused, *because* otherwise the liveness probe would kill the Pod mid-load and produce a\n  CrashLoopBackOff.\n- **Readiness probe**: whether to send traffic; failing removes the Pod from Service endpoints without\n  restarting it, because overload or a dependency outage should shed traffic, not restart the process.\n- **Liveness probe**: whether to restart; it should be cheap and must not depend on downstream systems,\n  or an incident in the database becomes a restart storm in the API.\n\n### Termination\nWhen a Pod is deleted it is marked Terminating, removed from Service endpoints (asynchronously, so\ntraffic can still arrive for a moment), its `preStop` hook runs, PID 1 receives SIGTERM, and after\n`terminationGracePeriodSeconds` (30 by default, and the clock starts before `preStop`, so the hook's\ntime counts against it) it receives SIGKILL. The server must stop accepting,\ndrain in-flight requests, including streams, and exit. For LLM responses that can run for minutes,\nraise the grace period and add a short `preStop` sleep so endpoint removal propagates first.\n\n### Scaling\nThe Horizontal Pod Autoscaler changes replica counts on CPU, memory or custom metrics. For GPU-bound\nservices CPU is a poor signal; scale on queue depth, in-flight requests or tokens waiting, through KEDA\nor custom metrics. The Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter adds nodes, but a GPU node takes minutes to boot\nand pull a multi-gigabyte image, so reactive scaling needs headroom or a warm pool.\n\n### AI workloads specifically\nModel weights can be baked into the image (simple, slow to pull), on a shared read-only volume, or\nfetched by an init container from object storage into an `emptyDir` with a node-local cache. Serving\nstacks such as vLLM or KServe run as Deployments with the pattern above; distributed training or\ninference runs on Ray via KubeRay; a PodDisruptionBudget stops node maintenance from evicting every\nreplica at once.","example":"A Deployment and Service for a GPU-backed inference API. The rollout never drops below three ready\nreplicas, the startup probe allows up to ten minutes of model loading before liveness checks begin,\nthe readiness endpoint gates traffic, and the grace period plus `preStop` sleep let long streaming\nresponses finish before SIGKILL. The image is referenced by digest so every node runs the same bytes.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata: {name: llm-api}\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  strategy: {rollingUpdate: {maxSurge: 1, maxUnavailable: 0}}   # never below 3 ready\n  selector: {matchLabels: {app: llm-api}}\n  template:\n    metadata: {labels: {app: llm-api}}\n    spec:\n      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 120       # let long streaming responses finish\n      nodeSelector: {accelerator: nvidia-l4}\n      tolerations: [{key: nvidia.com/gpu, operator: Exists, effect: NoSchedule}]\n      containers:\n      - name: api\n        image: registry.example.com/llm-api@sha256:3f9c...   # immutable digest, not :latest\n        ports: [{containerPort: 8000}]\n        envFrom: [{secretRef: {name: llm-api-secrets}}]   # frozen at start; mount as files for hot rotation\n        resources:\n          requests: {cpu: \"2\", memory: 8Gi, nvidia.com/gpu: 1}\n          limits:   {memory: 8Gi, nvidia.com/gpu: 1}          # no CPU limit: avoids throttling\n        startupProbe:                            # model load may take minutes\n          httpGet: {path: /health, port: 8000}\n          periodSeconds: 10\n          failureThreshold: 60                   # up to 10 min before giving up\n        readinessProbe:\n          httpGet: {path: /ready, port: 8000}    # 200 only after the model is loaded and warm\n          periodSeconds: 5\n        livenessProbe:\n          httpGet: {path: /health, port: 8000}   # cheap, no downstream calls\n          periodSeconds: 30\n        lifecycle:\n          preStop: {exec: {command: [\"sleep\", \"5\"]}}   # let endpoint removal propagate\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata: {name: llm-api}\nspec:\n  selector: {app: llm-api}\n  ports: [{port: 80, targetPort: 8000}]\n```","tradeoffs":["Declarative reconciliation gives self-healing and repeatable deploys, but you pay in a large object model and a control plane to operate; for one service on one box a managed container service is less to learn, and the balance flips once you have several services, GPUs, batch jobs and more than one team.","`maxUnavailable: 0` keeps capacity during rollouts but requires a spare GPU for the surge Pod; when GPUs are scarce you choose between a brief capacity dip and permanently reserved headroom.","CPU limits protect neighbours from a runaway process but throttle latency-sensitive Pods through the CFS quota; the common compromise is to always set requests and memory limits and rarely set CPU limits.","Autoscaling on queue depth tracks load correctly, but GPU node provisioning takes minutes, so reactive scaling needs idle headroom that costs money every hour it is not used."],"pitfalls":["No resource requests; the symptom is nodes overloaded and Pods evicted, or, with requests set too high, Pods stuck Pending with `Insufficient nvidia.com/gpu` in the events.","A liveness probe that calls the model or the database; the symptom is a restart storm during an incident that turns a slow dependency into a full outage.","No startup probe on a service that loads weights for minutes; the symptom is CrashLoopBackOff: the liveness probe kills the container mid-load (with default settings after about 30 seconds of failed checks), and the restart backoff grows toward its five-minute cap while the model never finishes loading.","Readiness returning 200 before the model is loaded; the symptom is a burst of 5xx responses right after every deploy.","The default 30-second grace period with no drain logic; the symptom is streaming responses cut mid-token on every rollout and node drain.","Deploying a `:latest` tag; the symptom is Pods on different nodes running different code depending on when each node last pulled.","OOMKilled with no application log; the symptom is restarts under load because the memory limit was set below the real peak of tokenizer plus model plus batch.","Treating `emptyDir` or local disk as durable for an index; the symptom is data gone after the Pod is rescheduled.","HPA on CPU for a GPU-bound service; the symptom is a growing queue while the autoscaler sees low CPU and never adds replicas."],"when":"Reach for Kubernetes when you run several services, need GPU scheduling, run batch jobs beside serving,\nshare infrastructure across teams, or deploy into enterprises that already run it, which is most of\nthem. Avoid it for a single container with modest load, where a managed serverless container platform\n(Cloud Run, AWS App Runner) or a simpler orchestrator (ECS, Nomad) costs less to operate; those\ngive you scheduling and restarts but a smaller object model, less portability and, for serverless,\nlimited GPU and long-request support. Even on a managed control plane (EKS, GKE, AKS) you still own\nnode pools, upgrades, add-ons and every manifest, which is where most of the operating cost lives. Measure time-to-ready for a new Pod, rollout duration, restart counts, Pending\nPods, and GPU allocation versus actual utilization. Day to day you live in `kubectl get`, `describe`,\n`logs` and `exec`, read events, write Deployments, Jobs and autoscalers, and package them with Helm or\nKustomize. In interviews, explain reconciliation, how a Deployment rolls out, the three probes,\nrequests versus limits, GPU scheduling and graceful termination.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes manifests or Helm charts, sets probes, requests and grace periods correctly, and debugs CrashLoopBackOff, OOMKilled and Pending Pods from `describe` output and events.","architect":"Designs the cluster topology (GPU node pools, taints, namespaces, quotas), chooses autoscaling signals, decides where weights live, and picks managed versus self-run control planes.","fde":"Deploys into the customer's cluster with their CNI, ingress, Pod Security Standards, registry mirrors, GPU operator version and restricted RBAC, and sizes node pools with their platform team."},"check":{"q":"After each deploy of your vLLM-based service, users see a burst of errors and some streaming\nresponses are cut off, even though the Deployment uses a rolling update. What is happening and how do\nyou fix it?","a":"Two separate failures. The errors at the start come from new Pods reporting ready before the\nengine has loaded, so the Service routes to them; fix it by making `/ready` return 200 only after the\nmodel is loaded and a warm-up request succeeded, and add a startup probe with a generous\n`failureThreshold` so liveness does not kill Pods during load. The cut-off streams come from old Pods\nreceiving SIGTERM and either exiting immediately or hitting the 30-second default grace period\nmid-stream; fix it by handling SIGTERM to stop accepting and drain in-flight requests, raising\n`terminationGracePeriodSeconds` to cover the longest stream, and adding a `preStop` sleep so endpoint\nremoval propagates before shutdown begins. Set `maxUnavailable: 0` with `maxSurge: 1` so capacity never\ndips, accepting one spare GPU during rollout, and verify by watching 5xx and stream-abort metrics\nacross a rollout."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `kubectl apply` of a Deployment manifest actually do?","options":["Starts the containers on the node your kubeconfig points at","Records desired state in the API server; controllers then create ReplicaSets and Pods and keep converging reality toward that spec","Builds the image and pushes it to the cluster's registry","Sends the manifest to every kubelet, which executes it once"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kubernetes is declarative: `apply` changes the stored spec and the Deployment and ReplicaSet controllers do the work, repeatedly, which is why Pods are recreated after failures. Nothing builds images, and kubelets only run Pods the scheduler has bound to their node."},{"q":"Which probe should decide whether a Pod receives traffic, without restarting it?","options":["livenessProbe","readinessProbe","startupProbe","the preStop hook"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A failing readiness probe removes the Pod from Service endpoints and nothing else, which is the right response to overload or a dependency outage. Liveness failure restarts the container, the startup probe only gates the other probes during initialisation, and preStop runs at termination."},{"q":"Why do GPU Pods often sit Pending while CPU Pods on the same cluster schedule immediately?","options":["GPU Pods must be created with `kubectl run` rather than a Deployment","The scheduler only honours GPU requests in the kube-system namespace","`nvidia.com/gpu` is an integer, non-overcommittable resource exposed by the device plugin, and GPU nodes are usually tainted, so the Pod needs a free whole GPU plus a matching toleration","GPU nodes do not run the kubelet"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CPU is requested in millicores on plentiful, cheap nodes, so a CPU Pod usually fits somewhere. A GPU request needs an entire unallocated device on a node whose taint the Pod tolerates; if none is free the Pod waits until one is released or a new node is provisioned."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","sources":["Humble, Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery","Forsgren, Humble, Kim, 2018 — Accelerate","Official docs: GitHub Actions"],"why":"Without a pipeline, integration happens late: weeks of divergent branches meet at release time and the\nmerge is where the bugs live. Tests run on developer machines with whatever happens to be installed,\nso \"passes locally\" carries no information about production. Deployment is a runbook followed by a\nperson, usually under time pressure, and the second execution differs from the first in some detail\nnobody wrote down. Nobody can say which commit is in production, and rollback means \"redeploy the old\none\", which cannot be rebuilt because the dependency resolution has moved on.\n\nEach of these is a feedback-delay problem. The cost of a defect grows with the distance between the\nchange that introduced it and the moment it is detected, because context is lost and other changes\npile on top. Continuous integration shrinks that distance by integrating at least daily against a\nbuild that must stay green. Continuous delivery extends the idea through to production with a\ndeployment pipeline whose stages give fast, cheap feedback first and slow, expensive feedback later.\nThe DORA research reported in *Accelerate* found that teams deploying more often with shorter lead\ntimes also had *lower* change failure rates: small, frequent changes are safer than large, rare ones,\nwhich is the opposite of the intuition behind \"test everything manually before the big release\".\n\nAI systems raise the stakes. A prompt edit is a behaviour change with no type checker; a model version\nbump can silently regress a task the team never wrote a test for; a retrieval index rebuild changes\nanswers without touching code. Without a pipeline that treats these as changes to be verified,\nquality drift ships unnoticed and is discovered by users.","how":"### The stages of a deployment pipeline\n1. **Commit stage, minutes.** Check out on a clean runner, install from the lockfile, lint, type-check,\n   run unit tests with the model client mocked or recorded, scan for secrets. It runs on every push,\n   so it must be cheap and deterministic; it exists to fail fast on the majority of mistakes.\n2. **Build.** Build the container image once, tag it with the git SHA, push it to a registry. The image\n   digest is now the artifact. \"Build once, promote many\" is a rule *because* rebuilding per\n   environment can resolve different dependencies or base layers, so production would run something\n   that was never tested.\n3. **Acceptance.** Start the real dependencies (Postgres, Redis) as service containers, run API tests\n   against the built image, run contract tests against provider APIs, and run a small, fixed\n   evaluation set against the prompts and model with pass thresholds.\n4. **Supply chain.** Audit dependencies, scan the image, produce a software bill of materials, sign the\n   image so later stages can verify they are deploying what this stage produced.\n5. **Staging.** Deploy automatically, run smoke tests against a production-shaped environment.\n6. **Production.** Promote the same digest, gated by an approval (delivery) or automatically\n   (deployment), progressively: a canary takes a slice of traffic while error rate, latency and online\n   quality metrics are compared against the baseline, then full rollout or automatic rollback.\n\nThe three common production strategies differ in what they cost and what they can detect. A **rolling**\nupdate replaces replicas a few at a time: no extra capacity beyond the surge, but old and new versions\nserve side by side and a bad version reaches every user before anyone compares metrics. **Blue-green**\nstands up a full second environment and switches traffic at once: rollback is an instant switch back,\npaid for with double capacity during the release, and it detects nothing that only shows under real\nload until all users are on it. A **canary** sends a small share of traffic to the new version and\npromotes only if its metrics hold: the only strategy that limits the blast radius of a bad release, but\nit needs enough traffic for the comparison to be statistically meaningful and metrics that actually\nreflect quality, which for LLM features means online evaluation signals, not just HTTP 5xx.\n\nWith GitOps, the pipeline's last step is a commit of the new digest to a configuration repository, and\na controller (Argo CD, Flux) reconciles the cluster to it. The config repo becomes the audit log of\nwhat ran when, and rollback is `git revert`.\n\n### Principles that make the stages work\n- **Trunk-based development with feature flags.** Small pull requests into a main branch that is\n  always deployable; flags separate *deploying* code from *releasing* behaviour.\n- **Pipeline as code**, versioned next to the application, so a pipeline change is reviewed like any\n  other change.\n- **Reproducibility.** Lockfiles for dependencies, base images pinned by digest, third-party pipeline\n  actions pinned to a commit SHA rather than a mutable tag.\n- **Fast feedback.** Cache dependencies keyed on the lockfile hash, parallelise test shards, and move\n  slow suites to a nightly or on-demand lane rather than letting the commit stage grow to thirty\n  minutes, because a slow pipeline makes developers batch changes, which is exactly the large, risky\n  merge the pipeline exists to prevent.\n- **Secrets through identity.** The CI job presents an OIDC token the cloud exchanges for a short-lived\n  role credential, instead of a stored long-lived key; secrets are masked in logs and never exposed to\n  jobs triggered by untrusted pull requests.\n- **Environment protection.** Production deploys require a reviewer, a branch rule, or both.\n- **Backward-compatible changes across a deploy.** During any rolling or canary release, old and new\n  versions run at once against the same database, queue and caches, and a rollback puts the old version\n  back on data the new one wrote. Schema changes therefore follow expand-contract: add the new column or\n  table first (old code ignores it), deploy code that writes both and reads the new one, backfill, and\n  only in a later release drop the old shape. Message and API formats follow the same rule.\n\n### What changes for AI systems\n- **Non-determinism.** Model outputs vary between runs, so unit tests mock or record the client and\n  assert on structure and control flow, while quality is measured in an evaluation stage with\n  thresholds over many cases, never with exact-match asserts on generated text.\n- **Cost and quota.** Real-model tests spend money and rate limit. Run a small smoke evaluation on\n  every pull request, the full set on main or nightly, under a CI-only key with a spend limit.\n- **Artifacts beyond code.** Model weights and indexes live in a registry or object store, referenced\n  by version; a change to a prompt template, a model version or a retrieval index must trigger the\n  evaluation gate exactly as a code change triggers tests.\n- **GPU work.** Tests that need a GPU run on self-hosted runners or as cluster Jobs, never on the\n  default runner.\n- **Rollback scope.** The deployable unit pins code, prompt and model version together, so a rollback\n  reverts all three.\n\n### How you know it is working\nThe four DORA metrics: deployment frequency, lead time for changes, change failure rate, and time to\nrestore service. Add pipeline duration and flake rate; a pipeline nobody trusts is one nobody waits for.","example":"A GitHub Actions workflow with two jobs. Every pull request runs lint, types and mocked unit tests,\nplus a small smoke evaluation that only runs when the secret is available (pushes and same-repository\npull requests). On `main`, the image is built once, pushed with the commit SHA as its tag, and the\nexact digest is rolled out to the cluster using short-lived credentials obtained through OIDC rather\nthan a stored cloud key. The `production` environment carries required-reviewer protection.\n\n```yaml\nname: ci\non:\n  pull_request:\n  push: {branches: [main]}\npermissions: {contents: read}                # least privilege by default; jobs widen it\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4             # pin to a commit SHA in a real repository\n      - uses: astral-sh/setup-uv@v5\n      - run: uv sync --frozen\n      - run: uv run ruff check . && uv run mypy src\n      - run: uv run pytest -q tests/unit      # model client mocked: deterministic, free, fast\n      - run: uv run pytest -q -m smoke tests/eval   # ~20 golden cases against a cheap model\n        if: github.event_name == 'push' || github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository\n        env: {LLM_API_KEY: \"${{ secrets.CI_LLM_KEY }}\"}\n  build-and-deploy:\n    needs: test\n    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    environment: production                  # protection rule: required reviewers\n    permissions: {contents: read, packages: write, id-token: write}   # id-token: OIDC to the cloud\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: docker/login-action@v3\n        with: {registry: ghcr.io, username: \"${{ github.actor }}\", password: \"${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\"}\n      - id: build\n        uses: docker/build-push-action@v6\n        with: {push: true, tags: \"ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}:${{ github.sha }}\"}   # repo name must be lowercase\n      - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4\n        with: {role-to-assume: \"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/ci-deploy\", aws-region: us-east-1}\n      - run: |                                 # promote the exact digest that was built and tested\n          aws eks update-kubeconfig --name prod\n          kubectl set image deployment/llm-api api=ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}@${{ steps.build.outputs.digest }}\n          kubectl rollout status deployment/llm-api --timeout=10m\n```","tradeoffs":["Running evaluations on every pull request catches prompt regressions early, but costs money, time and attention when the eval is noisy; the balance flips to a small smoke set per pull request plus a full nightly run once the per-pull-request eval bill or wait time starts pushing people to batch prompt changes or skip the gate.","Continuous deployment maximises flow by removing the human gate, but for regulated customers or high-blast-radius changes continuous delivery with an approval and a canary is the right stopping point.","Build-once-promote-many guarantees production runs what was tested, but forces every environment difference into runtime configuration rather than a rebuild, which demands discipline about config injection.","GitOps gives an auditable, declarative rollback path, but adds a reconciliation delay and another controller to operate; direct `kubectl` from the pipeline is simpler for a single team and few services.","Aggressive caching keeps pipelines fast, but a stale cache produces \"green in CI, broken in prod\"; key caches on lockfile hashes and expire them on base-image changes."],"pitfalls":["Tolerating flaky tests; the symptom is a \"just re-run it\" culture in which a real failure is merged because red no longer means anything. Quarantine flaky tests immediately and fix or delete them.","Unit tests that call the real model and assert exact strings; the symptom is intermittent failures that start the day the provider updates the model.","Rebuilding the image at deploy time; the symptom is production behaving differently from staging with the \"same\" commit because dependency resolution drifted between builds.","Long-lived cloud keys stored as CI secrets; the symptom is a leaked runner log or compromised action yielding permanent credentials. Use OIDC federation.","Third-party actions referenced by a mutable tag or branch; the symptom is a supply-chain compromise of that action silently running in your pipeline. Pin to a commit SHA.","Only code is versioned and rolled back; the symptom is a bad prompt requiring a hotfix pull request under pressure instead of a one-click revert.","`pull_request_target` workflows, which run with secrets in the base repository's context, that check out and execute the fork's code; the symptom is a malicious pull request exfiltrating secrets through a build script or log line.","A commit stage that grows past half an hour; the symptom is rising pull-request size and lead time, because developers batch changes to avoid waiting.","Shipping a destructive migration (rename or drop a column) in the same release as the code that needs it; the symptom is errors from the still-running old replicas during rollout, and a rollback that fails because the old code cannot read the new schema."],"when":"Set up a pipeline on day one for any codebase with more than one contributor or more than one deploy;\na five-minute lint-and-test workflow is enough to start. Add stages as risk grows: image build and scan\nonce you deploy containers, an evaluation gate once prompt or model changes affect users, canaries once\ntraffic is large enough for a comparison to mean something. Use path filters so documentation changes\nskip the heavy stages. Track the four DORA metrics plus pipeline duration, flake rate and eval-gate pass\nrate. In interviews, explain build-once-promote-many, why small frequent deploys are safer, how you test\nnon-deterministic model code, and how a CI job authenticates to the cloud without a stored key.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the workflow, mocks the model client in unit tests, maintains the smoke evaluation set, pins actions and base images, and reads failed job logs before anyone else does.","architect":"Defines the promotion path, environments and gates (eval thresholds, approvals, canary criteria), the artifact and config repositories, and the rollback policy that covers prompt and model versions.","fde":"Fits delivery into the customer's tooling (GitLab, Jenkins, Azure DevOps), their change windows and approval requirements, and builds the evaluation gate on their data."},"check":{"q":"A pull request changes the system prompt of your RAG assistant and bumps the model version.\nDescribe what the pipeline should do before this reaches users, and how you keep it affordable.","a":"Treat it as code first: the commit stage runs lint, types and unit tests with the model mocked,\nwhich costs nothing. Because files under the prompt and model paths changed, the evaluation gate\ntriggers: a fixed golden set of a few hundred questions with graded references runs against the new\nconfiguration, scored by deterministic checks and an LLM judge, and compared with the baseline from\nmain using thresholds on groundedness, refusal rate and output format; the pull request fails if it\nregresses beyond tolerance. Keep cost down by running a twenty-case smoke subset on every push and the\nfull set only when those paths change or a label is applied, under a CI-only key with a spend limit.\nThen build the image once, deploy to staging, smoke-test, and canary a small share of production\ntraffic while watching the same metrics online, with the prompt and model version pinned in the same\nartifact so a rollback reverts both."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why build the image once and promote the same digest through environments rather than rebuilding per environment?","options":["Because container registries charge per build","Because a rebuild can resolve different dependencies or base layers, so production would run an artifact that was never tested; promoting one digest guarantees identity","Because Kubernetes can only pull a given image once per cluster","Because tests only execute on the first build of a commit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The artifact that passed acceptance tests is the only thing known to work; any rebuild is a new, untested artifact. Registries do not limit pulls this way and test execution is a pipeline choice, not a constraint."},{"q":"Unit tests for code that calls an LLM fail intermittently with slightly different wording in the output. What is the best fix?","options":["Raise the temperature so outputs become more consistent","Automatically retry each failing test three times","Mock or record the model client in unit tests, and move quality checks to an evaluation stage with thresholds rather than exact-match asserts","Pin the tests to one model version and assert the exact string"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Generated text is non-deterministic by nature, so exact matches will keep breaking. Unit tests should verify control flow against a stubbed client, while quality is measured statistically over many cases. Retries hide real failures and higher temperature increases variance."},{"q":"Why is OIDC federation preferred over a stored cloud access key in a CI pipeline?","options":["OIDC tokens never expire, so the pipeline never needs to re-authenticate","The job presents a short-lived signed identity token that the cloud exchanges for temporary credentials, so there is no long-lived secret to leak or rotate","Kubernetes requires OIDC for any `kubectl` access from a pipeline","Stored keys cannot be restricted to a single repository"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The credential is short-lived, and the cloud's trust policy accepts the token only when its claims (repository, branch or environment) match, which removes the single most common CI leak. OIDC tokens are deliberately short-lived, and `kubectl` access works with many credential types."}]}]});
